(window.COLLECTIONS = window.COLLECTIONS || []).push(
{
  id: "battles", route: "battle", label: "Battles", glyph: "&#9876;",
  sub: (n) => n + " fields that decided the realm",
  intro: "Westeros is decided in a surprisingly small number of afternoons. What follows are the fights that actually moved the map — from the day three dragons ended the Reach's independence to the night the dead came over a wall in the North. Where the books and the show tell a battle differently, both tellings are noted.",
  items: [
  { id: "last-storm", name: "The Last Storm", sub: "1 BC — the Conquest",
    meta: [["Who", "Orys Baratheon and Rhaenys Targaryen vs. the Storm King"], ["Result", "House Durrandon ended in the male line"],
           ["Commanders", "Orys Baratheon, with Rhaenys on Meraxes · King Argilac Durrandon, called the Arrogant"],
           ["Strength", "A Targaryen host of moderate size against the fuller levy of the stormlands"],
           ["Ground", "A ridge on the road north of Storm’s End, in the worst weather anyone present had seen"],
           ["Dragons", "Meraxes — present, and for most of the day very nearly useless"]],
    blurb: "The one battle of the Conquest that a dragon almost failed to decide, because it rained.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The quarrel predated the Conquest by a couple of years and was, in its origins, a marriage negotiation that went wrong. Argilac Durrandon was the last of a line that claimed descent from Durran Godsgrief himself, an old warrior with a great deal of reputation, no son, and one daughter. He was hemmed in by the Reach on one side and Harren the Black on the other, and he cast about for an ally the way any cornered king does. What he offered the lord of Dragonstone was his daughter Argella’s hand and a stretch of disputed land along the Blackwater — a serious offer, and a compliment.",
      "Aegon Targaryen’s reply was the sort of thing that starts wars without meaning to and possibly while meaning to. He would take the land, he said, and he would gladly see Argella married — to Orys Baratheon, his oldest friend, his best commander, and a man widely believed in Westeros and never once confirmed by anyone in a position to know to be Aegon’s half-brother on the wrong side of the sheets. To Argilac this was not a counter-offer. It was an assessment, and the assessment was that a Durrandon princess was worth a baseborn soldier. He had the envoy’s hands cut off and sent back in a box with a remark about the only hands the bastard would ever have of him.",
      "So when Aegon landed and the war began in earnest, the stormlands were not a kingdom to be negotiated with. After Harrenhal burned and the riverlords changed their allegiance overnight, Aegon divided his strength three ways: north to the Vale under Visenya, west and south into the Reach under himself, and down the coast into the stormlands under Orys, with Rhaenys and Meraxes above him. Argilac did not wait to be besieged. Storm’s End is arguably the most difficult castle in Westeros to take and he had every reason to sit inside it, but he had spent his entire life not sitting, he had the larger army, and he had noticed something about dragons that nobody else had thought worth noticing: they are animals, and animals dislike weather.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The stormlands are named for what happens to them. Weather comes off the narrow sea and piles up against the coast, and the country between King’s Landing and Shipbreaker Bay is where it lands. The storm that broke over the two armies as they closed was severe even by local standards — rain coming sideways in sheets, wind strong enough to take pavilions off their pegs, and within an hour a battlefield of running mud with visibility of a few dozen yards. Every account of the day, from either side, begins with the weather, because everything else on the field was downstream of it.",
          "Argilac had the fuller strength of the stormlands: his own household, the marcher lords, the coast houses, and a heavy weight of horse. The numbers that come down are not worth quoting and were probably not counted at the time, but the disparity ran his way and he knew it. Orys had a mixed host — Aegon’s household men, the crownlands lords who had knelt in the first year, and riverlanders whose enthusiasm was recent and untested.",
          "And Orys had a dragon overhead who could not do very much. This is the part of the battle worth dwelling on. Dragonfire in rain does not behave the way dragonfire in dry grass behaves, and a dragon trying to hold a low pass in a crosswind is a large animal being pushed sideways over ground it cannot see. Rhaenys and Meraxes were in the sky, and their contribution to the day was real but nothing like decisive — the more useful thing they did was make Argilac hurry, because a commander who believes the weather is his ally will fight before it clears.",
          "The ground Orys took was a ridge across the line of march. It is unremarkable country and it was chosen for one reason: mud runs downhill, and a horse coming up a slope in mud arrives slowly.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The battle was conventional, long, and fought almost entirely with steel — which makes it the only major engagement of the Conquest that a Westerosi commander of the previous generation would have recognised. Argilac attacked, because there was never any prospect of Argilac doing anything else. The stormlander horse came up the ridge in the rain and were held; they pulled back, re-formed, and came again.",
          "That cycle is what killed his army. A cavalry charge is a single expenditure of momentum, and momentum is exactly what mud takes away. Each successive attack arrived at the top of the slope with less weight behind it than the last, into men who had spent the interval standing still. Argilac’s advantage in numbers was real but it was being fed into the hill a few hundred at a time, and the ridge was doing to his charges what water does to anything.",
          "Meraxes worked the flanks and the rear where she could, which in that weather meant intermittently. The effect was less burning than herding: a stormlander wing that felt a dragon somewhere behind it in the murk did not always break, but it did not manoeuvre freely either, and it could not be got round the ridge. That, rather than fire, is the dragon’s actual contribution to the Last Storm, and it is a useful corrective to the impression the Conquest usually leaves.",
          "The day was decided in the dark and in the rain, when Argilac’s wings finally went. His right gave way first, by most tellings, and once one flank folds on ground like that the rest is a matter of time and drowning. What the old king did then is entirely in character and was probably the only thing left that could have worked: he gathered what he had around him and drove straight at the enemy’s standard, on the theory that a host with a dead commander goes home.",
          "The accounts agree that he got there. They agree that the two men met, that it was the last fighting of the day, and that the Storm King went down in the mud in front of his own banner with a dozen men of consequence around him. They were also written afterwards by people with a professional interest in that particular version, and single combat between the two commanders at the climax of a battle is exactly the shape a story takes when it is retold often enough. What is beyond argument is that Argilac did not leave the field and that Orys did.",
          "There was no serious pursuit. The stormlanders who could still walk went south toward the castle or scattered inland, and Orys had no cavalry left in condition to chase anybody through that country in that weather.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The stormlands lost a very large part of their nobility in a single afternoon — not on the scale of the Field of Fire a short while later, but heavily enough that the region spent the first decades of Targaryen rule with an unusual number of young lords and dowager mothers. The Targaryen host took real losses too, which is worth noting because almost nowhere else in the Conquest did they.",
          "House Durrandon ended in the male line on that ridge, after a span of rule that the stormlanders themselves measured in thousands of years and the maesters measure more cautiously. Unlike the Gardeners, the Durrandons were not burned out of existence in one place; they were simply reduced to one unmarried girl in a castle.",
          "The castle itself never fell, because it was never assaulted. Orys marched on Storm’s End and found the gates shut and Argella Durrandon inside them, declaring herself Storm Queen and promising to burn the place around her rather than yield it. It is one of the better speeches of the period and it was answered by her own garrison, who had done the arithmetic of a siege against a dragon and reached a different conclusion. What happened at Storm’s End belongs in the fold below, but the outcome is that the strongest castle in the south changed hands without a stone being thrown at it.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It created House Baratheon, and it created it in a very particular way. Orys took Storm’s End, took Argella to wife, and then took the crowned stag, the black-and-gold, and the Durrandon words as his own. A conqueror adopting the arms and motto of the house he destroyed is not standard practice; it is closer to an admission that the stormlands were being handed a change of surname rather than a change of identity. Three hundred years later, when a Baratheon takes the Iron Throne, a good deal of his support in that region rests on a family that the region never entirely stopped regarding as its own.",
          "It also settled the southern half of the war before the Field of Fire was fought. With the stormlands under a man Aegon trusted absolutely, the Targaryens had a secure flank and a second army, and the Reach and the Rock were left facing the dragons alone.",
          "And it left one lesson lying in plain sight that almost nobody picked up. Dragons are weather. They are enormously less useful in rain, wind and cloud than in a dry summer, and the Last Storm is the demonstration. The number of Westerosi commanders over the following three centuries who successfully arranged to fight the Targaryens in bad weather is, on the record, zero.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "King Argilac Durrandon, the Arrogant, died on the ridge in the rain, the last Storm King of a line that had held the stormlands since the Age of Heroes. He was old, out of temper with the world, and correct about almost everything except the size of the problem; had the storm lasted one more day he might well have won, and the histories would read very differently.",
      "Argella Durrandon was bound, gagged and carried out through her own gates by the captains of her garrison, who delivered her naked to Orys Baratheon’s tent as a peace offering. By every account Orys covered her with his own cloak, had her fed and clothed, apologised for the manner of it, and treated her from that moment as what she was. He married her. The stormlands’ subsequent loyalty to the Baratheons is not entirely a matter of policy; a good part of it descends from that girl.",
      "Orys Baratheon became the first Lord of Storm’s End and Aegon’s first Hand of the King. He would go on to a much worse day in Dorne, where he lost his sword hand to the Wyl of Wyl, and to a long, patient, unattractive settling of that account years afterward. He never once claimed in public to be a Targaryen and never once denied it either.",
      "The names of the garrison captains who tied up their own queen and handed her to the enemy are not recorded, which is either an oversight or the most eloquent thing the chroniclers of the Conquest ever did.",
    ] },

  { id: "field-of-fire", name: "The Field of Fire", sub: "1 BC — the Conquest",
    meta: [["Who", "Aegon, Visenya & Rhaenys vs. the Reach and the Rock"], ["Result", "House Gardener destroyed"],
           ["Commanders", "Aegon Targaryen and his sisters · King Mern IX Gardener and King Loren I Lannister"],
           ["Strength", "Roughly ten thousand against something near fifty-five thousand"],
           ["Ground", "Open grassland in the Reach, dry at the end of a long summer"],
           ["Dragons", "Balerion, Vhagar and Meraxes — the only time all three flew to a battle together"]],
    blurb: "Fifty-five thousand men against ten thousand and three dragons. The dragons won in an afternoon.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Aegon Targaryen had landed with a small household, three dragons and a claim nobody in Westeros had asked for. His first year went better than it had any right to: the Crownlands lords came over one by one, Harrenhal was burned out of the war in a night, and the riverlords changed sides so gratefully that the whole middle of the continent fell into his hands without a proper campaign. What he had won, though, was a strip. The two richest and most populous kingdoms of the south were still intact, still independent, and had begun to notice that the problem was not going to solve itself.",
      "Mern IX Gardener, King of the Reach, and Loren I Lannister, King of the Rock, had spent generations glaring at one another across the marches. The dragons made them allies inside a single summer. Their calculation was not stupid. Dragons were terrifying and few; men were countable. Between them they could put more armed men in one place than had ever been assembled in Westeros, and the accepted wisdom of the age was that a beast, however large, is still one target and can be shot. Nobody living had seen dragons used properly in a field battle. Neither had Aegon.",
      "So the two kings joined their hosts and marched north to find him, and Aegon rode south to be found. He had perhaps a fifth of their numbers, most of it borrowed from lords who had knelt within the past year and could unkneel just as quickly if the day went badly. If he lost, the Conquest ended there and the story of the Targaryens in Westeros became a footnote about an ambitious exile from a dead empire. If he won, there was nothing left in the south to stop him.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The field was open country in the northern Reach, at the end of a long dry summer, with the grass high and yellow and the wind moving through it. The exact spot is argued over by maesters who were not there and has since been ploughed and grazed for three centuries. What every account agrees on is the state of the ground: it was tinder, and nobody thought to consider that a factor when choosing where to stand.",
          "The allied host was the largest ever mustered on the continent to that date — the figure that has come down is around fifty-five thousand, and while such numbers always flatter, the disparity was real and enormous. Mern brought the whole strength of the Reach, his sons and grandsons and brothers and cousins riding with him, a house so thick with heirs that its extinction seemed a mathematical impossibility. Loren brought the west. The two kings were not fools and their army was not a rabble: it was a conventional Westerosi host of the best quality, with a very heavy armoured centre and a great deal of horse.",
          "Aegon had something near ten thousand and no illusions about them. His line was there to hold ground and to keep the enemy from simply walking around the problem. His actual army was three animals. Balerion he rode himself; Visenya had Vhagar and Rhaenys had Meraxes. They had been used before, singly, mostly for burning gates and making a point. They had never all three been committed to a battle at once, and after this day they never would be again.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was ordinary and went badly for Aegon. The allied host came on in strength and the Targaryen line, outnumbered five to one, did what outnumbered lines do — it bent, it gave ground, and it began to look like a formality. This mattered more than it appears. The whole point of the allied plan was mass, and mass takes time to bring to bear; the deeper the enemy committed, the tighter they packed themselves into the space in front of them.",
          "Then the three dragons came in together, low, from behind their own line. There is no earlier engagement anywhere in the histories to compare it with, and the men on the ground had no drill for it. Dragonfire in a field battle is not a weapon that kills the men it touches so much as one that destroys the shape of an army. Formations exist because men stand where they are told; they stop existing the moment the men will not.",
          "The particular thing that decided the day was not the fire on the soldiers. It was the fire in the grass. The dry summer growth caught, the wind took it, and within a short time the allied host was inside a burning field rather than beneath a burning sky. Fire on open dry ground moves faster than armoured men on foot and considerably faster than horses that have gone out of their riders’ control. The centre could not go forward, could not go back through its own rear ranks, and could not go sideways into the flames. The dragons had not so much broken the army as fenced it.",
          "The horses went first, as horses always do. A destrier will carry a man into a hedge of spears because it has been trained to, but nothing trains it to walk into a grass fire, and the heavy cavalry that was supposed to be the allied army’s hammer became several thousand panicking animals moving in random directions through their own infantry. After that the collapse was not a decision anybody made. The rear ranks broke away and ran; the front ranks discovered they had nowhere to run to.",
          "The royal standards of the Reach were in the middle of that. Mern had placed himself where a king was expected to be, at the head of his household with his kin around him, which on any ordinary field is courage and on this one was the worst position on the ground. The Gardener banners went down and did not come up again. Loren Lannister, further along the line and with a clearer path to open country, got himself and part of the western horse out of the fire and away.",
          "The pursuit was brief and there was very little of it, because there was very little left to pursue. Aegon’s ten thousand had barely fought. The dragons themselves did not come away clean — the accounts speak of arrow wounds taken while they worked low over the ranks, and at least one of the riders was hurt, though the details vary from telling to telling and none of the injuries proved serious. When the fires burned out the field was a black plain with the shapes of men in it, and men have been calling it the Field of Fire ever since.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The figure usually given is four thousand burned, with perhaps another thousand killed by ordinary weapons, and ten thousand or more carried off the field with burns of the kind that were mostly a slower sentence. Those numbers come to us through chroniclers who liked round figures and had every reason to make the dragons sound like the end of the world, and they should be held loosely. What is not in doubt is the proportion of the harm: the overwhelming majority of the casualties were on one side, and the overwhelming majority of those were caused by fire rather than by steel.",
          "House Gardener was ruined absolutely, root and branch, in the space of an afternoon — a dynasty that had ruled the Reach since the Age of Heroes and had survived every previous disaster by having too many sons to lose. House Lannister was not. Loren kept his life, his castle, his gold and, in time, his lands, and the west came out of the Conquest with its structure entirely intact, which is a large part of why the west spends the next three hundred years being the most dangerous of the great houses.",
          "The house that was made was the one that did not fight. The Tyrells were the Gardeners’ hereditary stewards, holding Highgarden as administrators rather than as lords in their own right. With the royal line gone, the steward yielded the castle and the keys without a siege, and Aegon handed him the whole of the Reach for it. Three centuries of Tyrell wealth, Tyrell marriages and Tyrell ambition begin with one man deciding, correctly, that the war was over.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Strategically it ended the war in the south in a single afternoon. There was no second allied army; there could not be, because the men who would have formed it were on that field. Aegon spent the rest of the Conquest dealing with the north and the Vale by negotiation and with Dorne by an expensive failure, but the question of whether Westeros would be unified had been answered in the grass.",
          "Politically it set the terms of Targaryen rule for the next hundred and fifty years. The Field of Fire is the demonstration that made kneeling rational. Every lord who bent the knee afterwards did so with the arithmetic of that day in mind, and every Targaryen who sat the Iron Throne was obeyed partly because of a battle fought before any of them were born. The throne itself was forged from the swords surrendered on and after that field, which is either a piece of theatre or an unusually honest piece of furniture.",
          "It also created the template. Every later attempt to use dragons against a Westerosi army — and there are a small number of them, spread very thinly across three hundred years — is measured against this one, usually by people who have not noticed that the grass did half the work.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "King Mern IX Gardener died in the fire along with his sons, his grandsons, his brothers, his nephews and cousins — every male of the main line who could have carried the name forward, and most of the cadet branches with them. The last surviving Gardener of any degree did not outlive the year. It is the most complete extinction of a great house in the recorded history of Westeros, and it happened in less time than it takes to eat a meal.",
      "King Loren I Lannister escaped the field, was taken shortly afterwards, and knelt. Aegon confirmed him in Casterly Rock as Lord of the West, a mercy that cost the Targaryens nothing at the time and a great deal later.",
      "Harlen Tyrell, steward of Highgarden, surrendered the castle and was raised to Lord Paramount of the Mander in his dead masters’ place — a promotion the older houses of the Reach resented for the next three hundred years and never quite dared act on.",
    ] },

  { id: "hellholt", name: "The Fall of Meraxes at Hellholt", sub: "10 AC — the First Dornish War",
    meta: [["Who", "Rhaenys Targaryen vs. House Uller and Dorne"], ["Result", "A dragon killed by a scorpion bolt; the war that would not end"],
           ["Commanders", "Queen Rhaenys, on Meraxes · the garrison of Hellholt, House Uller"],
           ["Strength", "One dragon and rider against one castle’s scorpions"],
           ["Ground", "Hellholt, a Dornish castle above the Brimstone, in the deep desert"],
           ["Dragons", "Meraxes — the first of the three Conquest dragons to die"]],
    blurb: "The Conquest’s one clean failure, and the day Westeros learned a scorpion could kill a dragon.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Dorne is the exception to the whole story of the Conquest, and it is the exception for reasons of ground rather than heroism. Every other kingdom in Westeros had cities, castles that mattered, roads an army must use, and lords who could be killed or bought in one afternoon. Dorne had a desert, a scattering of strongholds with nothing worth burning in between them, and a nobility that had spent a thousand years being invaded across the marches and had drawn the appropriate conclusions. Against that, a dragon is a weapon looking for a target that will not present itself.",
      "Aegon and his sisters conquered Dorne on paper twice and each time discovered they had conquered sand. When the dragons came, the Dornish did not muster on a field to be burned; they emptied their castles, went into the desert and the mountains, and let the invaders occupy stone buildings full of nothing. The moment the dragons left, the lords came back down out of the hills and cut the throats of whatever garrison had been posted, and the process began again. There is no Field of Fire to be had against an enemy who declines to assemble.",
      "By the tenth year the Targaryens had settled into a war of reprisal, which is the kind of war dragons are worst at and cruellest in. Rhaenys and Meraxes ranged over Dorne burning towns and castles as punishment for a resistance that punishment only fed. She was doing this over Hellholt, the seat of House Uller in the deep south, a place with a reputation for producing people equally given to poison and to fury. The garrison had done something almost no Dornish garrison had done in ten years of war: instead of running, it had mounted its scorpions and waited.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Hellholt sits above the Brimstone, a river that runs yellow and stinking with sulphur out of the mountains, in about the least hospitable corner of an inhospitable country. It is not a great castle and it commanded nothing an invader wanted. Its only importance to history is what its garrison happened to be holding when Meraxes came over the walls.",
          "A scorpion is a mounted crossbow scaled up to throw a bolt the size of a spear. It is a siege weapon, meant to punch through shields and mantlets and the timber of an enemy’s works, and until this day nobody had recorded one being used successfully against a dragon in flight. The problem with shooting a dragon is not the toughness of the animal so much as the difficulty of the shot: a dragon on the wing is fast, high, and moving in three dimensions, and a scorpion is slow to load and slow to traverse. To hit one you need it to come low, come close, and hold something like a straight line — which is exactly what a dragon does when its rider brings it down to burn a wall.",
          "Rhaenys had been burning Dorne for years and had every reason to think Hellholt was one more afternoon’s work. Meraxes was a great dragon, third of the three that had won the continent, and had faced Dornish arrows before without consequence. The garrison had one advantage and it was a single-use advantage: they knew she was coming, they had loaded their engines, and they were prepared to lose the exchange of fire in order to win the one shot that mattered.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "There was no battle in any ordinary sense — an attack by one dragon on one castle lasts minutes, and this one lasted less. Rhaenys brought Meraxes down over Hellholt to do to it what she had done to a dozen other Dornish seats. The garrison’s scorpions loosed as the dragon came in low across the walls.",
          "One bolt found Meraxes in the eye. Precisely how — whether it was a marksman’s shot, a lucky one out of a volley, or, as the more romantic Dornish accounts have it, a single defender holding his nerve until the animal filled his sight — is not recoverable and the sources flatter whichever party is telling it. What is not disputed is the result. A dragon shot through the eye into the brain does not glide down; it drops, all at once, with its full weight, and it drops wherever it happens to be, which in this case was onto the castle it had come to burn.",
          "Meraxes came down inside the walls of Hellholt and the impact wrecked a portion of the castle as thoroughly as fire would have. Rhaenys was in the saddle, and what became of her from that instant is the hinge of the whole affair and belongs in the fold below — for the reason it has never been settled is a simple one: the only two witnesses who could have told it were the dragon and the castle, and both were destroyed in the same moment.",
          "This is the whole of the engagement. There was no host to rout, no pursuit, no second phase. A single spear-length bolt, launched from a weapon that had been sitting in Westerosi castles for centuries, ended in a few seconds the career of an animal that two kingdoms had failed to stop with fifty-five thousand men.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the Targaryens a third of their dragons and, far more importantly, the myth that their dragons could not be touched. Balerion and Vhagar were still alive and enormous, but the arithmetic of the realm had quietly changed. A weapon existed, it was already installed in every serious castle, and it had been proved to work. Every scorpion in Westeros looked different after Hellholt, and the Targaryens knew it.",
          "It cost Dorne everything short of surrender and then some. Aegon’s grief and his fury — the two are hard to separate in the accounts — turned the war from reprisal into something much worse. The years that follow are remembered in Dorne as the time the dragons stopped taking castles and started simply burning the country, hunting the Dornish lords with a persistence that had not been there before. It did not work. It never works. But a very large number of people died proving it did not work.",
          "And it cost the war any prospect of ending well. What had been a difficult conquest became a blood feud with a body count on the Targaryen side that no other kingdom had come close to inflicting, and it stayed unresolved for longer than any of the principals lived.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Strategically it ended the Conquest as an unbroken run of success. Aegon the Conqueror conquered six kingdoms and failed at the seventh, and the failure is instructive precisely because it was not a failure of force — he had more force than anyone in history. It was a failure of the whole theory that force decides things, run up against a people who had decided in advance not to give the force anything to land on.",
          "It also fixed Dorne’s peculiar status for the next century and a half. Dorne was not part of the realm after this; it was a thing the realm had failed to swallow and had agreed, exhausted, to leave alone. It came into the Seven Kingdoms in the end through a marriage two hundred years later, on its own terms, keeping its own laws — which is the single most successful piece of resistance in the recorded history of Westeros, and it was bought partly with the Queen the realm lost at Hellholt.",
          "The narrower lesson was the one about the scorpion, and it was learned and then, characteristically, half-forgotten. It resurfaces every time anyone in the following centuries has to fight a dragon on purpose, and the people who remember Hellholt do noticeably better than the people who do not.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Queen Rhaenys Targaryen died at Hellholt when Meraxes fell, in the eleventh year after the Conquest. Her body was never returned and its fate was never established. The Dornish let it be known, in the years afterward, that she had not died on impact — that she had lived a while under the ruin, or been taken, and that things had been done to her; the tales are numerous, mutually contradictory, and were clearly designed to be exactly as unbearable to Aegon as they in fact were. No version can be confirmed and several were plainly invented as weapons. The honest position is that she died at Hellholt and that everything past that point is Dornish psychological warfare, some of which may nonetheless be true.",
      "Meraxes was the first of the three Conquest dragons to die, killed by a weapon a footman could operate, which is the detail every later enemy of the Targaryens found most encouraging. Her skull, by some accounts, remained at Hellholt for years as a trophy of the one castle in Westeros that had killed a dragon.",
      "Aegon and Visenya answered with the years the Dornish call the Dragon’s Wroth, and it settled nothing. The war guttered out into an exhausted, unofficial peace that held less because either side wanted it than because both had run out of things to burn. The Conqueror died with Dorne still outside his realm, the only ambition of his life he did not achieve.",
    ] },

  { id: "redgrass-field", name: "The Redgrass Field", sub: "196 AC — the First Blackfyre Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "Daeron II vs. Daemon Blackfyre"], ["Result", "The Blackfyres broken, for a generation"],
           ["Commanders", "Prince Maekar and Prince Baelor Breakspear · Daemon Blackfyre and Aegor Rivers"],
           ["Strength", "Two large hosts of comparable size; no reliable count survives"],
           ["Ground", "Open field with a low ridge on one flank, in the riverlands"]],
    blurb: "The bloodiest battle between two Targaryen claimants before the Dance was refought in miniature.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Aegon IV spent his reign making the realm worse on purpose and then, dying, legitimised every bastard he had ever fathered. It was a spiteful act with no policy behind it, and it worked exactly as spite does: it did not unseat his heir, it simply guaranteed that somebody would try. Chief among the legitimised was Daemon, a young man of extraordinary physical gifts who had been given the ancestral Valyrian steel sword of the house — a gift with only one possible meaning, whatever the old king claimed he intended by it.",
      "Daeron II was the opposite of his father in every way that the realm found difficult. He was scholarly, patient, unmartial, and he had done the single most useful thing any Targaryen king ever did by bringing Dorne into the realm through marriage and treaty rather than fire. That settlement was also his weakness. A great many lords of the marches and the stormlands had spent their lives fighting Dornishmen, had lost fathers and sons to them, and now watched Dornish lords at court taking offices and honours. There were also whispers, carefully tended, that Daeron was not truly his father’s son at all.",
      "So the rebellion had a candidate, a grievance, a sword and a faction, and it broke in 196. Both sides raised their strength, manoeuvred through the riverlands, and finally accepted battle on an open field, because Daemon Blackfyre’s entire theory of himself required a field. He was a magnificent warrior with a magnificent sword and he genuinely believed that if the two hosts met and he was seen at the front of his, enough of the other side would decide the gods had spoken. It was not an absurd belief. It very nearly happened.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The field was flat, broad and unremarkable, with one feature that mattered: a low rise along one side, wooded enough at the crest to hide men. Nothing about the ground favoured either army. Both sides had come to fight a straightforward battle of hosts, and both got one, which is precisely why it was so expensive.",
          "The rebel host was built around Daemon himself and around a hard core of stormlanders, marcher lords and westermen with grudges. His chief lieutenant was his half-brother Aegor Rivers, called Bittersteel — another of the old king’s legitimised bastards, and a man whose defining quality was that he never in his life let anything go. Where Daemon fought for the throne, Bittersteel fought against the people who had it. That difference in motive shows up later.",
          "The loyalist host was commanded in the field by Daeron’s second son, Prince Maekar, a hard and humourless soldier who took the position of most exposure and held it. Somewhere out of sight, still marching, was the king’s heir, Prince Baelor Breakspear, bringing the stormlands loyalists and the Dornish spears up on a separate line — a deliberate arrangement, and the pivot the whole day turns on. And on the wooded rise stood a third half-brother, Brynden Rivers, called Bloodraven, with a company of archers he had raised and trained himself and who were not much liked by anyone.",
          "Neither host’s numbers are recorded in any form worth repeating. They were large by the standards of the century and closely matched, and the accounts agree that both sides expected the day to be decided by weight and by Daemon.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It opened as a collision and stayed one for most of the day. The two lines met, ground into each other, and refused to break. This is the rarest kind of medieval battle and by far the most lethal: a field battle is usually decided in its first hour by one side’s nerve failing, and when neither side’s nerve fails, what happens instead is hours of close work in armour in the sun.",
          "Daemon fought at the front of his centre, on foot and then mounted and then on foot again, and by every account — including the ones written afterwards by men with a professional obligation to dislike him — he was the best thing on the field by a distance. His portion of the line went forward. Maekar’s van took the worst of it and was slowly driven back, and for a long stretch of the afternoon the loyalist centre was being eaten.",
          "The moment that decided it came from the rise. Bloodraven had kept his archers out of the melee entirely, which cost him a great deal of reputation among men who thought that unchivalrous, and waited until the rebel command group was fixed in place and identifiable. Then he had them shoot — not a general volley into the mass, but concentrated shooting at a handful of specific men in the press below. It was, by the standards of the day, an ugly way to fight and an extremely effective one. The rebel centre lost its command in the space of a few minutes.",
          "What followed was the most dangerous half hour of the war for the loyalists, because Bittersteel did not do what a beaten commander is supposed to do. He gathered every horseman he could reach and drove them at the rise. The charge went up into the archers and through them, the two half-brothers met at the top with the whole battle burning below them, and Bloodraven came away from it having paid an eye for the ridge. For a short time it looked as though the rebellion might be rescued by pure rage.",
          "It was not, because of the arrangement made days earlier. Baelor Breakspear’s column came onto the field from the flank and rear while Bittersteel’s horse was committed uphill and the rebel line was already leaderless in its centre. A host caught between a line it cannot break and a fresh host it did not know was coming has no third option. The rebel army folded from the flank inward, and once folding started it did not stop.",
          "The pursuit ran into the evening and was thorough. Bittersteel got a body of survivors clear and off the field in reasonable order, which given the circumstances is a considerable feat and one he was never given credit for by anybody who mattered. The rest scattered into the riverlands and were hunted through it for days.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The figure the singers settled on is ten thousand dead, split roughly between the two hosts, and while that has the suspicious roundness of all such numbers it is not obviously inflated for a battle that went the whole day at close quarters. It was, up to that point, the most expensive single field in Westeros since the Dance.",
          "The families ruined were mostly marcher and stormland houses that had backed Daemon out of hatred for the Dornish settlement, and they were ruined in the usual way: attainders, forfeitures, and sons sent to the Wall. Daeron’s peace afterwards was noticeably milder than it could have been, which is entirely in character and which a good many loyalists thought was a mistake.",
          "The men who were made were the two archers’ men — in the sense that both of Daeron’s half-brothers walked off that field into the roles they would occupy for the rest of their lives. Bloodraven had won the battle by a method nobody wanted to praise, and got power, an eye’s worth of scar tissue, and a reputation as the kind of man you do not turn your back on. Bittersteel had lost it and got the certainty that the thing was unfinished.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It kept the throne with Daeron’s line and it settled, for the time being, that the Dornish settlement would hold. That is the concrete result, and it is not small: every later Targaryen king down to the end of the dynasty sits where he sits because of that afternoon.",
          "What it did not do was end anything. The rebellion had been built on a real grievance held by real houses, and beating an army does not unmake a grievance. The Blackfyre claim survived the field, went into exile across the narrow sea, and came back at intervals for the better part of a century, each time thinner and each time expensive.",
          "The deeper change was in how the realm thought about legitimacy. Before the Redgrass Field, a bastard with a sword and a following was a rumour. Afterwards he was a category. Every Targaryen king for four generations governs with one eye on the far shore, and a good deal of the crown’s foreign policy in the century that follows makes no sense at all unless you remember this field.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Daemon Blackfyre was shot down in the press by Bloodraven’s archers along with his twin sons Aegon and Aemon, who had been fighting beside him. The three of them went in the same volley. His champion Quentyn Ball, called Fireball, had already been killed in a chance encounter the day before the battle and never reached the field at all — a small accident that arguably cost the rebellion as much as anything that happened on the day.",
      "Aegor Rivers, Bittersteel, escaped the field and took ship for the Free Cities with several of Daemon’s surviving sons. There he founded the Golden Company out of the wreckage of the rebellion, an exile army with a standing promise to come home, and he spent the remainder of his life engineering further attempts. Neither he nor Brynden Rivers ever stopped fighting the other; both outlived the reign the battle preserved.",
      "Prince Baelor Breakspear, whose flank march won the day and who was by common consent the best king Westeros never got, died a few years later of a blow taken in a tourney melee. The succession that the Redgrass Field secured passed instead down Maekar’s line, with consequences that took two more generations to arrive.",
    ] },

  { id: "rooks-rest", name: "Rook's Rest", sub: "129 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "Aegon II & Aemond vs. Rhaenys Targaryen"], ["Result", "The first dragon killed by dragons"],
           ["Commanders", "Ser Criston Cole for the greens · Lord Staunton in the castle, Rhaenys Targaryen in the air"],
           ["Strength", "A green field army of moderate size against one castle and one dragon"],
           ["Ground", "A minor castle on the Crownlands coast, with wooded high ground close by"],
           ["Dragons", "Meleys · Vhagar · Sunfyre"]],
    blurb: "A modest Crownlands castle where both sides learned that dragons die.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The Dance of the Dragons opened as a paperwork war. Viserys I had named his daughter Rhaenyra heir and made his lords swear to her; on his death his widow’s faction crowned his eldest son Aegon instead, and for the first several months the fighting consisted of letters, arrests and the quiet business of finding out which castles would answer which raven. Both sides had dragons and neither side wanted to be the first to use them properly, because everyone with any sense understood what a dragon war would mean and hoped the other side would collapse before it came to that.",
      "The greens held King’s Landing but not the sea approaches to it. Corlys Velaryon had taken his fleet over to the blacks, and with it the Gullet, which meant the capital could be starved on the crown’s own doorstep. Meanwhile the Crownlands were a patchwork: castles a day’s ride from the city had declared for Rhaenyra and were sitting there unmolested, which is intolerable for a new regime that needs to look inevitable.",
      "Ser Criston Cole, Hand and commander of the green field army, went out to fix that. He took the small castles east of the city in turn, and the campaign had a second purpose that he did not advertise. Every castle he reduced was a demand for the blacks to respond. Dragonstone could not watch its declared friends be picked off one at a time and keep the friends it had left. Criston was not really besieging castles. He was fishing.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Rook’s Rest is a modest seat on the Crownlands coast, held by a lord who had come out for Rhaenyra early and loudly, and it is not the sort of place a war turns on. It has walls, a keep and a good enough position to be annoying, and nothing else. What made it the right bait was distance: near enough to Dragonstone that help could arrive within the day, far enough from King’s Landing that help would not expect what was waiting.",
          "The important terrain was not the castle at all but the country around it — wooded high ground within easy reach of the walls, and the sky above it. Criston brought a competent field army, siege equipment and, critically, scorpions and heavy bows sited to shoot upward rather than at the walls. He also brought no dragon of his own where anyone could see one.",
          "The blacks’ council on Dragonstone argued about the response and, according to every account, argued badly. What was sent was Rhaenys Targaryen — the Queen Who Never Was, sixty-odd years old, a rider of enormous experience on Meleys, a red she-dragon fast enough to be worth a great deal more than her age suggested. Whether she flew out as one part of a considered plan, or because she took the decision herself when the council would not take one, is a point on which the surviving histories disagree in a way that flatters whichever party each historian favoured.",
          "Concealed nearby, out of sight of both the castle and the sky, waited the other two. Aemond Targaryen had Vhagar, the last dragon of the Conquest, old, enormous and by a wide margin the most dangerous animal alive. King Aegon II had Sunfyre, a young dragon of great beauty and no great size. That both of them were present, and whether Aegon was expected to be, is another disputed point. Some accounts have Criston summoning the king; others have him arriving unannounced and unwanted, having decided that his war needed him to be seen in it.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was an ordinary assault. Criston’s men went at Rook’s Rest in earnest — properly, not as a feint, because a feint would have been read as one. The garrison held the walls and sent up smoke, and within a few hours the answer came out of the east.",
          "Rhaenys came in low over the besiegers and did what a dragon does to a siege line: she broke it open along its length, scattered the assault, and set the siege works burning. For a short interval the battle was simply over. A single dragon committed against unprepared infantry in the open is not a contest, and the accounts of this phase read like the Field of Fire in miniature. Criston’s host took real losses here, and he let it happen, which tells you what the losses were for.",
          "Then the trap closed. Vhagar and Sunfyre came up out of concealment and took Meleys from two sides at once. This is the moment the entire war pivots on, and it is worth being precise about why it worked. Dragons in the air are not soldiers in a line; a dragonrider’s advantage is height, speed and the ability to choose the pass. Rhaenys had spent hers coming down onto the siege lines. She was low, slow and committed, with an enemy above her and an enemy behind her, and there was no altitude left to trade for escape.",
          "Whether she could have run is the great argument about Rook’s Rest. Meleys was the faster animal and open sky lay to the east. One tradition holds that she was pinned before she understood what was happening and had no choice at all. Another insists she saw both of them, understood the arithmetic exactly, and turned into them anyway — on the reasoning that if the greens were going to have their king and their best rider in one place, this was the only chance anyone would ever get to remove both. The two readings produce very different women and the sources do not settle it.",
          "What is not disputed is that she fought. Meleys went at them rather than away, and the three animals came together in a tangle that dropped out of the sky as one mass. The fighting from that point was not aerial manoeuvre but something closer to a dogfight on the ground: claws, teeth and fire at contact range, with the riders along for it. Vhagar’s size was decisive. Weight tells in a grapple, and Vhagar was heavier than the other two together.",
          "Sunfyre came out of it wrecked — a wing ruined, unable to fly, and the king with him. Aegon II fell a considerable distance in the collapse and was picked out of the wreckage burned across much of his body with the bones on one side broken. Whether Aemond struck at his own brother in the confusion, or whether Aegon simply flew into the path of a fight he had no business joining, is the single most contested question of the whole war. The chroniclers of the day mostly ascribed it to accident; one notorious and unreliable source insisted it was deliberate; on the screen it is staged as an unmistakable choice. The honest answer is that only one man knew and he never said.",
          "With the sky cleared, the assault on the castle resumed and Rook’s Rest was carried by storm before the day was out. Criston had his castle. That was never the point of the day and everyone involved knew it.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "Meleys was destroyed — the first dragon in a hundred and fifty years to be killed by another dragon, and the moment the war stopped being a dispute and became something the whole realm should have been terrified of. The head was carried into King’s Landing on a cart and shown to the crowds, which was intended as reassurance and functioned, for anyone paying attention, as an announcement that dragons were mortal.",
          "The green host lost heavily in the opening phase — Criston paid a real price in men for the bait, and it went unremarked because the bait worked. The garrison and household of Rook’s Rest were destroyed with the castle when it fell, and the castle itself was left a shell for years.",
          "The king’s injuries were the most consequential casualty of the day. Aegon II was carried back to the capital insensible, and he remained incapable for most of the rest of the war. Sunfyre never properly flew again in the sense of being a weapon, though he crawls back into the story later in a way nobody expected.",
          "The man made by Rook’s Rest was Aemond. With his brother out of action and the realm’s largest dragon under him, he became the effective power in King’s Landing and stayed there. The man made and then quietly ruined was Criston Cole, who had conceived and executed the most successful operation of the war and got, for it, a crippled king and a colleague who no longer needed him.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Rook’s Rest is where the Dance turns from a succession dispute into a war of mutual destruction. Before it, both sides had dragons and both sides were, in effect, holding them back as deterrents. Afterwards the deterrent was gone, because it had been proved that a dragon could be trapped and killed by other dragons if you were willing to arrange it. Everything that follows — the hunts, the ambushes, the dragonseeds put into saddles by people who should have known better — proceeds from that demonstration.",
          "It also changed the leadership of both sides at a stroke, one by injury and one by loss. The blacks lost their most experienced rider and, with her, a certain amount of restraint at their council table. The greens lost a king to his bed and replaced him in practice with a one-eyed nineteen-year-old whose approach to policy was to burn whatever had annoyed him most recently.",
          "And it hardened a habit of mind that the war never recovered from: the belief on both sides that the answer to the other side’s dragon was your own dragon. That is the thinking that carries them all the way to the Gods Eye and the empty sky afterwards.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Rhaenys Targaryen died on that field with Meleys. Whether she fell in the air or in the wreck on the ground is not agreed; the accounts that had access to the body are silent on the manner and enthusiastic about the fact. She had been passed over for the throne once by a Great Council and once by the terms of her cousin’s will, had watched her own children’s inheritance go elsewhere, and had gone on fighting for her house anyway. She was the most competent commander either side possessed and neither side had listened to her.",
      "Aegon II survived his burns but never fully recovered from them; he spent months insensible, ruled in name only while his brother ruled in fact, and remained in pain for the rest of a reign that had barely started.",
      "The dispute over whether Aemond fired on his brother deliberately was never settled and became, in the years after the war, the standard example maesters use when teaching that eyewitness testimony and political convenience are hard to tell apart.",
    ] },

  { id: "honeywine", name: "The Battle of the Honeywine", sub: "129 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "The Hightower host vs. the black Reachlords"], ["Result", "A green victory that opened the road to Oldtown"],
           ["Commanders", "Ser Ormund Hightower · Lord Thaddeus Rowan and the black Reachlords"],
           ["Strength", "A large green host against a smaller and cornered black one"],
           ["Ground", "The banks of the Honeywine, on the approaches to Oldtown"],
           ["Dragons", "One arrived late and decided it — Daeron Targaryen on Tessarion"]],
    blurb: "A land battle in the Reach that a boy on a blue dragon turned at the last hour.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The Dance is usually told as a war of dragons over the Crownlands and the riverlands, which is where the famous names died. The Reach is the part that gets left out, and it should not be, because it is where the largest green army of the war was raised and where the war on the ground was actually won. Oldtown and Highgarden were the two poles of it. Highgarden’s Tyrells were a child and a regency and effectively sat the war out; Oldtown’s Hightowers were rich, numerous, and firmly for the greens, not least because Queen Alicent was a Hightower and the whole green cause was in a real sense a Hightower family project.",
      "Ser Ormund Hightower raised the strength of Oldtown and the southern Reach for King Aegon II and began marching it up toward the fighting. This was a serious army, one of the largest fielded by either side in the whole war, and its mere existence was a problem for the blacks, because if it reached the riverlands intact it would tip the balance of numbers there decisively. The blacks needed it stopped in the Reach, and they had partisans there to do it — Reachlords who had declared for Rhaenyra and who now tried to bring Ormund to battle before he could get clear of his own country.",
      "So the black Reachlords, under Thaddeus Rowan and others, moved to trap Ormund’s host against the Honeywine before it could gather its full weight. On paper it was a reasonable plan and it went reasonably for exactly as long as the battle stayed a matter of men.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Honeywine is the river that runs down to Oldtown, and the fighting took place on its banks on the seaward side of the Reach, close enough to the city that the outcome would decide whether Oldtown itself was threatened. A river battle constrains both armies; it fixes their flanks and it makes manoeuvre expensive, which tends to reward the larger and better-supplied host.",
          "That was the Hightowers. Ormund’s army was big, well-found and drawn from the most populous stretch of the most populous kingdom in Westeros. Against it the black Reachlords had assembled a coalition of houses that had declared for Rhaenyra, smaller in total and — the recurring weakness of the black cause everywhere — less unified in command.",
          "The one thing the greens did not initially have on the field was a dragon that could fight. That changed during the campaign, and the change is the whole point of the battle. Prince Daeron Targaryen, the youngest of Alicent’s three sons, a boy in his early teens, had been fostered at Oldtown and rode a young blue dragon named Tessarion, the Blue Queen. He and his dragon were with or near Ormund’s host, and the blacks either did not know it or did not weight it properly.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening went to the blacks, or at least did not go against them, which for the smaller army was the same as winning. The two hosts met along the river and the fighting was hard and even; for a good part of the day the black Reachlords were pressing Ormund’s army and there was a real prospect of pinning it against the water and breaking it. This is the phase the black accounts dwell on, because it is the last part of the day that suited them.",
          "Then Tessarion came down. A dragon entering a land battle that has been fought to a standstill does not need to kill many men to decide it; it needs to break the shape of one army while the other holds together, and a fresh, frightened animal ridden by a boy with everything to prove is entirely capable of that. Daeron brought the blue dragon onto the black host and the black host came apart. What had been an even fight became a rout in the span it takes a formation to decide it has had enough.",
          "The greens turned the collapse into a proper victory. With the black Reachlords broken and running, Ormund’s army was not merely saved but freed — the force that had been sent to trap it was destroyed, and the road up through the Reach and out toward the wider war was open. Several of the black leaders were taken; the coalition that had tried to stop the Hightowers in the Reach effectively ceased to exist as a field army.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The black cause in the Reach lost its army and, with it, the ability to keep the largest green host in the war penned in its own country. That is the concrete cost and it was large: everything Ormund Hightower’s army did afterward — and it did a great deal, some of it catastrophic for both sides — proceeds from its not having been destroyed on the Honeywine.",
          "It made Daeron Targaryen. He was knighted for the battle, given the byname Daeron the Daring, and became for a season the great hope of the green cause in the Reach — a boy who had won a battle from the air the way his ancestors were supposed to, at a point in the war when the greens badly needed a dragon victory that did not also cost them a king. The reputation was real and it was earned. What it led him toward is a fold-below matter.",
          "The material cost in men fell overwhelmingly on the black side and is not recorded with any precision, which is the standard state of affairs for every number in this war.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It secured Oldtown and the south of the Reach for the greens and it released Ormund Hightower’s host to march north and become one of the decisive instruments of the later war. The Honeywine is the reason the Reach ends the Dance as green country, and the reason a great green army was loose in the upper Reach and the riverlands in the war’s worst year.",
          "It also, in a smaller way, is a corrective to the way the Dance is usually remembered. The famous engagements are the dragon duels, and they were mostly ruinous for both sides. The Honeywine is one of the few battles of the war where a dragon was used the way the Conquest used them — decisively, from the air, against men who had no answer — and it belonged to the greens, and it is almost never mentioned.",
          "For the boy who won it, it set a trajectory. A thirteen-year-old who wins a battle and a byname in the same afternoon is a thirteen-year-old who will be at the next one, and the next one was Tumbleton.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ser Ormund Hightower survived the Honeywine and marched his host north to further victories that made him, for a brief while, one of the most successful commanders of the war on either side. His run ended at Tumbleton, which has its own entry; he did not outlive that field.",
      "Prince Daeron Targaryen, Daeron the Daring, came out of the Honeywine a knight and a hero at an age when most boys are still squires. He fought on with Ormund’s army and with Tessarion. His war, and his life, ran on into the two battles of Tumbleton and did not survive them; what became of him and his blue dragon belongs to that account.",
      "The black Reachlords who were taken were, on the whole, dealt with less harshly than the losers of most battles in this war, largely because the greens had bigger problems elsewhere and the Reach still had to be governed. Thaddeus Rowan’s own road ran on into the war’s uglier back half and ended badly, at the hands of his own side’s regime rather than the enemy’s.",
    ] },

  { id: "butchers-ball", name: "The Butcher's Ball", sub: "130 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "The black riverlords vs. Ser Criston Cole’s host"], ["Result", "A green army destroyed on the march; the Kingmaker killed"],
           ["Commanders", "Ser Criston Cole for the greens · the black riverlords, with dragons overhead"],
           ["Strength", "A green host caught strung out on the march against a converging black force"],
           ["Ground", "Open country in the riverlands, near a place called the Butcher’s Ball ever since"],
           ["Dragons", "Two black dragons on the field; the greens had none"]],
    blurb: "The man who engineered the whole green war walked his army into open country with no dragon, and the riverlands closed on it.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Ser Criston Cole is the closest thing the green cause had to an architect. He had crowned Aegon II, he had led the field army, and he had conceived the trap at Rook’s Rest that killed the first dragon of the war. He was called the Kingmaker and he had earned it. He was also, by the second year of the war, a man whose luck and judgement had both started to leave him at the same time, which is the most dangerous condition a successful commander can be in, because his confidence has not yet been told.",
      "After the fighting turned ugly and the riverlands became the war’s open wound, Criston took a green host out from the capital to campaign in that country. The riverlands were black-leaning, hostile, and — this is the part that undid him — full of dragons. Rhaenyra’s partisans there had riders, and Criston’s host had none. He was marching a large body of men on foot and horse through open country that the enemy could watch from the air whenever it chose.",
      "The black riverlords, gathering their strength, did the obvious thing, which Criston appears not to have believed they would do in time. They converged on him while he was on the march, strung out along a road with his baggage and his order of march exactly as vulnerable as an army in column always is. He was caught in the open, in country that suited the enemy, with no way to answer what was above him.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The battle takes its grim name from the field it was fought on, which is open riverlands country of no particular note except that a green army died there. There was no river crossing to hold, no ridge to anchor on, no castle to put a back against — just ground, and an army caught on it in the wrong formation.",
          "Criston’s host was a conventional green field army, decent troops badly placed. An army on the march is not an army in a battle line; it is a long thin thing optimised for moving, and turning it into a fighting formation takes time and space and, above all, warning. Criston got very little of any of them.",
          "The black force was a coalition of riverlords who had come together specifically to catch him, and its decisive component was not the men but the dragons over them. Two black dragons were on the field. That single fact settles the character of what followed: this was not a battle between two hosts so much as the destruction of a host that had no answer to the enemy’s principal weapon, on ground where it could not hide from it.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It was less a battle than an interception. The black riverlords closed on Criston’s column and pinned it before it could get itself into a proper line, and the dragons worked it from above while the riverland horse and foot came in on the ground. An army that is being burned from the air cannot form up to meet the enemy on the ground, and an army that is being pressed on the ground cannot scatter to escape the dragons; the two threats together left Criston’s host with no formation it could adopt that answered either one.",
          "The green host was broken and then slaughtered. The name the field acquired — the Butcher’s Ball — is the plainest description any battle in this war was given, and it was given by people who saw it. This was not a fight decided by manoeuvre and resolved by a rout with a few casualties; it was a killing. A large green army marched into that country and did not march out of it.",
          "What became of the man who had made the whole green war is a matter for the fold below, and it is kept there. What can be said in the open is that the destruction was total: a large green army marched into that country and did not march out of it, and the commander who had crowned a king and conceived the trap that killed the blacks’ first dragon got, for all of it, no victory to carry away from the last field he took the field on. There was no clever stroke, no trap, no last stand that turned anything. He had walked into open country without a dragon in a war that had become about dragons, and the country did to his host exactly what he had arranged for the blacks at Rook’s Rest.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The greens lost a field army and their most capable commander in the same afternoon, at a point in the war when they could replace neither. His loss took from the green side the one man who had consistently understood how to use its advantages, and the vacuum it left was filled — as vacuums in this war always were — by people with more dragon than judgement.",
          "The riverlands were confirmed as black country, held by black lords, and lethal to any green army that entered them without air cover. That lesson cost the greens an army to learn and they did not entirely act on it.",
          "The man made by the Butcher’s Ball was, in a sense, nobody — it was a battle that destroyed a reputation rather than building one. The black riverlords who won it are not much remembered as individuals, because the war’s memory attaches to riders and their dragons, and the men who ground down Criston Cole’s host were, for the most part, ordinary lords doing ordinary lethal work on their own ground.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It ended the green field army as a coherent instrument for a crucial stretch of the war and it removed the green cause’s ablest strategist. Everything the greens did afterward on land in the riverlands was improvised, and it showed.",
          "It also confirmed the war’s central, ruinous logic one more time: that on any given field, the side with a dragon beats the side without one, almost regardless of numbers or ground or skill, and that the only reliable answer to a dragon had turned out to be another dragon. Criston Cole had understood this better than anyone alive and it beat him anyway, because understanding a rule and having the means to obey it are different things.",
          "For the shape of the whole Dance, the Butcher’s Ball is one of the battles that pushed both sides past restraint. The green council had now lost a king to injury, its architect to the riverlands, and its patience entirely; what it reached for next were the dragonseeds and the mad gambles of the war’s final year.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ser Criston Cole, the Kingmaker, Hand of the King and Lord Commander of the Kingsguard, died at the Butcher’s Ball. The accounts have him refusing to flee and offering himself to be shot down where he stood, or cut down in the general destruction of his host; the versions differ and none is certain, but they agree he did not run and did not survive. He had crowned a king, led an army, and killed a dragon, and he died in a field named for slaughter with none of it having been enough. Whatever one makes of the man — and opinions of Criston Cole were violent in every direction even at the time — he did not outlive the war he had done more than anyone to start.",
      "The green cause never replaced him. The commanders who inherited his war were braver, less careful, and worse, and the disasters of the war’s last year owe a good deal to the absence of the one man who might have talked the green council out of them.",
      "The riverlords who destroyed his army went on fighting a war that, from this point, neither side could win in any way that left much standing. The Butcher’s Ball is one of the fields where the Dance stops being a contest and starts being an act of mutual exhaustion.",
    ] },

  { id: "tumbleton", name: "The First and Second Battles of Tumbleton", sub: "130 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "Two green dragonriders turn their coats, twice"], ["Result", "A town destroyed; two dragons dead; the green army wrecked from within"],
           ["Commanders", "Ser Ormund Hightower and the green host · the town’s black defenders · two treasonous dragonseeds"],
           ["Strength", "A great green army against a garrisoned town, and then the army against itself"],
           ["Ground", "Tumbleton, a market town on the road to King’s Landing"],
           ["Dragons", "Four in all across the two battles; two of them died, and the treachery ran both directions"]],
    blurb: "The largest green army of the war was betrayed into a town, and then the town’s conquerors were betrayed in their turn.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "By the last stretch of the Dance both sides had run short of the one thing the war was actually decided by, which was reliable dragonriders. The greens had lost Rhaenys’s killer at Rook’s Rest to a crippled king, and their field commanders were dying. The blacks, desperate for riders, had done the reckless thing and put common-born people of suspected Targaryen blood — the dragonseeds — onto the riderless dragons of Dragonstone. Some of these people were loyal. Some were opportunists who had been handed the single most valuable thing in the world and had their own ideas about what to do with it. Two of them, Hugh Hammer and Ulf White, are the reason Tumbleton is remembered.",
      "Ormund Hightower’s green host — the same army that had been saved on the Honeywine — had marched up from the Reach growing as it went and was now the largest army in the war, driving toward King’s Landing along the roseroad. It had picked up dragons: young Daeron on Tessarion from the start, and then, extraordinarily, two black dragonseeds who deserted the black cause and brought their dragons over to the greens. Hugh Hammer rode Vermithor, the old Bronze Fury, once the mount of the Old King himself and one of the largest dragons then living. Ulf White rode Silverwing, another great old dragon. The greens had, by treachery, acquired an overwhelming advantage in the air, and they marched it at Tumbleton.",
      "Tumbleton is a market town on the road to the capital, held for Rhaenyra, garrisoned and prepared to resist. It had walls of a sort and a large body of black-loyal men gathered to hold the road. What it did not have was any answer to four dragons, two of which had been on the enemy’s own side the week before.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The town and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Tumbleton was a prosperous town rather than a fortress — walls enough to matter against men, nothing at all against dragons. Its garrison was substantial, swollen by the levies of black-loyal lords who had come to hold the roseroad, and it was betrayed before the fighting even started. Two of the lords inside the town, seeing the size of what was coming, changed sides and opened the way. Treachery is the medium the whole battle is written in; it runs through every phase of it, in every direction, and no one who fought there comes out clean.",
          "The green host was enormous by the standards of the war and its air power was, briefly, decisive: four dragons — Tessarion, Vermithor, Silverwing, and the young Daeron’s command of the whole — against a town with none. On paper this was the most one-sided matchup of the entire Dance.",
          "The trouble, invisible on paper, was that two of those four dragons were ridden by men with no loyalty to anyone, who had already betrayed one side and had learned that a dragon makes its rider a power in his own right. The greens had bought an overwhelming force and had not asked closely enough what they had bought it with.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The first battle was quick and horrible. The green dragons burned Tumbleton. With the town betrayed from within and four dragons overhead, the black defence never had a coherent shape to lose; the walls were irrelevant, the garrison was scattered and slaughtered, and the town itself was set alight and largely destroyed. As a military operation it was a total green success. As anything else it was a massacre, and the sack that followed — the green host, and particularly its dragonseed riders and their followers, turning on the town’s people — was one of the worst atrocities of a war that had a great many.",
          "And then the victory rotted where it stood, because the two dragonseeds had no reason to stop. Hugh Hammer and Ulf White had crossed to the greens for advantage, not for love of Aegon II, and with Vermithor and Silverwing under them they were now among the most powerful individuals in the realm and behaved accordingly. The green host halted at Tumbleton and dissolved into feasting, looting and intrigue, its two most dangerous members openly speculating about crowns of their own. The largest army in the war stopped being an army and became a mob with dragons camped in a burned town.",
          "The Second Battle of Tumbleton is what happened when the blacks came back for it. A black host came up to retake the town, and the sequence of that day is genuinely tangled in the sources — a night attack, confusion in the dark, dragons roused into a fight none of their riders had planned. What is clear is the outcome. Two dragons were lost at Tumbleton in that second fight, and the great green army that had rolled up from the Reach effectively destroyed itself there. The treachery that had won the town cut the other way in the end, as treachery tends to; men who had already changed sides once were trusted by no one, watched by everyone, and stood in as much danger from their own side as from the enemy.",
          "The two battles have to be told together because the second is entirely the consequence of the first. The greens won Tumbleton so completely that winning it destroyed them: it stopped their army, corrupted it with plunder, and handed its fate to two men who could not be controlled. By the time the blacks came back, the thing that needed defeating had largely defeated itself.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the war two more of its dwindling dragons and it cost the greens their largest army, which arrived at Tumbleton as a war-winning instrument and left it as scattered survivors. The strategic momentum the Hightowers had built all the way from Oldtown ended in that burned town.",
          "It cost Tumbleton itself everything. The town was destroyed and its people massacred, and the accounts of the sack are among the passages the chroniclers of the Dance write most plainly, because there is nothing to dress up. This is one of the battles that is remembered less for who won than for what was done to people who were not soldiers.",
          "And it cost both causes any remaining pretence that the dragonseeds had been a good idea. Putting untrained, unloyal, common-born riders onto dragons to make up numbers had seemed clever when both sides were short of riders. Tumbleton is the bill for it: the seeds fought for themselves, betrayed whoever had them, and burned a town on the way to accomplishing nothing for anyone.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Tumbleton broke the green war effort at the moment it should have been winning it. An army that size, with that many dragons, driving on a King’s Landing already starving and turning against its own rulers, should have ended the war. Instead it ate itself, and the capital fell to the blacks by other means while the greens’ war-winning host lay wrecked in a burned market town.",
          "It is also the clearest single verdict the Dance passes on the whole logic of the dragon war. Both sides had spent two years believing that the answer to the enemy’s dragon was your own dragon, and that more dragons meant more victory. Tumbleton is the reductio: four dragons in one place, on the winning side, produced not a greater victory but a collapse, because the power the dragons gave their riders was greater than the loyalty anyone could command from them.",
          "After Tumbleton the war has very few dragons left and no armies worth the name, and it grinds toward its end on hunger, murder and the exhaustion of everyone still standing.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ser Ormund Hightower, who had commanded the great green host all the way up from Oldtown and won a string of battles with it, died at Tumbleton. The most successful green commander of the war on land did not survive his own greatest victory, which is as fair a summary of the Dance as any single fact provides.",
      "Prince Daeron Targaryen, Daeron the Daring, the boy who had won the Honeywine, is one of the war’s cruellest loose ends. The sources cannot agree on how or exactly when he died in the ruin of the green host at Tumbleton; some have him killed in the second battle, some in the disorder afterward, and his young blue dragon Tessarion did not long outlive him. A boy knighted for heroism at thirteen was dead not long after, in a burned town, with the army he had helped bring there destroying itself around him.",
      "Hugh Hammer and Ulf White, the two dragonseeds whose treachery won the first battle, did not survive the aftermath. Hammer, who had begun openly styling himself a king, was murdered by green men who had had enough of him; White met a similarly ugly end amid the wreck of the army he had betrayed his way into. Neither man got the crown he had briefly imagined, and their dragons — Vermithor the Bronze Fury and Silverwing — passed out of the war, one killed and one flown wild.",
      "Tumbleton the town was rebuilt, in time, as such places are. What was done there was not forgotten by the smallfolk of the Reach, and the two battles fought over it are among the reasons the Dance is remembered in that country as a calamity rather than a dynastic dispute.",
    ] },

  { id: "storming-dragonpit", name: "The Storming of the Dragonpit", sub: "130 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "The smallfolk of King's Landing vs. the dragons on the Hill of Rhaenys"], ["Result", "Four dragons dead; the city turns on its own"],
           ["Commanders", "No commander — a mob, and a prophet who lit it"], ["Strength", "Thousands of unarmed townsfolk against chained dragons in a stone dome"],
           ["Ground", "The Dragonpit, the great domed stable atop the Hill of Rhaenys"],
           ["Dragons", "Four killed in a single night by people with no weapons worth the name"]],
    blurb: "The night the smallfolk of King's Landing decided they had had enough of dragons and went up the hill to kill them.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "By the end of 130 AC King’s Landing had changed hands, changed rulers, and changed its mind about everything. Rhaenyra had taken the city, and taking it had not made her loved; she had raised taxes, executed people, lost the popular sympathy she arrived with, and presided over a capital that was hungry, frightened, and increasingly convinced that the dragons were the cause of all of it rather than the cure. The war had brought fire to the whole realm, and the realm had noticed that the thing all the fire came out of was stabled on a hill inside their own walls.",
          "The immediate spark was fear and rumour. A dragon called Sheepstealer, or the sight of a dragon in the sky, or simply the accumulated dread of a city that had watched the war come home — the accounts differ on the exact trigger, and it hardly matters, because the fuel had been piling up for two years. Into that walked the preachers, and one in particular, a wild-eyed prophet the histories call the Shepherd, who stood in the streets of Flea Bottom and told the crowds the plain thing that everyone was already feeling: that the dragons were demons, that they were the source of every horror the war had brought, and that the gods wanted them dead.",
      "It is one of the very few moments in the whole history of Westeros where the smallfolk — the people the great houses fight their wars on top of and otherwise never consult — act, in a mass, of their own accord, and change the course of the realm. They had no lord, no plan, and nothing to fight with. What they had was numbers and the specific, unbearable knowledge of what the dragons had done to them, and one night they went up the Hill of Rhaenys to end it.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Dragonpit was the great domed structure the Targaryens had built on the Hill of Rhaenys to house their dragons within the city — an enormous stone dome, its floor sunk below ground, with iron doors and a single great entrance. It had been built as a stable and it functioned, that night, as a trap. Several dragons were kept there, chained, in the dark, in a confined space they could not fly out of freely and could not manoeuvre inside.",
          "That confinement is the whole reason the impossible thing that happened could happen. A dragon in the open sky is very close to unkillable by a mob; a dragon chained in a stone room with a low roof, unable to get airborne, unable to use its speed or its height, surrounded on all sides at close range, is a large dangerous animal in a pit with thousands of people — which is a different and much worse proposition for the dragon.",
          "The attackers were the population of a great city: thousands of them, tens of thousands by some tellings, unarmed or armed with whatever came to hand — spears, knives, tools, stones, torches. They had no leaders worth the name and no tactics beyond mass and fury. The defenders were a handful of guards on the doors, who were simply drowned, and the dragons themselves, which did what dragons do and were killed for it anyway.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The mob broke down the doors of the Dragonpit and poured in. What followed inside that dome is one of the most horrifying scenes in the entire history of the realm and there is no way to make it anything less. The dragons burned attackers by the hundred — a chained dragon in a stone room is still a dragon, and the fire in that enclosed space killed people in numbers that beggar counting. But the mob did not stop, because a mob of that size with that much accumulated rage does not stop for casualties; the people at the front were pushed on by the people behind, and there were always more people behind.",
          "The dragons were killed the way you kill anything you cannot outfight one-on-one: by sheer weight of numbers, at close range, absorbing appalling losses to do it. Spears and knives found eyes and wings and the soft places; chains meant to hold the dragons also held them still to be struck at; the confined space that should have protected them from the sky instead denied them any escape from the floor. One by one, over the course of a night that the survivors never spoke of calmly, the dragons in the pit were brought down and butchered.",
          "The cost to the attackers was enormous — far more people died in the Dragonpit that night than dragons, by an overwhelming margin, most of them burned alive. But the exchange, from the mob’s point of view, was one they were willing to make, and it worked. By morning the dragons in the pit were dead, the great dome was a charnel house, and the population of King’s Landing had done with their bare hands and their bodies what two years of armies and dragonriders had not managed: they had killed dragons, several of them, in a single night.",
          "The mob did not stop at the pit. The city rose more generally that night and for days after; the Shepherd’s followers and the general disorder turned into a broader collapse of authority in the capital. What became of the queen and her remaining household in the days that followed belongs to the fold below, but the storming of the pit is the hinge: it is the moment the smallfolk stopped being the ground the war was fought on and became, briefly and terribly, participants in it.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the realm four dragons in one night — a catastrophic proportion of the dwindling number left alive anywhere in the world, killed not by rival dragons or by scorpions on castle walls but by a mob of unarmed townsfolk. Set beside the fact that killing a single dragon at Rook’s Rest had required two other dragons and a king’s whole scheme, the Dragonpit is the most efficient dragon-killing in the history of Westeros, and it was done by people with kitchen knives.",
          "It cost the smallfolk of King’s Landing a number of dead that no source seriously tries to total, and cost the city itself the terror of a night when it turned on its own rulers and its own protectors at once. The Dragonpit was never used again; the great dome stood as a burned ruin on its hill for the rest of the history of the Targaryens, a monument on the skyline to the night the city killed the dragons.",
          "Most of all it cost the Targaryen dynasty the thing the dynasty was built on. The dragons had been the argument for everything — for the Conquest, for the throne, for a century and a half of obedience — and after the Dance there were almost none left, and the last few were small, sickly, and dying. The storming of the pit is one of the deaths in that story, and symbolically it is the largest, because it was inflicted by the people the dragons were supposed to overawe.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It effectively ended dragons as a force in the world. Between the aerial duels of the war and this one night of butchery, the population of dragons collapsed from an armament that ruled a continent to a handful of dying animals, and none of the survivors bred a healthy line. The empty sky that defines the realm for the next century and a half — the reason the later Targaryens rule by marriage and council rather than by fire, the reason a rebellion three hundred years later can even be contemplated — begins in no small part in the Dragonpit.",
          "It also stands, uncomfortably, as the one time the smallfolk decided the course of the realm. The lesson the great houses took from it was not comfortable and was mostly suppressed: that the people they ruled were a force, that a hungry and frightened city could tear down the mightiest weapon in the world with its bare hands if it chose to, and that the thing keeping them from doing so more often was habit rather than any real inability. The Shepherd’s crowd is a ghost that haunts the back of every subsequent ruler’s mind, whether they admit it or not.",
          "For the Dance specifically, the storming of the pit is close to the end. A war fought over which Targaryen should command the dragons ended with a mob killing the dragons on its own initiative, which is either the bleakest joke in the histories or their sharpest piece of commentary, depending on the temper of the person telling it.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "The dragons killed in the pit that night were mostly the smaller and younger animals of the black cause, including Rhaenyra’s own Syrax, who is generally counted among the dead of the storming or its immediate aftermath. Their deaths, added to those already lost at Rook’s Rest, the Gullet, the Gods Eye and Tumbleton, brought the dragon population to the edge of extinction in the space of a single year.",
      "The Shepherd, the prophet who lit the mob, did not long outlast his moment. When order was reimposed on the city he and a great many of his followers were rounded up and executed — the standard fate of anyone who briefly reminds the great houses that they are outnumbered. He had, however, been proved right about the one thing he cared about: the dragons died.",
      "Rhaenyra herself did not survive the collapse of her hold on the capital that this night began. Driven from a city that had turned on her, her cause coming apart, she came at last to a very bad end that has its own dark fame; it is told elsewhere, and it was not quick. The war she and her half-brother had fought over the dragons outlived nearly all the dragons it was fought over.",
      "The Dragonpit was never rebuilt or reused. Its blackened dome stood over King’s Landing for the rest of the dynasty’s history, and long after anyone living remembered the night, it remained the plainest possible statement of how the age of dragons ended: not with a battle, but with a crowd.",
    ] },

  { id: "gullet", name: "The Battle of the Gullet", sub: "130 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "The Triarchy's fleet vs. the Velaryon blockade"], ["Result", "Both fleets ruined"],
           ["Commanders", "Sharako Lohar of Lys for the Triarchy · the Velaryon captains and the dragonriders of Dragonstone"],
           ["Strength", "Ninety Triarchy warships and a great many hired hulls against the blacks’ fleet"],
           ["Ground", "The Gullet — the narrow water between Dragonstone, Driftmark and the mainland"],
           ["Dragons", "Four came to the fight; the accounts differ on which arrived when"]],
    blurb: "The bloodiest sea battle in the history of the narrow sea.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Everything the blacks had, they had because of the sea. Corlys Velaryon’s fleet was the largest in Westeros, and when he declared for Rhaenyra it did not simply add ships to her side — it put a noose around the enemy capital. The Velaryons closed the Gullet, the narrow passage that all shipping bound for King’s Landing must use, and the city began to feel it in the price of bread within weeks. A blockade is a slow weapon, but it is a weapon that cannot be answered by courage, and the greens had no fleet worth the name to answer it with.",
      "So they bought one. The Three Daughters — Lys, Myr and Tyrosh, in the alliance called the Triarchy — had their own quarrel with the Velaryons going back to the wars in the Stepstones, and needed very little encouragement beyond gold and a promise of plunder. What sailed north was not a Westerosi royal fleet but a coalition armada under Lysene command: ninety warships proper, and behind them a much larger tail of hired and requisitioned hulls carrying men who had come for the sacking rather than the cause.",
      "The blacks knew a fleet was coming and did not know when. What made the timing catastrophic was an unrelated decision taken on Dragonstone: with the war going badly enough to think about the worst case, arrangements had been made to send Rhaenyra’s two youngest sons out of the country to safety across the narrow sea. Their ships were putting to sea, under escort, at the precise moment the Triarchy came up the passage. The two events had nothing to do with each other. That is what makes the Gullet the ugliest accident of the entire war.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Gullet is a funnel. Dragonstone and Driftmark sit on one side, the mainland on the other, and the water between narrows as it runs toward Blackwater Bay. It is the only sensible approach to King’s Landing from the east and it is a bad place to fight a fleet action, because there is nowhere to go. Ships engaged in the Gullet cannot manoeuvre out of contact, cannot easily disengage to leeward, and cannot avoid becoming a single tangled mass once the grappling starts. Both fleets knew this. Both went in anyway, one because it had no choice and one because it wanted the tangle.",
          "The Triarchy commander was Sharako Lohar of Lys, an admiral of real ability with a reputation for eccentricity that has probably been exaggerated by hostile chroniclers. His plan was not subtle and did not need to be: come up the passage in strength, overwhelm the blockade by numbers, and put the transports’ men ashore on Driftmark and Dragonstone to burn the Velaryon power at its root.",
          "The blacks’ strength on the water was the Velaryon fleet, superb ship for ship and heavily outnumbered on the day, scattered along the blockade line rather than concentrated. Their strength in the air was potentially decisive and, as it turned out, poorly coordinated. Prince Jacaerys Velaryon was at sea with the convoy on his dragon Vermax. The other riders were on Dragonstone — including several of the newly saddled dragonseeds, common-born Targaryen bastards who had claimed the riderless dragons of the island weeks earlier and had almost no experience of doing anything with them.",
          "The one clear fact about the numbers is that they were enormous by naval standards and that most of the men who died were in the water rather than on it. Beyond the ninety warships, the size of the Triarchy tail is not recorded with any confidence, and the totals that circulate afterwards vary by a factor that should make anybody cautious.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It began as a collision at the mouth of the passage. The Triarchy van came up and took the nearest blockading squadrons before they could concentrate, and the first phase was a straightforward matter of numbers: two and three ships on one, boarded, taken or burned. The blockade line was not so much broken as eaten from one end.",
          "The convoy carrying the princes was caught in the middle of this. It had put out on the assumption of an empty sea and found itself inside a fleet action, with warships on both sides of it and no room to run for shelter. One of the transports was taken by boarding and one got clear; which was which, and the sequence of it, is one of the details the accounts most disagree about, since almost everybody who could have described it was in the water within the hour.",
          "Jacaerys Velaryon brought Vermax into the fight immediately, alone, without waiting for the island. This was brave and, tactically, the worst possible decision. A dragon over a fleet has to come low to do anything, and low over ships means inside the effective range of every crossbow, every scorpion and every archer on every deck in the tangle — hundreds of them, shooting upward at a single target from all sides at once. That is a volume of shooting no dragon in the histories had faced before, and it is the specific tactical fact that decided the day.",
          "Vermax went down into the water. How is disputed. Some accounts have him brought down by shooting, others by a burning ship’s rigging fouling him as he passed low over the tangle, others that he simply went into the sea exhausted and drowned in the way that dragons do when they lose the air. Nobody watching had a clear view, because by then the middle of the Gullet was a single burning raft of grappled hulls with smoke lying across the whole of it.",
          "The dragons from Dragonstone came late. Three more got into the air and fell on the Triarchy fleet in earnest, and this phase was pure slaughter. Ships in a grapple cannot scatter; a fleet packed into a narrow passage and set alight burns as a unit. The fire spread from hull to hull along the tangle and the sea itself was covered in burning wreck and burning oil. The men who went over the side to escape the fire found that there was nowhere to swim to that was not also on fire.",
          "The Triarchy broke, but breaking in the Gullet was not an escape. The passage was choked with their own burning ships. Some got out to open water and some ran themselves ashore on Driftmark, where the landing parties they had brought for plunder were put into a fight they had not planned and were destroyed piecemeal against the islanders. The pursuit ran for the rest of the day and the burning went on into the night, visible from both shores.",
          "By dawn the invasion no longer existed as a fighting force. Neither did the blockade. The blacks had annihilated an enemy fleet in the narrow water and had spent, doing it, the instrument that had been strangling King’s Landing.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Gullet is remembered as the bloodiest sea fight in the history of the narrow sea and the figures usually cited run into many thousands drowned or burned. They are not reliable. Sea battles produce the least trustworthy casualty counts of any kind of engagement, because the dead are not on a field to be counted, and the Gullet’s dead were spread from Driftmark to the mainland shore over the following weeks. What can be said honestly is that both fleets were destroyed as fighting formations in a single day, and that the great majority of the loss was drowning and fire rather than blade.",
          "Driftmark was burned along much of its coast by the parties that got ashore, and the Velaryon towns took harm they were decades recovering from. The Velaryon fleet, the largest in Westeros and the accumulated work of generations, was reduced to a fraction of itself.",
          "The Triarchy was broken as a naval power by the same afternoon. The alliance of the Three Daughters had been the dominant force in the eastern narrow sea; it did not survive the losses or the recriminations that followed, and the Stepstones were left to whoever wanted them next, which is a story of its own.",
          "House Velaryon was ruined in ships and remained, for the moment, indispensable — the greens still had no fleet, and no gold left to hire another one. The blockade, however, was over, and King’s Landing ate again.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Strategically the Gullet was a black victory that functioned as a defeat. The invasion was annihilated and the greens’ one attempt to buy a navy came to nothing — but the blacks had won the war at sea by expending the thing that made winning at sea worth anything. The stranglehold on the capital was gone and could not be reimposed, and from that point the blacks’ advantage was entirely in the air, which is a much less patient and much less controllable instrument than a fleet.",
          "It also permanently changed how anybody sensible thought about dragons over ships. Before the Gullet, a dragon was assumed to be unanswerable by men who could not reach it. Afterwards it was understood that massed shooting from a packed fleet is one of the few things in the world that can reach one, and that a rider who goes low over a tangle is taking a risk of a different order from a rider over a battlefield.",
          "And it took the war past the point where either side could imagine a negotiated end. Both councils had now lost enough that the loss itself had become the argument for continuing.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Prince Jacaerys Velaryon, Rhaenyra’s eldest son and her heir, died in the Gullet with Vermax. He was in his middle teens. Accounts differ on whether he went into the water with his dragon or leapt onto an enemy deck as Vermax fell and was shot down there; the second version is the one the singers preferred and it may well be true. His body was not recovered. His mother was never told a version of it that she believed.",
      "The two younger princes had been sent out for safety and were separated by the battle. One ship carried Aegon the Younger clear and turned back for Dragonstone. The other, carrying Prince Viserys, was taken by boarding; the boy was found among the survivors, recognised as worth a ransom rather than a throat, and vanished into the Free Cities for years. He was thought dead by nearly everyone for the remainder of the war.",
      "Sharako Lohar survived the destruction of his own fleet, made his way to Westeros, and ended up in the greens’ service on land, where the aftermath of the Gullet followed him. His end came in a quarrel among his own supposed allies rather than at any enemy’s hand.",
    ] },

  { id: "gods-eye", name: "The Battle Above the Gods Eye", sub: "130 AC — the Dance",
    meta: [["Who", "Daemon & Caraxes vs. Aemond & Vhagar"], ["Result", "All four dead"],
           ["Combatants", "Two riders, two dragons, no army on either side"],
           ["Ground", "The sky over the Gods Eye, at sunset, with Harrenhal empty below"],
           ["Dragons", "Caraxes the Blood Wyrm · Vhagar, last of the Conquest"],
           ["Witnesses", "Disputed — the histories name almost none, and the singers name too many"]],
    blurb: "Two men and two dragons over a lake, by appointment.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "By the second year of the Dance both sides had discovered that dragons are a poor instrument of policy. They win engagements and they do not win wars, and every time one was committed the realm below got worse. King’s Landing had turned on its own rulers, the countryside was being burned by both sides, and the councils on Dragonstone and in the Red Keep were each running short of the two things that actually decide wars: food and men willing to fight for them.",
      "The riverlands were where it was being decided on the ground, and Harrenhal was the key to the riverlands — a ruin of a castle, far too large to garrison properly, but sitting on the crossing of every road that matters in the middle of the continent. It had changed hands more than once already. Prince Daemon Targaryen held it at this point with almost nothing: his troops had been drawn off to the fighting elsewhere, and he was sitting in the largest castle in Westeros with a skeleton household and a dragon.",
      "Aemond Targaryen had spent months burning the riverlands from the air on the theory that a country that has been sufficiently punished stops supporting the enemy. It does not, and he was by now the most hated man in the middle of the realm. The two of them had been circling each other for the whole war. Daemon was the older man by decades, the most experienced fighter in the family, and the one person alive who had beaten harder opponents than Aemond had ever met; Aemond had the largest dragon in the world and knew it settled most arguments by itself.",
      "The challenge was Daemon’s. He arranged, deliberately, to be found alone at Harrenhal, and let it be known where he was — an invitation that only worked because he knew exactly who he was inviting. What he was proposing was not a battle. It was a duel between the two most dangerous men on the continent, with the understanding on both sides that the survivor’s faction would probably win the war by default.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "There were no hosts. This is the only engagement in the histories of Westeros of any consequence at all that involved four combatants and no army, and it is the reason the accounts of it are so unreliable: there was almost nobody there to see.",
          "The ground was the Gods Eye, the great lake at the centre of the realm, with Harrenhal’s burned towers on its shore and the Isle of Faces sitting out in the middle of the water where the last of the green men are said to keep the old weirwoods. Whatever one makes of that, it means the fight was staged above the single most sacred and least visited place in Westeros, and no chronicler has ever been able to resist saying so.",
          "The hour was sunset, which matters tactically as well as poetically. The two riders came at each other over open water with the light low and behind one of them, at a height the accounts put at something above the clouds, and there was nothing beneath them but lake for miles.",
          "Caraxes, called the Blood Wyrm, was a long red dragon of vicious temper and considerable age, ridden hard for most of a lifetime by a man who had used him in real wars in the Stepstones. Vhagar was a century and a half old, the last living dragon that had flown in the Conquest, and simply larger than anything else in the world. On any straightforward reckoning Vhagar wins that matchup without effort, and Daemon knew it perfectly well. He was not planning a straightforward reckoning.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was conventional and brief. Both riders climbed for height, because height is the whole of dragon combat, and they made passes at each other above the lake with fire. Neither could hurt the other decisively this way. Dragonscale turns dragonfire tolerably well, and two experienced riders trading passes will do that for a long time without a result. This phase seems to have gone on for a while and did nothing.",
          "The decisive thing was that Daemon chose to close. A smaller, faster animal against a much larger one has one advantage — it can dictate the geometry — and exactly one way to use it, which is to force a grapple where the smaller animal is on the outside and where the fight stops being about size. Caraxes went in at Vhagar, into the teeth of the disadvantage, and got his jaws and claws into her at contact range. From that moment neither dragon could disengage. They were locked, and they were falling.",
          "What happened during the fall is the part that everybody knows and nobody witnessed. The version the histories settled on, and the one every singer in the realm has repeated since, is that Daemon Targaryen unbuckled himself from his saddle in the air, at whatever height that was, and jumped from Caraxes onto Vhagar’s back — landing behind his nephew as the two locked animals went down turning, and driving Dark Sister, the ancestral blade he had carried since he was a boy, down into the one eye Aemond had left.",
          "It should be said plainly that this is an extraordinary claim, that it comes to us through a small number of accounts written after the fact, and that the accounts do not agree on who saw it. One tradition names a single witness on the shore. Another says the fisherfolk of the lake villages watched the whole of it from their boats and it is from them the story comes. A third simply reports the result and declines to describe the middle, which is the most honest thing any of them do. What is certain is the state of the wreckage afterwards, and the wreckage is consistent with the story, which is not the same as proving it.",
          "The two dragons came down into the Gods Eye together, still locked. The lake is deep and it swallowed them. There was no pursuit, no rout and no rally, because there was nobody present to do any of those things — the largest single blow struck in the entire war took place in front of an audience of perhaps a dozen frightened people in fishing boats.",
          "In the days afterwards the lake gave up what it had taken, in pieces and slowly, and the remains were found and identified. One of the dragons had crawled out of the shallows onto the shore before it stopped. The other stayed in the water and was located much later by the fishermen who make their living over that ground.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "In men, nothing. In everything else, more than any other single engagement of the Dance. Two of the five or six most consequential people in the realm and two of the largest dragons in the world were removed from the war in the space of a few minutes, and neither side gained a foot of ground by it.",
          "Vhagar’s loss is the one that changed the shape of the world. She was the last of the Conquest dragons, the last animal alive that had been at the Field of Fire, and with her went the largest concentration of destructive power any single person had ever held. Nothing of her size has existed since, and on the current evidence nothing of her size is coming back.",
          "The greens lost the man who had actually been running their war and the weapon that made their war winnable, in one stroke, at a moment when their king was still an invalid. The blacks lost the most capable soldier they possessed and the one man whose judgement Rhaenyra had ever really deferred to, at the exact point in the campaign where she needed him most.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It ended the aerial war. There were dragons left after the Gods Eye — a handful, mostly young, mostly ridden by people with no business on them — but the era in which a great dragon could decide a campaign by arriving was finished on that lake. Everything that follows in the Dance is fought by men on the ground, ugly and slow, and the outcome is decided by hunger and by cities changing their minds rather than by fire.",
          "It also broke the Targaryens’ monopoly on being unanswerable. The house had held Westeros for a century and a half on a demonstrated fact, and within a few years of the Dance that fact was no longer demonstrable. Everything about how the realm is governed afterwards — the councils, the marriages, the careful cultivation of lords who could not previously have mattered — proceeds from the sky over the Gods Eye being empty.",
          "And it left behind an unresolved question about what exactly the lake swallowed, which the maesters have been arguing over for a century and a half without getting anywhere. The Gods Eye is very deep, and it kept most of its evidence.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Prince Aemond Targaryen died over the lake. When Vhagar’s remains were eventually recovered from the shallows, a body was found still strapped into the great saddle, and a sword was found where the accounts said it would be. That much is physical and about as close to proof as this battle offers.",
      "Prince Daemon Targaryen’s body was never found. Caraxes reached the shore and expired there with no rider aboard. The lake was searched and gave up nothing, and this is the single most argued-over absence in the histories of the realm. The official position is that he drowned in armour in deep water, which is entirely plausible. The unofficial positions are numerous: that he survived the fall and went to ground; that he had already arranged his disappearance and the duel was the cover for it; that he lived out his years in the Vale or on the Red Mountains with the dragonseed woman Nettles, whom he had been protecting against his own side’s orders in the weeks before Harrenhal. There is no evidence for any of these and they have never stopped being repeated. Several of them may come from the same interested party, since the only person who can be placed at Harrenhal with him in those last weeks — Alys Rivers — had every reason to shape the story that came out of it.",
      "Rhaenyra was told, and never accepted the version she was told. Aegon II received the news of his brother while still recovering from Rook’s Rest, and the war he inherited from that point was one neither of them could win in the air.",
    ] },

  { id: "ashford", name: "The Battle of Ashford", sub: "282 AC — Robert's Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "Robert Baratheon vs. the Reach, advance guard"], ["Result", "Robert’s only defeat of the war"],
           ["Commanders", "Robert Baratheon · Lord Randyll Tarly, ahead of Lord Mace Tyrell"], ["Strength", "A stormlander host against the vanguard of a much larger Reach army"],
           ["Ground", "Near the town of Ashford in the Reach, close to the Cockleswent"]],
    blurb: "The one battle Robert Baratheon ever lost, to the one commander in the Reach worth the name.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Robert Baratheon began the war in the worst strategic position of any of the rebel leaders and won it anyway, and Ashford is the low point from which he did it. When the rebellion broke, the stormlands rose for their lord, but the stormlands were nearly surrounded by loyalist country: the Reach to the west and south, the crownlands and the royal army to the north, and Storm’s End itself besieged behind him. Robert’s problem was that his enemies could concentrate against him faster than his allies in the north and the Vale could reach him.",
      "The largest loyalist army in the field was the Reach’s, mustered by Mace Tyrell of Highgarden, and it was moving to crush the stormland rising before the rebel factions could combine. Robert’s instinct, then and always, was to fight rather than wait, and there is a real argument that this instinct saved the rebellion more often than it endangered it — but at Ashford it very nearly ended it. He moved to meet the Reach army and struck at what he could reach of it.",
      "What he could reach was its vanguard, and its vanguard was commanded by Randyll Tarly of Horn Hill, who was and remained the best soldier the Reach produced in his generation — a hard, humourless, professionally excellent commander in the service of a lord who was neither. That mismatch, a first-rate battle captain leading the advance of a second-rate general, is the whole shape of the day.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The battle was fought near the town of Ashford in the Reach, on the approaches to it, in the broad fertile country that makes the Reach worth fighting over. The ground did not particularly favour anyone; the decisive factor was that the two forces that actually collided were badly mismatched in size, because of the order in which the Reach army arrived.",
          "Randyll Tarly commanded the Tyrell vanguard, out ahead of the main body under Mace Tyrell. A vanguard is a fraction of an army, but Tarly’s was a well-handled fraction and Robert did not know, or did not correctly weigh, how close the rest of the Reach host was behind it.",
          "Robert had the strength of the stormlands, or as much of it as had not been shut up in Storm’s End — a good army led by a man his soldiers would follow anywhere, which is a real military asset and also the thing that got them to Ashford. The trap, such as it was, was not a clever piece of ground; it was simply that Robert engaged the leading element of a much larger army with the rest of that army arriving faster than he had planned for.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The details of the fighting at Ashford are among the thinner in the war’s record, because it was a loyalist victory in a war the winners wrote up, and a defeat is always described more briefly than a triumph. What is clear is the shape. Robert attacked Tarly’s vanguard, and Tarly — outnumbered at the point of first contact, or roughly even — held, and then the weight of the Reach army came up and the balance turned hard against the stormlanders.",
          "Randyll Tarly’s reputation as a battle commander rests substantially on this day. He fought the leading division of the Reach host in a way that broke Robert’s attack rather than merely surviving it, and he did it before Mace Tyrell arrived to take nominal command of the victory — a pattern that repeats through Tarly’s whole career, in which he wins the battles and his liege lord receives them.",
          "Robert was beaten. He extricated what he could of his army and got away — the crucial thing about Ashford is that it was a defeat and not a destruction, and the difference is Robert’s own. A beaten commander who keeps his host together and withdraws in order has lost a battle; a beaten commander whose host dissolves has lost a war. Robert lost the battle, kept the army, and lived to reach Stoney Sept and then the Trident.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "In the immediate sense it cost Robert a battle and the initiative in the south, and it forced him into the fighting withdrawal that led, some weeks later, to his being run to ground at Stoney Sept, where the rebellion nearly ended in a single house-to-house search. Ashford is the first link in that chain.",
          "It cost the stormlanders casualties they could ill afford, given how outnumbered the whole rebel cause was at that stage of the war. But it did not cost Robert his army, which is why it is remembered as a setback rather than a catastrophe.",
          "For Randyll Tarly it was the making of a reputation that lasted the rest of his life. The line that followed him ever after — that he was the only man ever to defeat Robert Baratheon in the field — is very nearly true and was worth more to him than most men’s victories, because it was true of the one opponent everyone had heard of.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Strategically Ashford confirmed that Robert could not win the war in the south on his own, and pushed the whole rebel effort toward the eventual concentration of forces in the riverlands that produced the Trident. It is one of the reasons the decisive battle of the war was fought where it was fought.",
          "It also demonstrated, early, the pattern that decided the whole rebellion: that the loyalist cause was a collection of large armies indifferently led, and the rebel cause a smaller force with better commanders and a reason to fight. The Reach could beat Robert’s vanguard-sized fraction with Tarly in charge; it could not translate that into ending him, because the man at the top of the Reach army was Mace Tyrell.",
          "And it is a useful footnote to Robert himself. The man is remembered as an unstoppable warrior, and on a battlefield he very nearly was — but Ashford is the reminder that being the best fighter in a war is not the same as being the best general in it, and that Robert won his rebellion partly on his own arm and partly because his enemies handed it to him.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Robert Baratheon survived Ashford, survived Stoney Sept a few weeks later by the narrowest margin of the war, and went on to win at the Trident and take the throne. Ashford remained the only battle he ever lost, a distinction he wore lightly because it was so thoroughly outweighed by the one he won at the ford.",
      "Randyll Tarly served the winning side in the end — he bent the knee with the rest of the Reach after the Trident and the Tyrell surrender at Storm’s End — and spent the peace as the hardest and most respected lord in his region. His later road, in the next war, ran through choices that his pride and his contempt for weakness made for him, and it ended in fire; that belongs to another entry.",
      "Mace Tyrell took the public credit for Ashford as he took credit for most of what Tarly did, and it became part of the Highgarden story of the war. The gap between the lord who was named and the knight who did the work is one of the quieter running jokes of the histories, and everyone in the Reach knew it.",
    ] },

  { id: "siege-of-storms-end", name: "The Siege of Storm's End", sub: "282–283 AC — Robert's Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "House Tyrell besieging vs. Stannis Baratheon holding"], ["Result", "The castle never fell; the besiegers surrendered it"],
           ["Commanders", "Lord Mace Tyrell and Lord Paxter Redwyne · Stannis Baratheon, holding for his brother"],
           ["Strength", "The whole strength of the Reach outside the walls; a starving garrison within"],
           ["Ground", "Storm’s End, on Shipbreaker Bay — the castle built not to fall"]],
    blurb: "A year-long siege that starved a garrison to eating rats, broken by a smuggler in a small boat.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "When Robert Baratheon rode off to raise the stormlands and fight his war, he left his younger brother Stannis to hold Storm’s End, the ancestral seat, with a small garrison and the castle’s formidable walls. It was the sort of task that suits Stannis exactly: unglamorous, thankless, defensive, and requiring a degree of stubbornness that in him passed for a personality. It also came within an ace of starving everyone inside.",
      "Storm’s End is the strongest castle in the stormlands and arguably in the realm, built — the legend runs — by Durran Godsgrief with the help of Brandon the Builder, with a single enormous curtain wall so thick and so high that no army had ever taken it. It cannot be stormed. It can only be starved, and starving a castle requires that you also control the sea, because a castle on the coast can be supplied by water indefinitely. Storm’s End sits on Shipbreaker Bay, which is exactly what it sounds like.",
      "The Reach army that came to besiege it, under Mace Tyrell, could not take the walls and knew it. What it could do was sit down around the landward side and wait, while the Redwyne fleet under Paxter Redwyne closed the sea. Between them they sealed the castle off, and then the only question was arithmetic: whether the food inside would last longer than the war outside.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Storm’s End is a single drum tower and one gigantic curtain wall, with no weak angle and no second circuit to fight through — the whole defensive theory of the place is one perfect wall that cannot be got over, under, or through. Against such a castle a besieger has no assault worth attempting; the Tyrells did not seriously try. The siege was a blockade, not an attack, and blockades are won by whoever can endure the longest.",
          "The garrison was small — Stannis had not been left a large force, because the fighting men had gone with Robert — and it was holding a very large castle, which meant a lot of wall to watch and, crucially, no more mouths than necessary but also no reserve of supply. Storm’s End had not been provisioned for a year-long siege because nobody had expected one.",
          "Outside sat the largest regional army in the realm, comfortable, fed, and in no hurry. The Redwyne fleet held the bay and cut the one lifeline a coastal castle relies on. The Tyrell strategy required no cleverness at all, only patience, and Mace Tyrell had nothing but time. The garrison had the walls and an emptying larder, and the walls, for once, were the lesser asset.",
        ] },
      { h: "The starving",
        paras: [
          "The siege ran on for the better part of a year, and it is remembered not for any fighting — there was almost none — but for hunger. The food ran out slowly and then completely. The garrison ate its way down through everything a castle contains: the stores first, then the horses, then the dogs and cats, then, by the end, rats and boiled leather and worse. People died of it. The accounts that survive, mostly from those who lived through it, describe the ordinary horrors of a starving town, and Stannis Baratheon’s refusal to yield through all of it is the foundation of everything anyone ever believed about the man afterward, for good and for ill.",
          "This is the part of Stannis that the rest of his life keeps circling back to. He held Storm’s End past the point of reason, on the principle that his brother had told him to hold it and that yielding was a thing he would not do, while people under his command starved to death around him. It is simultaneously the most admirable and the most frightening thing about him: a sense of duty so absolute that it does not bend for suffering, including the suffering of the people the duty is supposed to protect. Every later reader of Stannis is really reading Storm’s End.",
          "What kept the garrison alive long enough for the war to be won elsewhere was not a sortie or a relief army. It was one man in a small boat. A smuggler ran the Redwyne blockade in the dark, on a moonless night, with his hull loaded to the waterline with onions and salt fish, and reached the castle with enough food to keep the defenders breathing a while longer. He did it, by most tellings, more than once. It was not a heroic quantity of food — onions and fish, not a relief of the siege — but it was the difference between a garrison that starved to death and one that lasted until the war was decided.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it ended",
        paras: [
          "The siege was not broken by battle. It was ended by the war moving elsewhere. While Storm’s End starved, Robert won at the Trident and the capital fell, and the whole loyalist cause collapsed at the top. Mace Tyrell was sitting outside a castle he could not take, in the name of a king who no longer had a throne, with an army that had just discovered it was on the losing side of a war that was over.",
          "So the Tyrells did the only sensible thing left: they surrendered. The besiegers laid down their arms to the besieged — the whole strength of the Reach, undefeated in the field, gave itself up to a starving garrison that could not have driven a determined child off the walls at that point. It is one of the stranger endings of any siege in the histories: the winners of the blockade surrendering to its survivors because the war had been lost behind their backs.",
          "The Tyrells were pardoned and kept their lands and their heads, which was Robert’s policy toward the great houses generally and a sensible one. Storm’s End had held. It had never fallen, it had never been stormed, and it had been kept for the winning side by a man who would not yield and a smuggler who would not stop rowing.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Concretely, it kept the stormlands’ chief castle and its ruling house in the war on the winning side, and it tied down the Reach’s enormous army for the better part of a year outside a single wall — an army that, had it been free to march north and join the royal cause, might have changed the arithmetic at the Trident. In that sense Stannis’s stubborn hold on an apparently minor castle was one of the quiet strategic facts of the whole war.",
          "It also set the two relationships that define the rest of Stannis Baratheon’s life. He held the castle and Robert gave the credit, and the lordship of Storm’s End, to their youngest brother Renly — a slight Stannis never forgot and never forgave, and one that colours everything the two of them do to each other twenty years later. And he acquired a debt to a common smuggler that he chose, uncharacteristically, to honour, which is the beginning of the one genuine friendship of his life.",
          "More broadly, the siege is the origin of the reputation that makes Stannis matter at all. Without Storm’s End he is Robert’s dour younger brother; with it he is the man who does not break, which is exactly the quality that makes people follow him into hopeless causes and exactly the quality that makes those causes hopeless.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Stannis Baratheon survived the siege and was rewarded, by his own reckoning, with an insult: Robert gave Storm’s End and the lordship of the stormlands to their younger brother Renly, and gave Stannis the smaller, colder seat of Dragonstone and command of the royal fleet. Stannis held that this was ingratitude for the hardest year of his life, and he was not entirely wrong. The grievance ran under everything he did for the rest of his days.",
      "The smuggler was Davos, later called Seaworth. Stannis rewarded him for running the blockade by knighting him and granting him land and a place — and, in the same breath, took the ends of the fingers of his left hand for the years of smuggling that had come before, on the grounds that a good deed does not cancel old crimes and that a just man pays both accounts. Davos kept the bones of the fingertips in a pouch around his neck and considered the trade a fair one, which tells you as much about Davos as it does about Stannis. He became the one man Stannis fully trusted and the one man who would tell him the truth.",
      "Mace Tyrell went back to Highgarden pardoned and undamaged, having lost a war without losing a battle, and House Tyrell carried on exactly as before. The memory of having surrendered the strongest army in the realm to a starving garrison was not one the Reach dwelt on, and their enemies were too polite, or too prudent, to raise it often.",
    ] },

  { id: "trident", name: "The Battle of the Trident", sub: "283 AC — Robert's Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "Robert Baratheon vs. Rhaegar Targaryen"], ["Result", "Three centuries of Targaryen rule ended"],
           ["Commanders", "Robert Baratheon, Eddard Stark, Jon Arryn and Hoster Tully · Prince Rhaegar Targaryen, with Prince Lewyn Martell and Ser Barristan Selmy"],
           ["Strength", "Two very large hosts; no count either side gave is trustworthy"],
           ["Ground", "A ford on the Trident, since called the ruby ford"]],
    blurb: "A warhammer, a breastplate of rubies, and the end of a dynasty in a river ford.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The rebellion began as a private outrage and became a war because the king could not stop escalating it. Rhaegar Targaryen took Lyanna Stark; her brother and father went to King’s Landing for justice and were destroyed by Aerys II in a manner that horrified even his own court; Aerys then demanded the heads of two more young lords from their guardian, Jon Arryn, and Jon Arryn raised his banners instead. That is four of the seven kingdoms in rebellion inside a season, and it happened not because the realm was ideologically opposed to the Targaryens but because the king kept giving people no third option.",
      "For the first year the rebels were badly placed. Robert Baratheon was cut off in the south and won several sharp fights he should not have had to fight; the crown very nearly had him at Stoney Sept and did not, because a riverlands army arrived in time and because a town full of people was willing to hide him. That failure had a price: Aerys blamed his commander, dismissed him, and replaced a competent man with a series of appointments made for loyalty. After Stoney Sept the rebel factions — stormlands, north, Vale and riverlands — joined into one host, and the war acquired a shape.",
      "That left the loyalists needing a decisive field battle and needing it soon, because time was running against them. Prince Rhaegar had returned from wherever he had been in the south, taken command of the royal army himself, and marched north to fight it. He had reason to want the matter settled personally: the war was about him, whatever else it had become, and he had a father in King’s Landing whose behaviour was making the crown’s position worse every week that it continued.",
      "Both hosts converged on the crossings of the Trident, which is where any army moving between the north and the south of Westeros has to go. If the rebels won they were between the royal army and the capital with nothing in the way. If Rhaegar won, the rebellion’s entire leadership was on one field and could be destroyed in one afternoon.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The battle took its name from a ford on the Trident and was fought in and around the crossing itself — shallow, wide, with the water running past a stony bed, and a good deal of the fighting done ankle- and knee-deep. A contested river crossing is one of the few pieces of ground in warfare that genuinely rewards the defender, because men in water cannot hold formation, cannot use a shield wall properly and can be shot at while doing neither. Both armies understood this. Neither could avoid the ford, because the ford was the only thing worth having.",
          "The royal host was the larger of the two and was drawn from the Crownlands, the Reach and Dorne, with a contingent of Dornish spears under Prince Lewyn Martell — present partly out of loyalty and partly because the Martells’ princess and her children were in the Red Keep and everybody understood what that meant. Ser Barristan Selmy of the Kingsguard rode with them. Rhaegar commanded the centre and the household in person.",
          "The rebel host was a coalition of four regional armies that had been separate armies until a few weeks earlier: Robert’s stormlanders, Eddard Stark’s northmen, Jon Arryn’s knights of the Vale and Hoster Tully’s rivermen, the last of them fighting over their own ground. Coalitions of that kind usually handle badly, and this one held together because all four commanders had known each other for years and because Robert Baratheon in the field had a gift for making men want to be near him.",
          "Neither side’s numbers survive in any form worth quoting. Both hosts were among the largest raised since the Dance, and the loyalists are generally reckoned to have had the advantage in weight and in heavy horse. The forces that matter for the outcome, though, were two men in the middle of it, and everyone on that field knew before it started that the day would probably come down to whether they found each other.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It opened as a straightforward fight for the crossing and was murderous from the first hour. Men went into the water and were held there. Neither side could establish itself on the far bank in strength for a long time, and the fighting worked back and forth across the ford in a way that produced heavy loss without producing progress. The chroniclers pass over this phase quickly because it is not interesting, which is a shame, since it is where most of the dying happened.",
          "The wings held. This is the fact that made the rest of the day possible. On the loyalist right and centre the royal army fought well and the Dornish under Prince Lewyn held their ground longer than anyone had a right to expect from a contingent whose loyalty was, at best, purchased with hostages. On the rebel side the northmen and the Valemen kept their formations on ground that made formations nearly impossible. Because neither flank collapsed, neither commander could win by manoeuvre, and both of them were pushed toward the middle.",
          "Then Robert Baratheon and Rhaegar Targaryen found each other in the ford, and every account of the battle collapses to that one point, which is both a distortion and the truth. It is a distortion because a battle of that size is not decided by two men, and the truth because this one was.",
          "The two of them were not evenly matched in the way the songs suggest. Rhaegar was a very good knight, trained to the highest standard of the age, competent with a sword and generally reckoned the better technician. Robert was something else — enormously strong, entirely without fear, and fighting with an antique weapon that nobody sensible used any more because it was heavy, clumsy and only good for one thing. A warhammer does not need to find a gap in plate. It does not care about plate at all. It transfers the whole of a very large man’s strength through a small area of steel and lets the steel do the rest.",
          "They fought in the water, on foot and in armour, in a river with the rest of the battle going on around them. Rhaegar had come to the field wearing his house’s black armour with rubies worked into the breastplate in the shape of a three-headed dragon, which is a thing you do when you expect to be recognised at a distance and when you have some notion of yourself as the last word in an argument. Robert put the hammer into the middle of it. The breastplate failed, the rubies came out of their settings, and they went into the river and downstream. Men were still finding them in the shallows years afterwards, which is why nobody has called it anything but the ruby ford since.",
          "Whatever else the royal army had, it did not have a second plan. The centre broke where its commander had been standing, and the collapse ran outward along the line from there. Once a river-crossing fight breaks, it breaks completely, because the losing side has to retreat through the water it has just spent the day standing in.",
          "Robert had been hurt badly enough in the exchange that he could not lead the pursuit, and Eddard Stark took the host on. It was pursued hard, and the royal army did not reform. Ser Barristan Selmy was taken off the field with wounds that should have finished him and was carried, unconscious, into the rebel camp — where Robert, told who it was, gave orders to have him treated rather than hanged, which is one of the more revealing decisions of Robert’s life and one he made without appearing to think about it.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Trident was expensive on both sides in a way the histories tend to skate over because the outcome was so dramatic. It was a full-scale field battle between the two largest hosts in the realm, fought over a river, and settled at close quarters over most of a day. Tens of thousands were engaged and the loss was proportionate. No trustworthy count exists.",
          "The loyalist command was destroyed as a body — two of the Kingsguard among them. The Dornish contingent, which had been sent north essentially at knifepoint, was cut up badly enough that Dorne’s account of the war afterwards begins with a grievance and only gets worse from there. Ser Barristan Selmy was one of the few of that company to be carried off the field alive.",
          "The house ruined was the royal house, though it took another few weeks to become obvious. Every man who might have organised a defence of the capital was on that field, and after it there was no royal army left in the field anywhere between the Trident and King’s Landing.",
          "The men made were the rebel commanders, in ways that shaped the next twenty years: Robert became a king, Eddard Stark became the man who took the host south and had to live with what he found there, Jon Arryn became the Hand who actually ran the realm, and Hoster Tully’s house went from a border power to kin of the new regime.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It ended a dynasty that had held the Iron Throne since Aegon’s Conquest, and it did so at a stroke rather than by attrition. Three hundred years of Targaryen rule finished in a river in the space of an afternoon, and the men who did it had gone to war a year earlier over a kidnapping and a murder, with no programme of any kind for what came next. A great deal of what goes wrong in the following two decades comes from that: the rebellion won a battle and then had to invent a government.",
          "It also fixed the two facts the rest of the saga argues about. First, Robert’s claim to the throne rested on having won it, with a thin Targaryen descent produced afterwards as decoration, which meant the throne was now something that could be taken by whoever could take it — and every claimant in the War of the Five Kings knows that. Second, the question of what Rhaegar and Lyanna actually were to each other was never answered on that field, and the entire main story is downstream of the answer.",
          "The Trident is the hinge. Nearly every character in the saga is either a consequence of that day, a survivor of it, or engaged in trying to undo it.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Rhaegar Targaryen died in the ford. The accounts that claim to know say he spoke a woman’s name as he went down, and that the name was not his wife’s — a detail that meant nothing to the men who reported it at the time and everything to the people who worked out what it implied, many years later.",
      "Prince Lewyn Martell of the Kingsguard was killed on the field, and Dorne has never regarded its involvement in the war as voluntary. Ser Jonothor Darry fell with him. Ser Barristan Selmy, gravely wounded, was spared and pardoned by Robert, and served the new regime for seventeen years.",
      "Robert was wounded badly enough that Eddard Stark led the march on King’s Landing without him. What Ned found when he arrived ended their friendship for a time and never fully healed it, and it sent him south again alone, to a tower in the Dornish mountains, for reasons he explained to no one for the rest of his life.",
      "Rhaegar’s wife and children were still in the Red Keep. What happened to them is the subject of the next entry.",
    ] },

  { id: "sack-of-kings-landing", name: "The Sack of King's Landing", sub: "283 AC — Robert's Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "Tywin Lannister's host vs. an open gate"], ["Result", "The Targaryens finished"],
           ["Commanders", "Lord Tywin Lannister, Ser Gregor Clegane and Ser Amory Lorch · nominally King Aerys II"],
           ["Strength", "A full western host against a city with no field army left"],
           ["Ground", "King’s Landing, walled, crowded, and full of hidden wildfire"]],
    blurb: "Not a battle so much as a demonstration of what Tywin Lannister does when the door is opened for him.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Tywin Lannister spent almost the entirety of Robert’s Rebellion doing nothing. He had served Aerys II as Hand for twenty years, had been humiliated by him repeatedly and publicly, and had gone home to Casterly Rock with the west intact and unspent. When the banners went up he did not answer the crown’s summons and did not join the rebels either. He mustered, sat on his hands, and waited for somebody to win. It was cold, disloyal and completely correct, and it is the clearest single illustration of how the man thought.",
      "After the Trident the answer was obvious. The royal army was destroyed, the rebel host was marching south, and the capital had a mad king, a garrison, a Kingsguard and no means whatever of stopping what was coming. At that point Tywin marched — not to the rebels’ camp to join them, which would have made him one lord among four, but to the gates of King’s Landing, which he could reach first.",
      "Aerys had made himself impossible to defend even by people who wanted to. In the last months he had installed pyromancers in place of counsellors, elevated a guild alchemist to the office of Hand, and had wildfire caches placed beneath the sept, the guildhalls, the stables, the streets and the Red Keep itself — hundreds of jars of it, laid over months, on the understanding that if he could not hold the city nobody would have it. He had also convinced himself, against all recent evidence, that the great houses would ultimately come to heel.",
      "So when a western host appeared beneath the walls professing loyalty to the crown, the question put before the king was whether to open the gates. His small council was divided; one voice of long standing and considerable authority argued strongly that Lord Tywin had come as a friend and should be admitted. The king took that advice. There is a case that this was the single most consequential piece of counsel given in the entire century.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "King’s Landing in 283 was a walled city of half a million people, built up against itself, with narrow streets, no citadel worth the name apart from the Red Keep, and a population that had spent a year hearing about what was coming. Its defensibility on paper was decent — high walls, seven gates, the river at its back. Its actual defensibility was nil, because a city is only as strong as the field army that relieves it, and there was no longer a field army anywhere south of the Trident.",
          "The garrison was the City Watch, a body of men whose function is policing rather than war, and whatever household troops remained in the Red Keep. Several of the Kingsguard were dead on the Trident or dispatched elsewhere on the king’s errands. What was left in the keep was a handful of sworn shields, a household, a queen, a pregnant princess-in-law with two small children, and a king who had gone somewhere his own council could not follow.",
          "Outside was a full western host, unbloodied, well provisioned, under the most methodical commander in Westeros, with two subordinates chosen for the specific work he had in mind. Ser Gregor Clegane and Ser Amory Lorch were not distinguished soldiers. They were instruments, and Tywin had a long-standing practice of keeping instruments of that kind on his books precisely so that certain orders would never need to be written down.",
          "There was one other feature of the terrain that neither side counted: the wildfire. Somewhere under the streets the city was carrying enough of it to erase itself, placed by men who answered directly to the king and to nobody else.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It was not fought. That is the point of it. The gates opened, the host came in as friends, and the sack began within the hour — which means the order had been given before a single man passed the wall, and that everybody in that column knew what they were entering the city to do.",
          "The first phase was the standard grammar of a sack, done efficiently: the gates secured from the inside, the Watch overwhelmed or bought or simply irrelevant, the main routes taken, and then the men turned loose on the streets. King’s Landing had not been sacked in living memory and had no procedure for it. The killing and the burning and the rest of it ran through the poorer districts first and worst, as it always does, and continued through most of a day and a night.",
          "The second phase was happening simultaneously and was the actual operation. A body of picked men went straight for the Red Keep, and they were not there to loot it. Tywin’s purpose in reaching the city before the rebels was to arrive with a gift that could not be refused — proof, delivered in person, that the west had ended the Targaryen line rather than merely joined the winning side late. That required specific people, and it required them to be past saving before anybody with authority could object.",
          "Meanwhile, in the throne room, the king was giving the order he had been preparing for months. The wildfire caches were to be lit — the whole city, with the enemy inside it and everyone else too. Aerys had told himself a story in which he rose from the ashes as something other than a man. The order went to the pyromancer he had made his Hand, and it was never carried out, because one of the men standing in that room as the king’s sworn protector made a decision about what his oath was actually for, and went after the alchemist rather than the enemy at the gate. Half a million people are alive in the following decades because of it, and not one of them ever knew.",
          "The third phase was arrival and management. Eddard Stark came down the kingsroad with the rebel host and found the city already taken, the sack in progress or just concluding, and a Lannister in possession of the Red Keep. What was presented to Robert Baratheon shortly afterwards, when he came up, were bodies wrapped in crimson cloaks and laid at the foot of the throne as an act of homage — a gesture calculated to make refusal impossible, because refusing it would mean saying out loud that the new king found the deaths inconvenient.",
          "Robert accepted. That decision, made in a room full of exhausted men within hours of the end of a war, poisoned the next twenty years. Ned Stark did not accept it, said so, and the two of them quarrelled so badly that he rode out of the city and out of the friendship, south, on business of his own.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The city itself paid most of the bill and is the part least written about. Thousands of ordinary people were killed in a sack that served no military purpose whatever, since the city was not resisting. Whole districts were burned. Every account of the Sack of King’s Landing that dwells on the Red Keep is, in a small way, doing the thing Tywin wanted done, which is treating the deaths of three people as the story and the deaths of thousands as weather.",
          "The Targaryen presence in the capital was extinguished — the king, the royal household, and the heir’s wife and children. The dynasty’s only remaining members were a boy and an unborn child on Dragonstone, got out to sea ahead of the rebels by loyal servants in the last days.",
          "House Lannister was made by it, in the most literal sense. Tywin had spent the whole war conserving his strength and cashed the entire investment in one afternoon: a royal pardon, a place at the centre of the new regime, and within a year a marriage that put his daughter on the throne beside the new king. He bought the next twenty years of Lannister supremacy at the price of a reputation he had never much wanted anyway.",
          "The man ruined by it was one of the men who did the right thing. The knight who stopped the wildfire was seen only as the one who broke his oath, and he never explained himself to anyone with the standing to hear it. He carried the name he was given at seventeen for the rest of his career, and it is the story’s cleanest demonstration that a reputation is made by what is convenient to believe rather than by what happened.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It made Robert’s reign possible and it made it rotten from the foundation. The new king took the throne with the deaths of children on the account of one of his own great lords and chose not to pursue it, which meant the regime began with an unpunished crime that everybody knew about. Dorne never accepted it, never forgot it, and spent the following two decades in a condition of formal loyalty and actual estrangement.",
          "It also created, in a single day, most of the personal grudges that the main saga runs on. The Martells’ grievance, the Starks’ mistrust of the Lannisters, the position of the man who stopped the wildfire, the surviving Targaryens across the narrow sea raised on an account of this day — all of it starts here. A very large proportion of the deaths in the War of the Five Kings, sixteen years later, can be traced back through somebody’s memory of the sack.",
          "And it established the operating principle of the new order, which was that overwhelming force applied without warning to people who trust you is effective, cheap and survivable. Others were watching, and others took the lesson. Two decades later a bride’s family will apply it at a wedding, and the man who taught it will be pleased with them.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Aerys II Targaryen was killed in the throne room by Ser Jaime Lannister of his own Kingsguard, after Jaime had first killed the pyromancer carrying the order to fire the wildfire caches. Jaime was found sitting on the Iron Throne when the rebels arrived, which is the image everybody kept, and he was called Kingslayer for the rest of his life by people who never learned what the alternative had been.",
      "Princess Elia Martell of Dorne was raped and murdered in the Red Keep by Ser Gregor Clegane, who had already killed her infant son Aegon. Her daughter Rhaenys, a small girl, was found hiding and stabbed to death by Ser Amory Lorch. Neither man was ever charged. The bodies were wrapped in Lannister crimson and presented to Robert Baratheon, who let it pass. Doran and Oberyn Martell did not, and the debt they opened that day is called in, at length and at great cost, two decades later.",
      "Queen Rhaella and Prince Viserys had been sent to Dragonstone before the end. Rhaella died there in childbed during a storm, bearing a daughter. The two surviving children were taken across the narrow sea by a handful of loyalists, and the younger of them spent her childhood being told a version of this day.",
      "Grand Maester Pycelle, who had urged the king to open the gates, kept his chain and his seat on the small council for another sixteen years, and was very old and very useful to the same family for all of them.",
    ] },

  { id: "tower-of-joy", name: "The Tower of Joy", sub: "283 AC — Robert's Rebellion",
    meta: [["Who", "Eddard Stark’s party vs. three of the Kingsguard"], ["Result", "Ten men dead; two survivors; a secret kept"],
           ["Commanders", "Eddard Stark, with six companions · Ser Arthur Dayne, Ser Gerold Hightower and Ser Oswell Whent"],
           ["Strength", "Seven against three"], ["Ground", "A lonely tower in the Red Mountains of Dorne, called the tower of joy"]],
    blurb: "Seven men, three of the finest knights in the world, ten graves, and the one secret the whole story turns on.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The war was already won when the last fight of it was fought. The Trident had gone against the loyalists, King’s Landing had fallen, and the Targaryen cause had collapsed everywhere it mattered. There was nothing left to fight for — except that Eddard Stark still did not know where his sister was, and three of the seven Kingsguard were not at any of the places where the war had actually been decided.",
      "That second fact is the strange one, and it is the key to everything. When a dynasty falls, its sworn guard is supposed to be at its centre — protecting the king, or the queen, or the heir. But three of the Kingsguard, and not the least of them, were not at the Trident with Rhaegar, and were not in the capital, and were not with the queen and the surviving royal children who had been sent to safety at Dragonstone. They were in the Red Mountains of Dorne, at an out-of-the-way tower, guarding something that in the calculation of the finest knights in the realm mattered more than the last Targaryen king or the last Targaryen heir.",
      "Ned Stark had ridden south from the Trident looking for Lyanna, the sister whose taking by Rhaegar had lit the whole war. What he found, at the tower where the trail ended, were three men in the white cloaks of the Kingsguard, standing in the road, who had no intention of standing aside. He came with six companions, all of them northmen or riverlanders who had come the whole war with him. It was seven against three, and the three were the ones everybody there expected to win.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The men",
        paras: [
          "The three Kingsguard were not an ordinary garrison. Ser Gerold Hightower, the Lord Commander, called the White Bull, was the senior knight of the order and had held the white cloak since before the war. Ser Oswell Whent was a hard and capable man of a riverlands house. And Ser Arthur Dayne, the Sword of the Morning, who carried the pale greatsword Dawn forged from the heart of a fallen star, was by common and near-universal agreement the finest knight in the Seven Kingdoms — the man other great swordsmen measured themselves against and admitted they fell short of. Even Jaime Lannister, who is not generous about other men’s skill, regarded Arthur Dayne as the best he ever saw.",
          "Against them Ned Stark brought six: among them Howland Reed, the small crannogman lord of the Neck, and a handful of northern and riverland companions whose names are remembered because of who did not come back rather than for anything they did before. On paper it was a mismatch in the northmen’s favour by numbers and in the Kingsguard’s favour by everything else, and everyone present understood which of those mattered more.",
          "Why the three of them were there is the whole point of the encounter and it is answered only obliquely in the surviving accounts. They were guarding the tower. They had chosen to be there rather than anywhere else in a collapsing realm, and Kingsguard do not choose freely — they go where the king’s blood is. That is the shape of the secret, laid out in the open by the mere fact of who was standing guard and where.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "There is no reliable blow-by-blow account of the fight at the tower of joy, and there cannot be, because the story of it comes down through exactly two people and one of them never spoke of it and the other kept a secret. Most of what is known is known through Ned Stark’s memory, returned to years later in fragments, and through a dream — which is to say the most important small battle in the whole story is also the one the story is least willing to describe plainly.",
          "What is certain is the arithmetic of the outcome. Ten men fought and only two walked away from that tower. The three Kingsguard were among the eight who did not — the Sword of the Morning among them, which by itself tells you how hard the fight was, because Arthur Dayne did not fall to seven ordinary swords cheaply — and so were four of Ned’s six companions. When it was over, two men were left standing on the northern side: Eddard Stark and Howland Reed.",
          "That the crannogman came away and the greater warriors did not is itself a quiet piece of information the story plants and does not explain, and readers have spent a long time reading meaning into it. The show stages the fight as a set-piece with swords and a twist about how Ned survived it, and it is deliberately not the same as the version in Ned’s memory in the books; the honest thing to say is that the exact choreography is unknown and probably meant to be. The frequently repeated line — that had it been a fair fight, or a chivalrous one, the outcome would have gone the other way — comes from Ned himself, and it is as close to an admission of how the northmen won as the story ever gives.",
          "What the fight was for was inside the tower, and Ned climbed to it when the fighting was done. What he found there is the hinge of the entire saga, and it belongs, more than anything else in this whole collection, in the fold below — because it is the one secret the books have spent their entire length not saying out loud, and putting it in the open text here would do to a reader exactly what the story has worked hardest to avoid.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the lives of three of the seven Kingsguard, including the man widely held to be the best knight who ever lived, and four of Ned Stark’s companions. Ten men fought at a nameless tower in the mountains for a war that was already over, and eight of them are in the ground there. Ned took his companions’ bones home and raised a cairn from the stones of the tower, which he then pulled down; the site is a grave and a ruin and nothing else.",
          "It cost Ned Stark something less countable, which the rest of his life is spent paying. He came away from that tower carrying a promise and a secret, and both of them shaped every important decision he made for the following fifteen years — his marriage, his reputation, the one stain on his honour that everyone believed they understood and nobody did. The tower of joy is the reason Ned Stark is the man he is in the main story, and the reason he is so badly misread by everyone around him, including the people who love him.",
          "In the ledger of the war it cost almost nothing and changed nothing about who held the throne. In the ledger of the whole story it may be the most consequential ten minutes anyone spends, and its cost is still being paid at the very end.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Militarily, nothing. The war was won before the swords came out. This is the rare battle whose entire importance is informational — it is not about ground or armies or a throne, it is about what one man learned at the top of a tower and what he did with it for the rest of his life.",
          "It changed Eddard Stark completely and invisibly. He went up to the war a straightforward man and came down from that tower carrying the one thing he could never tell anyone, and he carried it so well that it cost him his reputation and, eventually, his head, because keeping the secret meant letting the whole realm believe a lie about him that he would not correct. Everything tragic about Ned Stark’s story runs back to a promise made in that tower.",
          "And it set up the question the entire saga is, at bottom, built to answer. The three best knights in the realm chose to guard that tower rather than their king; a war’s worth of consequence follows from why. The story has been unfolding the answer, slowly and by inches, ever since, and the tower of joy is where it is buried.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ser Arthur Dayne, the Sword of the Morning, died at the tower, killed in the fight — by Ned Stark’s account not in a way that reflected well on how the numbers fell. Ned carried the great sword Dawn back to Starfall and returned it to House Dayne, to Arthur’s kin, personally; it was the act of a man honouring an enemy he had killed and had no wish to have killed. Ser Gerold Hightower and Ser Oswell Whent died beside him, all three of them keeping a post they had chosen over everything else the falling dynasty could have asked of them.",
      "Four of Ned Stark’s six companions died there too — among them Ethan Glover, Martyn Cassel, Theo Wull, Willam Dustin and Mark Ryswell in the northern reckonings, though the lists vary. Ned brought their bones north, and Lady Dustin’s bitterness toward House Stark in later years traces in part to a husband who rode south with Ned and came home as a memory.",
      "Lyanna Stark was in the tower, and Ned found her there, in a bed of blood, dying. What she said to him before she died, and what he took from that room and raised as his own, is the secret the whole story keeps — and it is kept here too, on purpose, because it is the one ending in this entire collection that a reader most deserves to reach on their own. Howland Reed, the only other survivor, never spoke of any of it, which is the most reliable thing anyone did with the truth of that day.",
      "Eddard Stark walked down from the tower of joy and did not truly come back from it. He built his whole later life around what happened there, and it killed him in the end, in another southern city, keeping faith with a promise nobody else knew he had made.",
    ] },

  { id: "greyjoy-rebellion", name: "The Greyjoy Rebellion", sub: "289 AC — the Iron Islands rise",
    meta: [["Who", "The Iron Islands vs. the Iron Throne"], ["Result", "The rebellion crushed; the Greyjoys broken"],
           ["Commanders", "Balon Greyjoy and Victarion · Robert Baratheon, Stannis Baratheon and the assembled lords of the realm"],
           ["Strength", "The Iron Fleet and the ironborn levy against the combined power of the Seven Kingdoms"],
           ["Ground", "Fair Isle at sea, and the bridges of Pyke by storm"]],
    blurb: "The ironborn declared a kingdom, lost their fleet at Fair Isle, and watched their fortress come down in a storm.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Six years after Robert Baratheon took the throne, Balon Greyjoy decided he could take a crown of his own. The reasoning was not stupid on its face. The realm had just fought a long civil war; its great houses had bled; the new king was known to prefer feasting and hunting to governing; and the Iron Islands had spent the whole of the Targaryen peace being told they could no longer live the way the ironborn believe men are meant to live, which is by taking rather than by growing or trading. Balon proposed to end that, declare the islands independent, and revive the Old Way by force.",
      "He opened the war the way the ironborn always open wars, with fire and surprise. The Iron Fleet fell on Lannisport in the night and burned the Lannister fleet at anchor — a real blow, cleanly struck, and for a moment it looked as though the audacity might pay. But a raid is not a war. Burning an enemy’s ships in harbour hurts him; it does not defeat him, and it does not stop the rest of a continent from mustering against you. Balon had struck the Westerlands and thereby guaranteed that Tywin Lannister, among others, would want him destroyed.",
      "What Balon had failed to reckon with was that Robert Baratheon, whatever his faults as a king in peacetime, was a genuinely formidable war leader who became a better version of himself the moment there was a war to fight, and that the same lords who had beaten the Targaryens were all still alive and could be assembled again. The whole realm mustered against the Iron Islands, and the islands, for all their ferocity at sea, are small, poor, and few. The arithmetic was always going to catch up with the audacity.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The war had two decisive actions and they are quite different in character. The first was at sea, off Fair Isle, where the Iron Fleet was met and destroyed. The second was on land, in the storming of Pyke itself, the Greyjoy seat.",
          "The Iron Fleet is the one genuinely first-class instrument the ironborn possess — longships and larger warships crewed by men who have been sailors and raiders their whole lives, in their home waters, fighting the one kind of war they are actually the best in the world at. On open water, ship for ship and man for man, the ironborn are dangerous out of all proportion to their numbers. The problem is that there are never enough of them, and the rest of the realm can build and crew more ships than the islands ever can.",
          "Against the ironborn came the assembled power of the Seven Kingdoms: a royal fleet and the fleets of the Reach and the Westerlands, a great host of lords, and Robert Baratheon at the head of it in the element he was made for. Among the commanders was Stannis Baratheon, given command at sea, whose cold competence was about to win the war’s decisive battle — and, in the way of Stannis’s whole life, be somewhat overshadowed by his brother’s more glamorous work on land.",
        ] },
      { h: "Fair Isle and the storming of Pyke",
        paras: [
          "The Battle of Fair Isle was where the rebellion was really decided, though it is the storming of Pyke that everyone remembers. Stannis Baratheon brought the royal fleet against the Iron Fleet off Fair Isle, in the waters between the island and the mainland coast of the Westerlands, and destroyed it. The details are not richly recorded, but the outcome is unambiguous and it was catastrophic for the ironborn: the Iron Fleet, the one weapon that made independence conceivable, was broken on the water. Victarion Greyjoy commanded the ironborn ships and was beaten by a soldier who had learned the sea the way he learned everything, methodically and without joy. Once the fleet was gone, the islands could neither raid nor be defended, and the war was effectively lost — everything after Fair Isle was the realm collecting on a debt the ironborn could no longer pay.",
          "With the sea cleared, the royal host landed on the islands and came to Pyke, the Greyjoy stronghold — a castle built out along a headland and on great stacks of rock standing in the sea, connected by bridges, about as forbidding a place to assault as exists in Westeros. The storming of Pyke is Robert’s battle and it is the one the singers kept. A tower was brought down — by a storm, by siege engines, or by both, the accounts lean on the drama rather than the engineering — and the breach let the attackers onto the bridges. The fighting on the bridges of Pyke was close, vicious, and exactly the kind of desperate work Robert Baratheon lived for; he was in the front of it, warhammer and all, and it became one of the founding stories of his reign.",
          "It was also where a number of reputations were made and one man in particular distinguished himself: Ser Jorah Mormont, among others, was knighted for his part in the storming, and Thoros of Myr made his name going first onto a bridge with a flaming sword. The breach was forced, the castle was taken, and Balon Greyjoy, sitting in the heart of the strongest fortress the islands possess, watched the whole realm come over his walls and had to yield.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the Iron Islands their fleet, their brief independence, and a great many of their fighting men, and it cost House Greyjoy nearly everything. Balon’s rebellion had been built on the belief that the Old Way could be revived by force in the modern realm, and Fair Isle and Pyke were the answer: it could not, because the modern realm was simply too large and too rich to be raided into submission by a few islands.",
          "It cost Balon his sons. Of the price the rebellion exacted from the Greyjoy family directly, the details belong in the fold below, but the shape of it is that Balon began the war with more heirs than he ended it with, and that the terms of the peace took something from him that he never stopped resenting.",
          "It cost the ironborn, more broadly, the last serious attempt to live the Old Way for a generation. The lesson of the Greyjoy Rebellion is the same lesson the ironborn refuse to learn every time: that reaving works against the weak and the isolated, and that against a united continent it is simply a way of getting a great many ironborn killed for a fleet’s worth of plunder.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It confirmed Robert Baratheon’s throne in the one way it had not yet been tested — that the man who had won it by rebellion could hold it against rebellion, decisively and fast. A king who cannot put down a rising is not a king for long; Robert put this one down inside a single campaigning season, and it bought his reign years of quiet.",
          "It reshaped the Iron Islands for a generation, forcing them back under the Iron Throne’s peace and taking a hostage to guarantee it — a Greyjoy child raised away from the islands, in a great southern house, as surety for his father’s good behaviour and as a lever if the good behaviour failed. That arrangement is one of the quiet time bombs of the whole saga, and it goes off, in its own way, years later.",
          "And it left Balon Greyjoy alive, unrepentant, and nursing a defeat he regarded as unfinished rather than final — which is exactly the condition in which a beaten rebel waits for the next moment of weakness in the realm that beat him. When that moment came, in the shape of a much larger war between the great houses, Balon was ready to try again, having learned from Fair Isle and Pyke almost nothing at all.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Balon Greyjoy survived his rebellion and kept his life and his seat, on the Iron Throne’s usual terms for a defeated great lord: submission, and a hostage. He never accepted that he had been beaten so much as interrupted, and he spent the years afterward waiting for the realm to falter again so he could reave in earnest. When it did, he crowned himself once more rather than join anyone else’s war — the same mistake, made again, with the same fundamental misreading of how small the islands are against everyone else.",
      "Two of Balon’s three sons died in the rebellion — Rodrik before the walls of Seagard, cut down in the fighting on the mainland, and Maron killed when the sea tower of Pyke came down in the storming. Their deaths left Balon with one living son, Theon, and it was Theon who was taken from him at the end of the war and given to Eddard Stark to raise as a ward and a hostage. A boy raised in another man’s house, belonging fully to neither the North nor the islands, is the personal cost of the Greyjoy Rebellion, and the story spends years collecting on it.",
      "Victarion Greyjoy, who had lost the Iron Fleet at Fair Isle, survived to command a rebuilt one and to nurse the ironborn’s grievances into the next war and beyond it, eventually far across the world. Stannis Baratheon, who had actually won the war at sea, watched his brother take the glory of Pyke, and added it to the growing account of services rendered and thanks withheld that he kept for his entire life.",
    ] },

  { id: "whispering-wood", name: "The Whispering Wood", sub: "298 AC — the War of the Five Kings",
    meta: [["Who", "Robb Stark vs. Jaime Lannister"], ["Result", "Jaime captured"],
           ["Commanders", "Robb Stark, aged sixteen · Ser Jaime Lannister"],
           ["Strength", "The whole of the Stark horse against a detachment of the Riverrun siege"],
           ["Ground", "A wooded valley near Riverrun, at night"]],
    blurb: "The night a sixteen-year-old boy trapped the finest swordsman in the realm in a wood.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Eddard Stark had gone south as Hand of the King and had been arrested for treason. In the north his eldest son called the banners, and what marched out of Winterfell was an army raised by a boy who had never commanded anything, on behalf of a father who was already beyond helping. Robb Stark’s stated war aim at this point was extremely limited: get his father and sisters back. Everything else that follows is improvisation.",
      "The strategic position was bad. Two Lannister armies were in the field. Ser Jaime Lannister had come up from the west, smashed the Tully host in the open, and was besieging Riverrun with a large army split into three separate camps around the castle — Robb’s grandfather inside, his uncle a prisoner, the riverlands being stripped. Lord Tywin Lannister was further east with the larger and more dangerous host, positioned to catch anything coming down the kingsroad. Robb had one army and two enemies, and moving against either exposed him to the other.",
      "He also had a river problem. To reach the riverlands at all he had to cross the Green Fork, and the only crossing was the Twins, held by Lord Walder Frey, an old man with a large family, a strong position and no intention of committing to anybody’s war for free. The price of the crossing was negotiated, and the terms included marriages — a promise made by a boy in a hurry, of a kind that seems cheap at the time.",
      "Once across, Robb did the thing that made his reputation. Rather than choose between his two enemies, he decided to fight both at once with one army by dividing it and lying about which half mattered.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Whispering Wood is a wooded valley near Riverrun, close enough to the castle that a force operating from the siege lines could ride into it and back in a night. Its shape is the whole battle: a bowl with high wooded sides and one usable route through the bottom. Cavalry that enters it in column cannot deploy, cannot see and cannot easily turn round. It is, in short, the best ambush ground within reach of the siege, and Robb had local men who knew it and Jaime did not.",
          "The Stark host was divided at the Twins. The larger part, mostly foot, went east up the kingsroad under Roose Bolton with orders that amounted to making a great deal of noise and not winning. Robb took the horse — a few thousand of them, the accounts vary — west and south, moving fast, hiding his numbers, and cutting off every scout and outrider the enemy sent. On the page the diversionary force is the greater part of the army and is meant to survive; on the screen Robb speaks of sending two thousand men to their deaths, which is more dramatic and rather less like the plan that was actually executed. Either way, its purpose was identical: to persuade Tywin Lannister that the northern army was in front of him.",
          "It worked because of the outriders. The single most important tactical act of the campaign was unglamorous — the systematic killing or capture of every enemy scout in the region, so that the Lannister commanders were operating blind while Robb was not. The direwolf that ran with the Stark van has a good deal to do with why the enemy scouts kept not coming back.",
          "Jaime Lannister was, at this point in his life, an outstanding fighter and an indifferent general. He was besieging Riverrun with his army in three camps, which is sound practice for investment and appalling practice if anybody attacks you, since no one camp can reinforce another quickly. When word reached him that a northern force was in the neighbourhood, he did not believe it could be the main host — that was supposed to be up the kingsroad in front of Lord Tywin — and he rode out with a body of horse to deal with what he assumed was a raiding party.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It was fought at night, in trees, in the dark, which is the reason no coherent narrative of it exists and why every retelling is a collection of fragments. Robb had his horse dismounted and waiting on both slopes of the valley, with archers above them, and had left the floor of the wood empty and inviting.",
          "The Lannister column came in along the bottom in the dark, strung out, in no formation to speak of because there was no formation the ground would permit. The opening was a signal — horns from the high ground on both sides at once — and then arrows down into a column that could not see where they were coming from and had no shieldwall because it was riding, not fighting.",
          "The first shock came from the slopes. Men and horse came down into the valley floor from two directions simultaneously, and hit a column that was already halted and already taking casualties. The specific tactical fact that decided it was the geometry: a mounted column in a defile has its strength in its length and its weakness everywhere else, and once the head and tail are both engaged the middle cannot get out. Jaime’s men were not beaten by better soldiers. They were beaten by being unable to become an army before the fight was over.",
          "The moment it turned — insofar as a battle that was decided before it started can be said to turn — was Jaime’s own response, which was characteristic and very nearly effective. Rather than try to extricate the column, he identified the enemy commander’s position and drove straight at it with everything he could gather, on the reasoning that if he could reach a sixteen-year-old boy in the dark the whole thing would come apart. He got a long way. Men of Robb’s own guard went down stopping him, and the accounts agree that he was within a short distance of the boy when he was finally pulled off his horse and buried under numbers.",
          "After that it collapsed quickly. A force that has lost its commander in a place it cannot retreat from does not conduct a fighting withdrawal; it dissolves into individual men trying to find a way out of a wood in the dark. The pursuit was mostly a matter of collecting them. A large number were taken alive, which mattered enormously — prisoners of rank are currency, and Robb had just acquired the most valuable single piece of currency in Westeros.",
          "The wood was only half of it. At first light the Stark host came out of the trees and fell on the siege camps around Riverrun, which were now leaderless and had no idea what had happened in the night, while the garrison sortied out of the castle into their rear. The camps, unable to support one another by design, were destroyed one after another. The siege of Riverrun ended inside a day.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Stark losses in the wood were remarkably light for a battle of that consequence — the advantage of ambushing a column at night is that most of the killing is done by people who are not being shot at. They were not nothing, and the men who were lost were lost in the worst place: several of the young lords riding in Robb’s own guard went down in front of Jaime Lannister’s charge, and they were the heirs of houses that had followed the Starks south out of loyalty. Robb had to write those letters at sixteen.",
          "The Lannister loss was catastrophic and mostly consisted of men taken rather than men killed. The besieging army was destroyed as a formation, a great many knights and lords of the west went into captivity, and the west’s campaign in the riverlands stopped dead.",
          "The house made was Stark. Robb went into that wood as a boy whose banners had been called for him and came out of it as a commander that grown men would follow anywhere. Within days the riverlords in his camp had proclaimed him a king, which was not his idea and which he did not refuse.",
          "The man ruined, temporarily, was Jaime Lannister — taken alive, held for a year and more, and reduced from the most feared sword in the realm to a bargaining counter in a tent. What that does to him is the most interesting thing that ever happens to the character, and it starts in this wood.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It broke the Lannister strategy in the riverlands in one night. Tywin Lannister, who had spent the campaign manoeuvring against a diversion, found that the real northern army was behind him, his son’s army did not exist any more, and his line of communication to the west was compromised. He withdrew east toward Harrenhal, which is a considerable admission from a man who did not make them.",
          "It also transformed the war politically. Up to this point Robb Stark had been a loyal lord’s son trying to free his father. After the Whispering Wood and the camps he was undefeated in the field, held the king’s uncle prisoner, and had the riverlords in his debt — and men in that position get offered crowns. The declaration of an independent north and riverlands is a direct consequence of a battle fought in the dark by a boy who had wanted a prisoner exchange.",
          "And it established the pattern that the rest of Robb Stark’s war follows: he wins his battles and finds that winning them settles less each time, because battlefield success and political position are not the same currency. The Whispering Wood is the purest demonstration of what he could do with an army. It is also the campaign in which he mortgaged a promise to buy a bridge, and promises of that kind are held by people who keep accounts.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "The men of Robb’s guard cut down in front of Jaime Lannister’s charge included Daryn Hornwood and Torrhen and Eddard Karstark, sons and heirs of two of the great northern houses. Lord Rickard Karstark lost two sons in that wood in the space of a few minutes and never afterwards regarded the war, or Jaime Lannister, or in the end Robb Stark himself, as anything but a debt owed to him. That grievance festers for two more years and detonates in a way that costs Robb half his cavalry at the worst possible moment.",
      "Jaime Lannister survived captivity, and the person who eventually came out of it was not the person who went in.",
      "The bridge that made the whole campaign possible was bought with a marriage promise to House Frey. Robb Stark did not keep it. Lord Walder Frey, an old man with a long memory and no interest whatever in being laughed at, waited two years and settled the account at a wedding in his own hall, with the northern army inside his walls and bread and salt already served. Everything Robb won in the Whispering Wood was undone in a single evening by a debt he incurred on the way to it.",
    ] },
  { id: "green-fork", name: "The Battle of the Green Fork", sub: "298 AC — the War of the Five Kings",
    meta: [["Who", "Tywin Lannister vs. the northern foot"], ["Result", "A Lannister victory over the wrong army"],
           ["Commanders", "Lord Tywin Lannister, with Ser Kevan · Lord Roose Bolton, with Tyrion Lannister among the enemy"],
           ["Strength", "A large Lannister host against a large northern one, both mostly infantry"],
           ["Ground", "Open ground along the Green Fork of the Trident"]],
    blurb: "Tywin Lannister won a battle, decisively, against the half of Robb Stark’s army that was there to lose it.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The Green Fork is one of the two halves of a single manoeuvre, and it makes no sense told on its own. When Robb Stark brought the North south to answer the arrest of his father, he faced the classic problem of the smaller, faster army against the larger one: Tywin Lannister’s host stood between him and the Lannister forces besieging Riverrun, and if Robb tried to march past it he would be caught and destroyed, while if he tried to fight it head-on he would simply lose. So Robb did the thing that makes his brief war famous. He split his army.",
      "He sent the greater part of his infantry — the slow foot, the bulk of the levy — south along the Green Fork under Roose Bolton, with orders to find Tywin, engage him, and above all fix him in place. Their job was not to win. Their job was to be an army in front of Tywin Lannister so convincingly that he did not notice the other, smaller, all-mounted half of Robb’s host slipping around him through the crossings to fall on the force besieging Riverrun. The Green Fork was a feint conducted at the scale of a full battle, and the men fighting it were, in the coldest sense, expendable.",
      "Among them, entirely against his will, was Tyrion Lannister. Captured and released earlier in the war, he had fallen in with a band of the mountain clans of the Vale — wild, quarrelsome, spectacularly violent hill people whom he had bought with promises of weapons and plunder — and had been delivered by his own father into the van of the Lannister host, in the most exposed position on the field, as a way of getting him killed with plausible deniability. The Green Fork is thus one of the very few battles in the story observed from the inside by someone who understood exactly how little his own side valued him.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The battle was fought on open ground along the Green Fork of the Trident, the kind of broad country that suits a straightforward clash of two infantry-heavy hosts and offers no clever ground to either side. This was deliberate on nobody’s part; it was simply where the two armies met when Bolton’s northmen came far enough south to be found.",
          "Tywin Lannister’s host was large, well-ordered, and commanded by the most feared soldier of his generation, with his brother Ser Kevan as his reliable second. Tywin arrayed it conventionally and well: a strong centre, cavalry on the wings, and — the detail that mattered — his weakest and least reliable troops, including the hill clans and the newly arrived Tyrion, placed on the left, where he expected them to break and where their breaking would do him the least harm.",
          "Roose Bolton commanded the northern foot, and Bolton is the second key to reading this battle. He fought it the way he did everything, which is to say carefully, and with a close eye on the preservation of Roose Bolton. The northern host he led was substantial but it was, by design, the part of Robb’s army that could be spent, and Bolton fought it in a manner consistent with knowing that.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It was a big, blunt, conventional battle, and Tywin won it clearly. The two hosts came together in the ordinary way and the Lannister strength and discipline told; the northern line was pushed and eventually broken, and by the end of the day Tywin Lannister held the field and had inflicted heavier losses than he took. On the plain accounting of who held the ground afterward, it was a solid Lannister victory.",
          "The battle’s most famous stretch is the least strategically important, and that is the point of how it is told. Tyrion, stuck in the van with his hill clansmen, survived by a combination of luck, the clansmen’s ferocity, and the frank cowardice of ducking the worst of it — he is knocked cold early and wakes to find the battle won, which is his own sardonic verdict on the whole affair. The mountain clans fought like the wild things they were and came away blooded, paid, and armed, which was the entire arrangement.",
          "The thing that makes the Green Fork a hollow victory is not visible on the field at all. Tywin had beaten an army; he had not beaten Robb Stark’s army, or at least not the part of it that could hurt him. While he was winning decisively along the Green Fork, the real blow was being struck elsewhere, and the news of it turned his victory to ash within a day or two. Bolton had done his job to the letter: he had presented Tywin with a battle worth winning, and the winning of it had cost Robb nothing he was not prepared to lose.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the North a large part of its infantry — real men, really killed, in a battle their own king had sent them to fight as a distraction. This is the uncomfortable underside of Robb Stark’s brilliance as a young commander: the manoeuvre that made his reputation was paid for by the deaths of the men in the half of the army that was never meant to win, and they died beating no one and holding nothing.",
          "It cost Tywin Lannister something less tangible and more valuable: certainty. He had fought and won a full-scale battle against a Stark host and been made a fool of by it, because the host he beat was the decoy. For a man whose entire authority rests on never being outmanoeuvred, being outmanoeuvred by a boy of sixteen — even a boy who spent other men’s lives to do it — was a wound to exactly the thing Tywin most needed intact.",
          "For Tyrion it cost a great deal of fear and bought a great deal of information. He came out of the Green Fork alive, having watched his father try to spend him, and the knowledge of precisely where he stood in Tywin’s accounting shaped every dealing the two of them had for the rest of Tywin’s life.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "The Green Fork’s importance is entirely relative to the Whispering Wood, which it made possible. Read alone it is a Lannister win; read as half of a plan it is the successful sacrifice that let Robb Stark catch and shatter the other Lannister army and take its commander. The two battles have to be held together, and held together they are one of the most elegant pieces of generalship in the whole saga.",
          "It also introduced, quietly, two of the war’s decisive players in their true colours. Roose Bolton is first seen properly here, husbanding his strength and fighting a battle whose purpose was to lose usefully — a man whose care for the preservation of his own is exactly the quality that makes him so dangerous later, when the North’s survival and Roose Bolton’s survival stop pointing in the same direction. And Tyrion is shown the precise value his father places on him, which is a lesson he does not forget.",
          "Strategically it confirmed that Robb Stark could not be beaten by conventional means, because he would not fight conventional battles when a clever one was available. It took the Lannisters most of the war and a broken guest-law to solve a problem that the Green Fork first showed them they had.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Tywin Lannister came away from the Green Fork with a victory that curdled almost at once, and he spent the following stretch of the war on the back foot, pulling his forces about the riverlands in response to a boy who kept beating him. He solved the Robb Stark problem in the end, but he solved it with a letter and a wedding rather than a battle, having concluded — correctly, and to his lasting credit as a strategist and lasting infamy as a man — that he could not solve it on a field.",
      "Roose Bolton survived the Green Fork, as Roose Bolton survived most things, by making sure the cost fell on other men. The care he showed for his own strength here is the same care that eventually let him change sides at the decisive moment with an intact following to bring to the other table.",
      "Tyrion Lannister survived by being knocked unconscious early and by the ferocity of the hill clansmen he had bought, and walked away with his life, his clan allies, and a clear-eyed understanding of his father’s hopes for his longevity. The mountain clans got their weapons and their plunder and went home to the Vale richer and better armed than they had come.",
    ] },

  { id: "oxcross", name: "The Battle of Oxcross", sub: "299 AC — the War of the Five Kings",
    meta: [["Who", "Robb Stark vs. Stafford Lannister"], ["Result", "A green Lannister army destroyed in its sleep; the west laid open"],
           ["Commanders", "Robb Stark, with Grey Wind · Ser Stafford Lannister"], ["Strength", "A mounted northern host against a raw Lannister army still forming up"],
           ["Ground", "Oxcross, in the hills of the Westerlands near Casterly Rock"]],
    blurb: "Robb Stark did the thing no army in living memory had done — he took the war into the Westerlands, and killed a Lannister host before it was awake.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "After the Whispering Wood and the relief of Riverrun, Robb Stark held the initiative and a captured Kingslayer, and had to decide what to do with a war he was winning battle by battle without getting any closer to a settlement. Killing Lannister armies in the riverlands was not translating into anything permanent, because Tywin could always raise another and the war’s centre of gravity — the throne, the gold, the Lannister power base — lay far to the west, in country no enemy army had entered in living memory.",
      "So Robb did the audacious thing again. Rather than sit in the riverlands trading battles, he took his fast, mounted host and drove west, over the hills and into the Westerlands themselves — into the Lannisters’ own rich, unravaged home country, the source of the gold that was paying for the whole war on the other side. It was a raid at the scale of an invasion, and its purpose was as much economic and political as military: to show the realm that the Lannisters could be reached in their own heartland, and to force Tywin to turn away from the riverlands to defend his own gates.",
      "Waiting in the west, sort of, was a new Lannister army. Ser Stafford Lannister — Tywin’s uncle by marriage, an unremarkable man given an important job because of his name — had been raising fresh troops around Casterry Rock and Lannisport to replace the losses of the war so far. This army was large but green: newly levied, half-trained, not yet properly formed into a fighting host, and camped in the false security of country that had not seen an enemy in generations. It did not expect Robb Stark, because nobody expected an army to come that far that fast.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Oxcross lies in the hills of the Westerlands, on the approaches to the heart of Lannister country, and the ground’s only real significance is that Stafford Lannister’s army was camped there in the belief that it was safe. A camp is not a battlefield; it is an army at its most vulnerable, spread out, unarmoured, its horses picketed and its watch thin, and the whole disaster of Oxcross turns on Robb catching Stafford in exactly that state.",
          "Robb’s host was the mounted arm of his army — the same fast striking force that had won the Whispering Wood — men who could cover ground quickly and hit hard, unencumbered by the slow foot that Bolton had spent at the Green Fork. Against a formed and ready enemy on good ground they would have had a hard fight. Against a sleeping camp they were a catastrophe waiting to be delivered.",
          "Stafford’s army was numerous and worthless in equal measure, at least on that night. Raw levies in camp, unready and unwarned, are not really an army yet; they are a large number of individual men who have not learned to act as one, and the one thing that turns a crowd like that into a fighting force — time, drill, a competent commander who has prepared them — was exactly what Robb denied them by arriving before they were ready. The show and the books agree closely on the essentials here; the show simply compresses the campaign around it.",
        ] },
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It was a night attack on an unprepared camp, and those are not battles so much as slaughters with a thin excuse. Robb’s host got in among Stafford’s army before it could form up, and the result was exactly what a surprise assault on sleeping, half-trained troops always is: no line, no order, no chance to bring numbers to bear, just a camp full of men trying to find their weapons and their feet in the dark while mounted northmen rode through them.",
          "Robb’s direwolf, Grey Wind, is a real factor in the accounts of Oxcross and not merely a flourish. The wolf went through the horse lines and the men in the dark as something the Lannister levies had no way to fight and no experience of imagining — a large, silent, purposeful predator killing sentries and hamstringing horses ahead of the riders. Part of what made the surprise total was that the things meant to give warning, the pickets and the watch, were being killed before they could raise the alarm. It is one of the clearest instances in the story of the direwolves being genuine weapons of war rather than pets.",
          "The green army never had a battle to fight. It was overrun where it lay, and its numbers — which should have been its strength — became its undoing, because a large body of untrained men packed into a camp in a panic is a mob trampling itself in the dark. By morning Stafford Lannister’s host had ceased to exist as a fighting force, at a cost to Robb that was trivial by comparison. It is the cheapest major victory of Robb’s whole war and the one that did the Lannisters the most direct economic harm.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "It cost the Lannisters a whole army — the reserve of manpower Tywin had been raising to replace his losses — destroyed before it could take the field, and it cost the Westerlands their sense of safety. Robb’s host then spread out across the west and did what an unopposed army does in rich, undefended country: it raided, burned, and plundered the Lannister heartland, taking gold, cattle, and prisoners, and doing real damage to the economic engine of the enemy’s war effort. The gold that paid the crown’s debts came from these hills, and for a season an enemy was loose among them.",
          "It cost Tywin Lannister the initiative and forced exactly the reaction Robb had wanted: Tywin had to break off from the riverlands and turn west to deal with the Stark host loose in his own lands, which relieved the pressure on Robb’s allies and rearranged the whole strategic map of the war. For a while, on the field, everything Robb touched turned to victory.",
          "What it did not do — and this is the tragedy embedded in Robb’s whole run of brilliance — is bring him any closer to winning the war. Oxcross was a triumph that produced plunder, prestige, and a strategic headache for Tywin, and none of those were the same as a settlement. Robb kept winning battles; the war kept not ending; and the gap between the two is where his story turns to ruin.",
        ] },
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Tactically it confirmed Robb Stark as the most dangerous field commander in the war and the direwolves as real assets in it, and it demonstrated that the Lannister heartland was not the sanctuary everyone had assumed. That demonstration had a political value beyond its military one: it damaged the aura of Lannister invincibility at exactly the moment Tywin most needed it intact.",
          "Strategically, though, Oxcross is one of the battles that shows why Robb ultimately lost. He was operating deep in the west, far from his own base, at the end of a long supply line, winning battles that did not compel a surrender — and while he was doing so, the political ground beneath him was shifting: the Tyrells were about to come into the war on the Lannister side, his own bannermen were beginning to tire, and events at home in the North were moving against him. A war of brilliant raids can only be won if the enemy breaks, and the Lannisters did not break.",
          "Oxcross belongs, in the end, to the same lesson as the Whispering Wood and the Green Fork: that Robb Stark could win any battle he chose to fight, and that winning battles was not the same as winning the war. He proved it here at the enemy’s own doorstep, and it still was not enough.",
        ] },
    ],
    fate: [
      "Ser Stafford Lannister died at Oxcross, killed in the destruction of the army he had been raising — a man remembered almost entirely for having lost it, which is an unkind but not inaccurate epitaph. His death and the loss of his host were a real blow to Tywin, who had counted on those men.",
      "Robb Stark came out of Oxcross at the very height of his fortunes: undefeated, loose in the enemy’s heartland, holding the Kingslayer, called King in the North by men who believed in him completely. Everything after this point is the slow reversal of that height — the loss of Winterfell, the breaking of a marriage promise to House Frey, the defection of Roose Bolton — and the reader who knows where the road ends reads Oxcross with a particular kind of dread, because it is very near the last of the good days.",
      "Grey Wind, whose work in the dark at Oxcross made the surprise total, went the whole way with his master, and shared the end that came to the Starks at another man’s wedding on the Green Fork — an end told in its own entry, and one that the triumph at Oxcross does nothing to soften and everything to sharpen.",
    ] },

  { id: "blackwater", name: "The Battle of the Blackwater", sub: "299 AC — the War of the Five Kings",
    meta: [["Who", "Stannis Baratheon vs. King's Landing"], ["Result", "Stannis broken; the Lannister-Tyrell alliance made"],
           ["Commanders", "Stannis Baratheon & Ser Imry Florent · Tyrion Lannister, then Tywin Lannister & Mace Tyrell"],
           ["Strength", "A very large fleet and army against a thin garrison and a good engineer"],
           ["Ground", "The mouth of the Blackwater Rush and the city’s southern wall"]],
    blurb: "A chain, a river full of wildfire, and a dwarf who saved a city that never thanked him.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "By the second year of the war the five kings had sorted themselves into two problems and three sideshows. Robb Stark was winning battles in the riverlands without getting closer to the capital. Balon Greyjoy was raiding the North for his own reasons. And Stannis Baratheon, having absorbed the greater part of his dead brother’s enormous stormlands and Reach host, was sitting on Dragonstone with the largest fleet in Westeros and the only claim to the Iron Throne that was arguably legal.",
      "Stannis’s reasoning was sound and, in the way of Stannis, entirely unsentimental. King’s Landing was the throne. It was also the softest target in the realm: Tywin Lannister was hundreds of miles west chasing Robb Stark around the riverlands, the city’s garrison was a corrupt watch of gold cloaks and a scattering of sellswords, and the boy on the throne was a child with no army of his own. A single fast strike up the Blackwater Rush would put Stannis inside the walls before Tywin could turn around. Every week he delayed made that less true.",
      "What Stannis did not adequately account for was that the Hand left holding the city was Tyrion Lannister, who had spent the preceding months doing three quiet things: purging the small council of men who would open a gate, hoarding the pyromancers’ output of wildfire in the thousands of jars, and having the smiths of the city forge an enormous iron chain. He had also, without telling his sister, sold the last politically valuable Baratheon-adjacent asset the crown possessed in exchange for the one thing that could actually save it — the Tyrells of Highgarden, and the eighty thousand swords the Reach could raise. That bargain was struck in secret and was still riding hard for the city when the fleet came up the river."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Blackwater Rush enters the bay in a broad, deep, tidal mouth beneath the southern face of King’s Landing. It is navigable by war galleys all the way to the city’s river wall, which is why the capital sits there. It is also a bottleneck. A fleet that rows into that mouth has a stone city on one bank, open shore on the other, and only one way out — back the way it came, into the tide.",
          "Stannis came with a fleet that dwarfed anything the crown could put on the water, and an army swollen by the lords who had knelt to Renly and then, on his death, knelt to whoever was next. That army’s composition mattered enormously. A large part of it consisted of stormlanders and Reachmen who had loved Renly, had never particularly loved Stannis, and were following him because the alternative was losing their lands. It was a host held together by arithmetic rather than loyalty, which is a thing that fights well until the first bad half-hour.",
          "Stannis gave the fleet to Ser Imry Florent, his good-brother by his queen — a competent enough man of no naval genius, chosen partly for family reasons, which is how the Baratheon-Florent court did most things. Imry’s orders were speed. He rowed straight into the river mouth in full strength without first sending light craft ahead to sound the water or scout the banks. In the novels that is explicitly the decisive error and it is named as such at the time.",
          "The city could field a few thousand gold cloaks of doubtful quality, a handful of Kingsguard, some hundreds of Lannister household men and a mercenary company or two. Its real defences were engineering: hoardings on the walls, spitfires and catapults on the ramparts, and Tyrion’s two secrets. On the page, the chain was strung on winches in towers at the river mouth and lay slack beneath the water while the fleet went over it. On screen the chain is discussed and then quietly dropped, and the trap is the fire alone. The page’s version is the crueller one, and the difference is worth knowing."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was almost gentle. Stannis’s van rowed into the mouth on the tide at night, brushed aside the token royal squadron that came out to meet it, and began putting men ashore on the near bank to work toward the Mud Gate. From the walls it looked exactly like what it was: a city about to fall in a few hours. Along the water came one more ship, a heavy hulk under a single sail, drifting badly and apparently abandoned, and the leading galleys let it come on because a derelict is not a threat.",
          "It was full to the deckhead with wildfire. When it broke open in the middle of the packed fleet, the substance did what wildfire does — it burned on water, it burned on stone, it burned on men in the river, and it could not be smothered or drowned. The fire spread ship to ship through a formation too tightly packed to disperse. Green flame stood up off the Blackwater high enough to be seen from the far shore. Hundreds of vessels were destroyed in the space of a few minutes without a single blow struck by a defender.",
          "In the novels the chain then came up, and that is the tactical hinge. Raising it did not keep the fleet out; it kept the fleet in. Everything still afloat in the river mouth was locked inside a burning basin with a tide running against it and no exit. The result was less a naval battle than an incineration with a lid on it.",
          "And yet the fire did not win the day, because the fire was on the water and Stannis’s army was on the land. Stannis had already landed a substantial force on the near bank, and once he understood what had happened to his fleet he did the only thing left to him, which was to press the assault on the walls immediately and with everything he had. Ladders went up against the southern wall. Rams came at the King’s Gate and the Mud Gate. The gold cloaks, who had never been more than adequate, began to shred.",
          "The critical hour came when the defenders on the wall lost their nerve. In the novels the man who broke was Sandor Clegane — burned as a child, sent out again and again on sorties through the postern into a night that was on fire, and finally refusing to go back out. His departure left the Mud Gate sector without its most effective commander at the worst moment. On the page and on the screen alike it was Tyrion who took what men would follow him, went out through a sally port, came around behind the ram crews on the strand, and destroyed them. That sortie is what held the gate long enough for the field to change.",
          "The field changed from the west. Tywin Lannister had broken off from the riverlands and force-marched his army south, and Mace Tyrell had brought the Reach up the roseroad to join him. They came into Stannis’s rear on the near bank at the last possible hour, an unbroken host of fresh horse hitting an army that had spent the night watching its own fleet burn. Stannis’s line, already brittle because so much of it was Renly’s men, did not so much break as evaporate. The killing stroke was psychological as well as physical: at the head of the Tyrell van rode a knight in Renly Baratheon’s distinctive green armour, and the stormlanders who had followed Renly and had been told he was gone looked up and saw him coming at them. The novels give that armour to Ser Garlan Tyrell; the show gives it to Ser Loras. Either way it was a deliberate piece of theatre and it worked precisely as intended.",
          "The pursuit was short and one-sided. Stannis’s landed force was cut apart against the river it could no longer cross. What could be dragged off the strand and rowed away was a fraction of what had come up the water at dusk."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "Stannis lost the great majority of his fleet and a very large part of his army in a single night, along with most of the lords of the stormlands and the Reach who had come to him after Renly. He never again commanded a force of that size. The financial and naval loss was the more permanent of the two: ships take years and a treasury to replace, and Stannis had neither.",
          "The city paid in gold cloaks and in whole districts of the riverfront, which burned. The Lannister regime paid almost nothing in men it valued, which is exactly why the battle is remembered the way it is.",
          "The Tyrells paid nothing at all and collected everything. They had arrived at the perfect moment with the whole strength of the Reach intact, had saved the throne in the eyes of the city, and had a granddaughter ready to marry it."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "The Blackwater ended the war as a five-sided contest. From that night on it was the Lannister-Tyrell alliance against everyone else, and that alliance had food, gold, numbers and the capital. Robb Stark’s brilliant campaign in the riverlands became irrelevant in an afternoon because he could no longer win the war even by winning every battle in it.",
          "It also fixed Stannis’s strategic position for the remainder of his story. Having lost the south he had to look for a war he could still join, and the only one available was in the North.",
          "And it established the pattern the rest of the saga runs on: the person who solves the problem and the person who receives the credit are almost never the same person, and the gap between them is where most of the story’s bitterness comes from."]}],
    fate: [
      "Tyrion led a sortie through the Mud Gate that held the wall at the critical moment and had his face opened by one of his own side. The city celebrated Tywin. It is the clearest single instance of the story’s rule that being useful and being rewarded have nothing to do with each other.",
      "The blade was swung by Ser Mandon Moore of the Kingsguard, almost certainly on instruction from Cersei, and Tyrion survived only because his squire Podrick Payne put a spear through the man. He woke days later stripped of the Handship, disfigured, and formally thanked by nobody.",
      "Davos Seaworth’s ships were in the leading squadron when the wildfire went up. In the novels four of his sons died on the river that night; the screen keeps one of them, Matthos, and kills him there. Davos himself was thrown clear and washed up half-drowned on a spit in the bay.",
      "Among the dead on Stannis’s side were Lord Bryce Caron and a long list of stormlanders, and the fleet’s commander Ser Imry Florent, whose decision to row in blind had put them all there. Stannis withdrew to Dragonstone with a fraction of his host and spent the following season a broken king looking for a new war.",
      "Sandor Clegane’s refusal to return to the fire ended his service to House Lannister. He walked out of the city that night and out of the war."] },

  { id: "red-wedding-battle", name: "The Red Wedding", sub: "299 AC — the War of the Five Kings",
    meta: [["Who", "House Frey & House Bolton vs. their own guests"], ["Result", "The northern army destroyed"],
           ["Commanders", "Lord Walder Frey & Lord Roose Bolton, with Tywin Lannister’s consent"],
           ["Strength", "A northern host at rest, disarmed by custom, inside another man’s walls"],
           ["Ground", "The Twins — a bridge-castle on the Green Fork, and the camps around it"]],
    blurb: "Not a battle. A massacre under guest right, which is worse.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Robb Stark had won every battle he fought and was losing the war anyway. After the Blackwater the crown had the Reach behind it, Winterfell had been burned by an ally turned enemy, his brothers were believed dead, his sisters were in Lannister hands, and his own bannermen were beginning to count the cost. He needed to end the war with a stroke that restored the North’s position, and the plan he settled on was to take Moat Cailin from the south and drive the ironborn out of his homeland. To do that he had to march an army back north. To do that he had to cross the Green Fork.",
      "There is exactly one crossing of the Green Fork in that country, and House Frey owns it. The Freys had been bought once already, at the start of the war, when Catelyn Stark negotiated passage in exchange for a pledge that her son would marry one of Lord Walder’s daughters. Robb then married a woman of his own choosing instead — a Westerling girl of the westerlands in the novels, a Volantene healer in the show — and in doing so handed the most reliably aggrieved man in Westeros a grievance he could dine out on for the rest of his life.",
      "The Freys withdrew from the war. Without the crossing and without their strength Robb’s campaign was finished, so an apology was arranged: Robb’s uncle Edmure Tully would marry a Frey daughter in his place, the wedding would be held at the Twins, and the northern army would be permitted to cross afterward. Walder Frey accepted with a show of grumbling reluctance that everybody read as characteristic and nobody read as tactical. What was not visible from the outside was that Roose Bolton — Robb’s most senior northern commander, who had spent the war spending other men’s soldiers and preserving his own — had already opened a correspondence with Tywin Lannister, and that the terms of the arrangement were the North itself."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Twins is not a castle so much as a fortified bridge: two towers on opposite banks joined by a span over the river, each with its own gate and its own hall. It is a structure built entirely around control of movement. Nothing crosses without permission, and anything inside it can be divided from anything else by shutting a door.",
          "The northern host camped on the near bank in the fields around the castle, in the ordinary sprawl of an army at rest at a wedding — pavilions, cookfires, wine issued by the host, weapons stacked, horses picketed, sentries thinned because there was no enemy within a hundred leagues. Robb’s direwolf, which had a reliable habit of objecting to people who meant his master harm, was penned separately on the pretext that it made the household nervous.",
          "The party that entered the castle for the feast was small: the king, his mother, his wife on the screen, his uncles, and the senior lords and captains of the northern and riverland host. That is the whole point of the design. Almost every man capable of holding the army together was placed inside one room, under a roof belonging to somebody else, in courtesy dress, with their swords at the door.",
          "Against them Walder Frey had his own household in numbers, the retinues of a very large family, and crossbowmen quietly seated in the musicians’ gallery above the hall. Outside, Bolton’s men were camped among the northmen as comrades of two years’ campaigning, positioned by unit rather than by chance, and the Frey levies had been drawn up within reach of the camps. Roose Bolton wore mail beneath his wedding clothes; on the page Catelyn Stark notices it a few moments too late to matter.",
          "And over all of it lay guest right, the oldest law the North keeps — that a man who has eaten his host’s bread and salt beneath his host’s roof is safe there, and a host who breaks it is cursed by gods old and new. Bread and salt were served. This was not an oversight. It was the mechanism. Guest right was the only thing disarming that hall, and the Freys served it deliberately, knowing what it bought them."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The wedding itself was performed properly, and the feast that followed was long, loud and dull in the way of feasts, which is what allowed the arrangements to be completed while everyone watched. The bride was bedded with the usual noise, which removed a large part of the company from the hall and left the remainder scattered, drunk and pleased with themselves. Then the doors were closed. In a hall with two hundred guests and one exit, closing the doors is the entire tactical plan.",
          "The signal was the music. The musicians in the gallery — who had been indifferent all evening, and who were not musicians — struck up a song about a great house that had annihilated a lesser one for presuming to rival it. It is a Lannister song, and Tywin Lannister was two hundred miles away, and the choice of it was a message from him: this is what happens, and this is who arranged it. Anyone in that hall who knew the tune knew what it meant, but the crossbows in the gallery were already levelled by the time the meaning arrived.",
          "The killing inside the hall was over quickly. Crossbows from a gallery into a seated, unarmoured, half-drunk company at fifteen feet do not leave much for swords to finish. There was no line, no melee, no possibility of rally; the men who mattered were dead or dying within a minute or two of the first bolt. Restraint is the right register here, so let the record simply say that the Frey household did the work with knives afterward and that the leadership of the northern army ceased to exist between one course and the next.",
          "The second killing was the larger one and is the part usually forgotten. Simultaneously, outside the walls, the northern camps were attacked. Frey levies came at them from the castle side while the Bolton contingents already inside the encampment turned on the men beside them. An army asleep in its tents, its lords absent at a feast, its arms stacked, attacked at close quarters by troops it had been marching with for two years, has no defence at all — there is nobody to give an order and nobody to form on. Some northmen got to horses. Most did not. The Stark host was not defeated in those camps; it was dismantled.",
          "The one thing the plan could not control was the river. The Twins straddles it, and men who ran the right way and could swim went into the water in the dark and out the other side. The escapes were few and mostly accidental — a commander who had stepped outside, a party that had not yet come up, individuals who happened to be beyond the gate. Everything within the trap was lost.",
          "By dawn the Freys held the crossing, the Boltons held what remained of the northern army as their own, and the war in the riverlands had been decided without a battle being fought. It cost Walder Frey a reputation he had never much used and Roose Bolton nothing whatsoever."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The North lost the greater part of a generation of fighting men in one night, and the great houses of the North lost the sons and heirs they had sent south. The riverlands lost the leadership of their rebellion. Riverrun was besieged and forfeited, the Tullys were dispossessed, and the war that had begun with a lord going south to serve as Hand of the King ended with his family holding nothing.",
          "The Freys were made materially rich and socially radioactive in the same evening. They were given lands, a castle and a lordship; they also acquired a name that no house in Westeros would sit at table with, which is a real cost in a society that runs on hospitality. Guest right is not sentiment. It is the load-bearing custom of a country where every journey depends on a stranger’s roof, and it had been broken publicly and cynically for money.",
          "Roose Bolton got the North. Tywin Lannister got the war ended for the price of a letter, and said so plainly afterward: a dozen men at dinner is cheaper than a battle, and he considered anyone who found that objectionable to be confusing sentiment with arithmetic."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Strategically the Red Wedding ended the northern rebellion outright and left the crown effectively unopposed in the south. It also removed the only army in Westeros that had been winning, at a moment when the realm was about to need one.",
          "Politically it did something worse and slower. It taught every lord in the Seven Kingdoms that the oldest protections were negotiable if the payment was large enough, and it left the North occupied by a house it despised, garrisoned by a family it would not forgive. Occupations founded on an atrocity are cheap to establish and very expensive to keep.",
          "It is also the moment the story stops pretending. Up to that night the war has the shape of a contest between a just cause and an unjust one. Afterwards the reader and the viewer both understand that the story is not that kind of story, and that competence, honour and the winning of battles are not what decides who ends up holding the ground."]}],
    fate: [
      "Robb Stark died in that hall, and the crossbow bolts that opened the killing were meant for him from the first. His mother Catelyn died moments after him, having taken a hostage and found that Walder Frey did not value his own wife enough for it to work. Roose Bolton delivered the final blow to the king himself.",
      "On the screen Robb’s wife Talisa was present and was among the first killed, along with the unborn child. The novels are different in a way that matters: Jeyne Westerling was not brought to the Twins at all and survived the night, which leaves a loose thread the books have not yet pulled.",
      "Grey Wind was penned away from his master precisely because he could not be fooled, and was shot in the kennels. The mutilations the Freys performed on the bodies afterward were a deliberate piece of political humiliation, and Westeros duly noticed.",
      "Edmure Tully, whose wedding it was, was taken alive and held for years as a lever against Riverrun. The Blackfish escaped by an accident of timing, being outside the hall when the doors shut. Arya Stark and Sandor Clegane arrived at the gate as the camps were being destroyed and were turned away from it, which is very probably what saved her.",
      "Roose Bolton was named Warden of the North and Walder Frey was given Riverrun. Neither man enjoyed the tenure for long, and the reckoning when it came was carried out by people the Freys did not think of as dangerous.",
      "In the novels one further thing came out of the Green Fork that the screen omits entirely: something was pulled from the river days later by the brotherhood without banners, and what walks the riverlands afterward under the name Lady Stoneheart hangs Freys as a matter of policy."] },

  { id: "castle-black", name: "The Battle of Castle Black", sub: "300 AC — beyond the Wall",
    meta: [["Who", "The Night's Watch vs. Mance Rayder's host"], ["Result", "The Watch holds; Stannis arrives"],
           ["Commanders", "Mance Rayder, Tormund, the Magnar of Thenn · Donal Noye and Jon Snow for the Watch"],
           ["Strength", "A claimed hundred thousand free folk against roughly a hundred defenders"],
           ["Ground", "Seven hundred feet of ice, one tunnel, and an undefended southern face"]],
    blurb: "A hundred sworn brothers holding seven hundred feet of ice against a hundred thousand free folk.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Mance Rayder had done the impossible thing: he had united the free folk. Ninety-odd clans, raiders, cave-dwellers, giants, Thenns and hornfoot men who had spent all of recorded history killing one another had been talked, bullied and married into a single migrating host. He had not done it out of ambition. He had done it because something was coming out of the far north that no village could stand against, and the only wall in the world that might was held by an order sworn to keep him on the wrong side of it.",
      "That is the tragedy under the whole engagement. Mance was not raiding. He was evacuating a country. What he wanted was to get his people south of the Wall and out of reach of the dead, and the Night’s Watch could not permit it without ceasing to be the Night’s Watch. Both sides were correct and both sides knew it, which is why the fighting is unusually free of villains and unusually full of dead men.",
      "The Watch, meanwhile, was in no condition to hold anything. The Great Ranging had gone north the previous year with two hundred and more of the order’s best and had come back as a handful of survivors with a dead Lord Commander and no plan. Of the nineteen castles along the Wall, only three were still garrisoned at all. The Watch that faced Mance amounted to something like a hundred men at Castle Black, many of them old, several of them boys, and none of them in command in any settled sense — the Lord Commander’s seat was vacant and the officers spent the last weeks before the attack arguing about who should sit in it."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The Wall is a defensive work of such extravagant scale that it becomes a problem for the men on top of it. Seven hundred feet high, three hundred miles long, sheer on its northern face and unclimbable in armour: nothing on earth gets a host over it. But nothing gets a hundred defenders along it either. A garrison of a hundred spread over three hundred miles is not a garrison, it is a rumour, and Mance knew exactly how thin it was.",
          "So he split his force, which was the correct answer and the only one available to him. The bulk of the host — the mammoths, the giants, the mass of the clans — came at Castle Black from the north to fix the defenders in place and to break into the one tunnel through the ice, which is the only door in three hundred miles. Meanwhile a picked raiding party had been sent over the Wall far to the east weeks earlier, and was to come up on Castle Black from the south, where the castle has no wall at all. Castle Black was never fortified against its own side. It did not need to be.",
          "The southern party was led by Tormund and by the Magnar of Thenn, and it included the raiders who had climbed the ice in the small hours with hemp and hooks. The northern host was Mance’s main strength, with giants riding mammoths at the head of it and a great deal of tar-soaked timber for the tunnel.",
          "The Watch had ice, height and stored ordnance, and very little else. Its defences amounted to the tunnel’s three iron gates and a wall of ice-blocks behind them, casks of oil and pitch, barrels of stones for dropping, and archers on the parapet. On the page the man who organised all of it was Donal Noye, the one-armed armourer, who took command because nobody else would and turned out to be the best soldier on the Wall. The screen distributes his work between Ser Alliser Thorne and Jon Snow, and adds a piece of defensive machinery — an enormous iron scythe on a chain, swung down the face of the Wall to sweep climbers off it — that the novels do not have."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It opened in the dark and it opened in the wrong place. The southern party struck Mole’s Town first, the village of hangers-on a few miles down the kingsroad, and destroyed it, both to cover their approach and to draw the garrison out. Then they came up on Castle Black from behind while the fires beyond the Wall told the defenders to look north.",
          "The northern attack went in more or less simultaneously, and this is where the Watch’s numbers stopped mattering, because two attacks against a hundred men mean fifty men in each place. Giants and mammoths came up the causeway to the tunnel mouth. Free folk swarmed the northern face with hooks and lines. On the parapet, seven hundred feet up, a handful of brothers worked the winches, poured burning oil down the ice and cut climbing lines, and the arithmetic of the drop did the rest: a man knocked off the Wall does not come back.",
          "The fight for the castle itself was the ugly one. The southern party got in among the buildings, where there were no walls and no formation and the defenders were fighting between their own halls and stables in the dark. The Watch was outnumbered several times over at that end and held mostly because the yard is a confined space and the free folk had no siege discipline. Both tellings agree that the southern assault came within very little of taking the castle outright, and that the officer who steadied it and put the reserves where they were needed was Jon Snow, who had been north with these people, knew how they fought, and was until that week under suspicion of being one of them.",
          "The decision, though, was in the tunnel. Everything else was noise: if the gate went, the entire host came through and the Wall was finished for good. Mance sent giants against it, and a giant harnessed to a mammoth can pull an iron gate off its hinges. The novels give the tunnel to Donal Noye and a few brothers, fighting in a black stone throat behind the outer gate, against Mag the Mighty — the largest of the giants — in a space too narrow for either to retreat, and Noye buys the Wall its night. The screen restages the same defence with Grenn and five brothers holding the inner gate against a giant, and the substance is identical: a small number of men are asked to stand in a corridor and not leave it, and they do not.",
          "The Watch held the tunnel and held the yard, at a cost it could not have paid twice. The following night Mance would have come again with the same host against a garrison reduced by half, and the outcome of that would not have been in doubt.",
          "It was not decided by the Watch at all. Stannis Baratheon, having read the same letters everyone else had ignored, had landed his surviving strength at Eastwatch and marched west along the Wall. He came into the wildling camp in column with heavy horse — armoured cavalry against an unarmoured host encamped in the open with no cavalry of its own and no line facing south. The free folk had no answer to it. Nobody has an answer to it. The host that had taken a year to gather came apart in an afternoon and the war beyond the Wall was over.",
          "It is worth stating plainly what that means. Every king in Westeros had received the Night’s Watch’s appeal for help. Exactly one of them came, and he came because he had lost his own war and needed a new one, and he was still the only one who came."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Watch lost a large fraction of the men it had left, which for an order already down to three manned castles was close to fatal. It also lost the one thing it had been trading on for centuries: the belief that the Wall could not be reached. It had been reached, from both sides, by an enemy with no engines and no discipline.",
          "The free folk lost far more. Their host was scattered, their camps taken, and tens of thousands of people who had crossed a continent to escape what was behind them were left in the open beneath the Wall with winter coming on. That is the real bill, and it is paid by people who were never soldiers.",
          "Mole’s Town was destroyed with everyone in it. The village had no defences and no strategic value, and it is a fair index of what the campaign was actually like for the people between the two armies."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Stannis’s intervention converted the Wall from a lost cause into a base of operations, and gave him something no other claimant had: a legitimate cause, a defensible seat, and the beginnings of a northern following. It also put a king with an army and a strong opinion about religion in the same courtyard as an order sworn to have no politics, which was going to be a problem.",
          "For the Watch it forced the question it had been avoiding for eight thousand years. There were now tens of thousands of free folk in front of the Wall, and only two things could be done with them: leave them where they were, or let them through. Leaving them was not a neutral act, because of what was coming behind them, and everyone at Castle Black who had been north knew it.",
          "And it settled the succession. The order elects its Lord Commander, and it had just watched one particular steward do the job all night without holding it."]}],
    fate: [
      "Ygritte died in the yard at Castle Black. On the page she is killed anonymously in the confusion of the fighting, which is the crueller and better version; the screen gives the arrow to the boy Olly, whose family had been killed by the raiders, and thereby sets up a great deal of what happens to Jon Snow afterward.",
      "Donal Noye died in the tunnel with Mag the Mighty, each killing the other. The Wall’s survival is very largely his doing and the novels give him no monument for it. Grenn and the brothers who held the inner gate in the show’s version died there too.",
      "Pyp, Styr the Magnar of Thenn and a long list of brothers and free folk fell in the castle. Mance Rayder was taken alive when Stannis broke the camp, and what Stannis and his red priestess did with a captured king who would not kneel is a matter the two tellings disagree about.",
      "Jon Snow was elected Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch soon afterward, largely on the strength of that night, and used the office to bring the free folk through the Wall. That decision was correct, was necessary, and cost him more than the battle did."] },

  { id: "hardhome-battle", name: "The Massacre at Hardhome", sub: "301 AC — beyond the Wall",
    meta: [["Who", "The living vs. the Army of the Dead"], ["Result", "Thousands added to the enemy"],
           ["Commanders", "Jon Snow and Tormund · the Night King and his lieutenants"],
           ["Strength", "Several thousand free folk and a few dozen crows against a host that grows as it kills"],
           ["Ground", "A ruined harbour town under a cliff, with the sea at its back"],
           ["Telling", "Screen only — the novels record Hardhome as a disaster but do not depict this battle"]],
    blurb: "The moment the war everyone was ignoring arrived in front of witnesses.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Jon Snow’s policy as Lord Commander rested on a single unpopular proposition: that every free folk man, woman and child left north of the Wall was not a rival but a future enemy soldier. The Watch’s eight-thousand-year quarrel with the wildlings had been, in strategic terms, a quarrel over grazing land. The actual enemy did not care who owned the land and recruited from both sides equally, by killing them.",
      "So the Watch went north to bring people home. Hardhome was the largest concentration of free folk left in the world — a ruined settlement on a cove of the Shivering Sea where thousands who had not marched with Mance had gathered, and the only place they could be lifted from in numbers, because it had a harbour. Jon took a small party of brothers, a fleet of ships hired from a captain who wanted paying, and Tormund, without whom no wildling would have listened to a crow at all.",
      "The negotiation on the beach was the difficult part and by any reasonable measure it succeeded. It was not universally accepted — the Thenns in particular would not go — but enough elders agreed that the boats began loading. What the plan could not accommodate was that the enemy had a schedule of its own and had arrived at the ridge above the town.",
      "One point of fact worth stating clearly: this engagement exists on screen only. The novels have Hardhome as a place of ill fame and a botched rescue, with a ranging sent by sea and letters coming back describing dead things in the water, but the battle depicted here is the show’s invention. It is also the single most useful thing the show ever did for the story’s stakes, because it took an abstraction and showed a room full of people what it costs."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Hardhome sits on a cove beneath a high ridge, with the sea in front and a wall of rock behind. In a fight against an ordinary enemy that is defensible ground: one approach, a natural funnel, and a line of retreat by water. Against this enemy every one of those features became a liability. The high ground belonged to the attacker from the first minute. The sea meant the defenders could not disperse. And the funnel merely concentrated an assault that did not need to be concentrated.",
          "The free folk had raised a timber palisade with a gate, which was adequate against raiders. There were several thousand people inside it, most of them not fighters — families, elders, children — with a fighting element of perhaps a few hundred and a handful of Night’s Watch. There was one giant, Wun Wun, who was worth a company on his own.",
          "The attacking host had no formation, no commanders in the ordinary sense, no supply and no need of any. It had four things the living did not: it did not tire, it did not fear, it did not need to be told what to do, and every casualty it inflicted became a reinforcement. Above it, on the ridge, stood a small number of White Walkers — the intelligent, armed and articulate part of the enemy — and the figure the show calls the Night King.",
          "Against all of that the living possessed exactly three effective weapons, and only two were present. Fire kills the risen dead. Dragonglass shatters a White Walker. Valyrian steel does both, and there was one such sword in Hardhome that night, on Jon Snow’s hip, and neither he nor anyone else yet knew what it could do."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It began with weather, which is how it always begins. The wind turned, a wall of snow came down off the ridge, the dogs went frantic, and the light failed in the middle of the day. Then the ridge above the town moved. What came down was not a charge in any recognisable sense — no line, no wedge, no shock — but a landslide of bodies that fell down a cliff face, broke on the rocks, and got up.",
          "That was the first tactical lesson and it was delivered in about ten seconds: the terrain damage that would destroy a living army does nothing at all. A drop that kills men merely delivers wights. From that moment the defenders were not holding a position, they were holding a door in front of a rising flood, and the flood had no bottom.",
          "The gate held briefly, and the fight at the palisade is the only phase in which the defence functioned as a defence. Fighting men held the line, the giant broke the pressure wherever he stood, and the elders drove the noncombatants toward the boats. But a shield line works by making an enemy pay to advance, and this enemy had nothing to pay with and no reason to stop. Behind the palisade the killing was in the alleys and the huts — close, unformed, individual, the worst possible ground for men in armour and the best for an enemy with no interest in survival.",
          "Then came the second lesson, which was worse than the first. As the free folk fell, the enemy commanders raised them where they lay. There was no interval, no burial, no chance to burn the bodies. A defender who went down became an attacker inside the line, behind the shields, among the families, immediately. This is the mechanic that makes the war against the dead insoluble by ordinary means: every unit of force the living commit is a unit of force loaned to the enemy the moment it fails. Attrition does not favour the side that starts with more men. Attrition does not favour anybody but them.",
          "The one thing that went the living’s way happened almost by accident. Jon Snow was caught by a White Walker in the hall where the dragonglass had been stored and, having lost the obsidian, met the blow with his own sword — and the Walker came apart like ice under a hammer. That single exchange changed the strategic picture of the entire war: Valyrian steel, of which a small and countable number of blades exist in the world, works. It was information bought at the cost of a town, and it was the only thing worth anything that came out of the night.",
          "The withdrawal was the last phase and it was a withdrawal in name only. Boats were rowed off the beach loaded past their marks while thousands were still on the shore and there was neither time nor space to come back for them. The rearguard fought to the water’s edge and got aboard by yards. What the survivors watched from the boats was the enemy commander walking down to the shoreline, raising his arms without hurry, and the whole population of the town — everyone who had not reached the water, including the ones who had died in the boats’ own shadow — standing up together to face them.",
          "There was no pursuit, because none was necessary. The enemy had gained several thousand soldiers in an afternoon and lost, so far as anyone could count, one."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The free folk lost the last large concentration of their people outside the Wall, and the Night’s Watch lost most of the party it sent. The ships got away with a fraction of those they came for — enough to matter politically, nowhere near enough to justify the trip.",
          "The greater cost was to the enemy’s advantage rather than the living’s loss, and it is the same number counted twice: thousands subtracted from one side and added to the other, which is a swing of double the casualty figure. No campaign in the histories works like that.",
          "There was a political cost as well. Jon Snow returned to the Wall having spent brothers’ lives to save wildlings and having failed to save most of them, which to a garrison that had spent its whole career killing wildlings looked less like strategy than like proof."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It ended the argument about whether the threat was real, at least for the men who were there. Everything the Watch had been saying for years and everything the realm had been dismissing as northern superstition was demonstrated in front of a few hundred witnesses, several of whom were free folk chieftains who had been on the other side of a war a season earlier.",
          "It also converted the free folk into allies in fact rather than in policy. People who have watched their own dead turn on them do not need to be persuaded about who the enemy is, and the alliance that eventually stood at Winterfell is founded on that beach.",
          "And it established the two rules that every later engagement with the dead had to be built around: kill the commanders rather than the host, and never let your own casualties lie where they fall. Both rules would be forgotten at least once when it mattered."]}],
    fate: [
      "Karsi, the chieftain who had spoken for the boats and put her own children on them first, was the last of the elders holding the line and did not get off the shore. So did the Lord of Bones, killed earlier by Tormund in the argument over whether to trust a crow at all, and the Magnar of the Thenns, who had refused to board and was among the first taken.",
      "Most of the Night’s Watch party sent with Jon Snow did not return. Those left on the beach were raised with the rest, which meant that the men who came back watched brothers they had eaten with the previous week stand up on the shore and look at them.",
      "The dragonglass stores brought north for the evacuation were lost in the town, which set the supply of the only cheap weapon against the enemy back by a year.",
      "Jon Snow carried the news of what Longclaw had done back to Castle Black, and it was received by an order that had already decided he was a wildling in a black cloak. The mutiny that followed happened for many reasons, and his insistence on this one was chief among them."] },

  { id: "battle-of-the-bastards", name: "The Battle of the Bastards", sub: "302 AC — the North",
    meta: [["Who", "Jon Snow vs. Ramsay Bolton"], ["Result", "Winterfell retaken by the Starks"],
           ["Commanders", "Jon Snow, Sansa Stark and Tormund · Ramsay Bolton and Lord Umber"],
           ["Strength", "Rather over two thousand against about six thousand"],
           ["Ground", "Open rolling field south of Winterfell, chosen by the defender"],
           ["Telling", "Screen only — the novels have not reached this battle"]],
    blurb: "A cavalry envelopment, a wall of corpses, and a rescue from the Vale.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The Boltons held the North by right of an atrocity and by grant of a crown a thousand miles away. That is a weak title in any country and a particularly weak one in a region that keeps its grudges in writing. Roose Bolton had understood this and had governed accordingly — cautiously, through marriages, letting the northern houses sulk. His son did not govern at all. By the time Ramsay Bolton held Winterfell in his own name he had alienated the Karstarks’ neighbours, hanged people who had not yet decided to oppose him, and made an enemy of the one house whose name still commanded the North’s obedience by mistreating the girl he had married to borrow it.",
      "Against him came the least likely restoration in the story. Jon Snow, no longer bound by the Watch, and Sansa Stark, who had escaped Winterfell and had every reason of her own, spent a season riding round the North asking houses for men. They got very few. The great houses had lost their sons at the Twins, the Manderlys and the Glovers would not move, and what finally marched south from Castle Black was a small force of northern hill clans, a scattering of loyal houses, the child Lady of Bear Island with sixty-two men, and several thousand free folk fighting for a country that had been shooting at them since the dawn of the Age of Heroes.",
      "Sansa, who knew Ramsay better than anyone alive, told her brother plainly that the army was too small and that he must not fight until it was larger. She also, without telling him, wrote to Petyr Baelish, who had the Knights of the Vale — several thousand heavy horse, uncommitted, and the finest cavalry in Westeros. Her reasons for withholding that from Jon are the sharpest character question the show ever set itself, and the honest answer is a mixture of mistrust, self-protection and the habits of somebody who has survived four courts by never showing a card early.",
      "This battle exists only on screen. The novels are heading somewhere in the vicinity, but nothing resembling this engagement has been published, and none of it should be read back into the books."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Ramsay chose the field, which in this battle is close to the whole story. He drew up on open, gently rolling ground south of Winterfell — no woods to hide a flank, no marsh, no river, no defile. Bare ground favours the larger army absolutely, because it lets numbers be brought to bear everywhere at once and gives the smaller force nothing to anchor on. Jon accepted the ground because he had nowhere else to fight: he could not besiege Winterfell with two thousand men in winter, and he could not stay in the field long enough to starve it.",
          "The numbers were roughly six thousand against a little over two. Ramsay had cavalry, archers in quantity, and disciplined foot in armour. Jon had free folk who fought as individuals, a giant, some hundreds of northern spears and a stiffening of horse. On paper it is not a battle, it is a formality.",
          "The two commanders were opposites in a way that decided everything. Jon Snow was a genuinely capable field officer — brave, quick in a crisis, personally formidable, and a very good judge of a defensive position, as Castle Black had shown. But he fought as a man who leads from the front, and a commander at the front cannot see his own army. Ramsay Bolton was no soldier at all in the professional sense and had no interest in becoming one. What he had was an instinct for other people’s weak points and the patience to arrange a situation in which someone else destroyed themselves. He did not plan to defeat Jon Snow’s army. He planned to make Jon Snow ruin it for him.",
          "Jon’s dispositions were sound as far as they went: infantry in the centre, his small cavalry on the wings, orders to receive the Bolton charge rather than deliver one, and specific instructions that the line was not to advance under any provocation. Ramsay’s dispositions were sound in a different way: archers forward, cavalry held on both wings out of the initial contact, and the mass of his infantry kept back in close order behind everything with pikes and interlocking shields, which is not a formation for winning a firefight. It is a formation for closing a bag."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was not a manoeuvre. Ramsay brought Jon’s young brother Rickon onto the field between the armies, cut him loose and told him to run, and had archers shoot at him while he ran. It was not sadism for its own sake, or not only that: it was aimed at a specific man for a specific tactical purpose. Jon rode out alone to meet the boy, at the gallop, into the open ground.",
          "That single act destroyed the northern battle plan in about a minute. Jon was now isolated in front of his own line with the enemy cavalry to his front, and his infantry could either stand and watch their commander be ridden down or advance to save him. They advanced. The order not to move had been the one thing holding the army’s shape, and the man who gave it had made it impossible to obey.",
          "The first shock was therefore a meeting engagement on ground of Ramsay’s choosing with Jon’s line already disordered from its advance and thinned by archery. It was fought to a rough standstill in the centre, in a press of men in the open with corpses accumulating underfoot, and it went on long enough for both sides to become thoroughly committed and thoroughly blind.",
          "The turn came from the wings. Ramsay’s cavalry, which had taken no part in the opening, now came in from both flanks simultaneously — a double envelopment, the oldest and most effective manoeuvre in the field, and one that ordinarily requires a beaten enemy or an extremely disciplined one to execute. Ramsay could execute it because his enemy had walked forward into the space between his wings of their own accord. The northern flanks were rolled in.",
          "Then came the part that makes this battle distinctive. The Bolton infantry advanced from the rear in close order, shields locked and pikes levelled, and closed the ring — not to break the northern army but to compress it. As the ring tightened the northmen and free folk were pressed into a space too small to swing a weapon in. Men died on their feet without a blow being struck at them, suffocated and trampled by their own side. The Boltons meanwhile were dragging their dead and the enemy’s into a rampart around the circle, so that the survivors were penned by a wall of bodies as well as by shields. It is a battle of annihilation in the classical style, and for perhaps a quarter of an hour it worked perfectly.",
          "Ramsay’s error was structural and it was the error of a man who had never commanded anything. In order to close the ring he had committed every soldier he possessed to its circumference. He had no reserve, no cavalry left in hand, and a formation that was facing inward. An army arranged in a circle around its enemy has its back to the whole of the rest of the world.",
          "The Knights of the Vale came onto the field in column from the south and struck that circle in the rear at the charge. Heavy horse against unsupported infantry facing the wrong way is not a fight. The ring came apart from the outside in, and what had been the tightest encirclement in the story became several thousand men in the open with cavalry among them and nowhere to form. The pursuit ran all the way back to Winterfell’s gates.",
          "The castle itself was carried almost at once. Ramsay had left a token garrison because he had never expected to need one, and the gate was broken in by the giant who came up with the assault. Winterfell was in Stark hands again for the first time since the war began."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The northern host was very nearly destroyed in the process of winning. The free folk in particular were slaughtered — they had come south on Jon Snow’s promise, they were in the centre of the ring, and a great many of them never got out of it. The house contingents that had answered the call lost heavily out of small numbers, which for a place like Bear Island means most of the fighting men it had.",
          "The Bolton army ceased to exist. So did House Bolton’s hold on the North, which had never rested on anything but that army and the fear of it.",
          "The victory was also a debt. It had been bought with the Vale’s cavalry, at the price of an obligation to Petyr Baelish, who did not lend armies out of family feeling."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Winterfell returned to the Starks, and with it the North’s allegiance — the lords who had refused to march now arrived promptly to say they had always meant to. Within days the assembled northern and Vale lords were being asked to choose who should lead them, and the man they chose was not the one the law of inheritance pointed at, which stored up a problem for later.",
          "Strategically the North was now a unified kingdom again at the exact moment the Wall was about to need one, and its army had just been reduced to a third of what it should have been in order to achieve that.",
          "And it left two people who had retaken their home together holding entirely different views about who had actually won it — one of whom had held a decisive card in reserve, and one of whom had nearly lost an army because she did."]}],
    fate: [
      "Rickon Stark was shot down in the open ground between the armies, a few strides short of his brother’s hand. The archery was deliberately arranged to fail until the last shot, because the point was never the boy.",
      "Wun Wun the giant broke Winterfell’s gate and died in the courtyard immediately afterward, shot by Ramsay personally as the castle fell. He was the last giant known to be alive.",
      "Smalljon Umber, who had delivered Rickon to the Boltons, died in the melee at Tormund’s hands. Ramsay Bolton was taken alive in the courtyard and given to Sansa, and what she did with him was to leave him in his own kennels with his own hounds, which had not been fed for a week. She did not stay to the end of it, but she stayed long enough.",
      "House Bolton was extinguished. Its flayed man came down off Winterfell’s walls and the direwolf went back up.",
      "Jon Snow was proclaimed King in the North in the great hall a short time later, on the initiative of a ten-year-old girl who shamed the assembled lords into it. Sansa watched from the side of the room, and Petyr Baelish watched Sansa."] },

  { id: "loot-train", name: "The Loot Train Attack", sub: "303 AC — the Reach",
    meta: [["Who", "Daenerys, Drogon & the Dothraki vs. the Lannister column"], ["Result", "A field of fire, three centuries on"],
           ["Commanders", "Daenerys Targaryen and Qhono · Ser Jaime Lannister and Lord Randyll Tarly"],
           ["Strength", "One dragon and a khalasar against an army strung out on the march"],
           ["Ground", "Open plain on the goldroad, south of the Blackwater Rush"],
           ["Telling", "Screen only — no equivalent engagement occurs in the novels"]],
    blurb: "The first time in a hundred and fifty years that a dragon was used on a Westerosi army.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Daenerys Targaryen had landed in Westeros with overwhelming force and had spent the opening weeks of the campaign being comprehensively outmanoeuvred. Her advisers had counselled a war fought by proxy and by siege — Westerosi armies besieging Westerosi castles, so that she would not arrive as a foreign conqueror burning her own future subjects. It was politically intelligent and militarily disastrous. Her fleet was ambushed and destroyed at sea, Dorne and Highgarden were knocked out of the war within days of each other, and her attack on Casterly Rock took a castle that had been deliberately emptied.",
      "The Lannister counterstroke had been the best-executed operation of the war. While the Unsullied were being drawn west to the Rock, the main Lannister field army had marched south into the Reach, taken Highgarden — which had been stripped of its own strength — and turned the richest region in Westeros from an enemy asset into a supply base. Two things came out of Highgarden. The first was gold, enough to pay the crown’s enormous debt to the Iron Bank and buy sellswords with the remainder, and it went ahead under escort and reached the capital. The second was the harvest: wagon after wagon of grain, at the beginning of winter, which is the commodity that actually decides whether a besieged city can hold.",
      "It was the food column that Daenerys attacked, and she attacked it because she had finally run out of patience with being advised. Every clever, careful, Westerosi-flavoured plan she had been given had lost her a battle. She had three dragons and a horde of light cavalry, and she chose to use them in the only way they had ever worked."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "The column was on the goldroad, moving north-east toward King’s Landing across open, dry, harvested farmland — no forest, no defile, no fortified position within a day’s march. That country is chosen by armies because wagons move well on it. It is the worst possible ground on which to be caught by cavalry and the worst conceivable ground on which to be caught by anything airborne.",
          "The Lannister force was a marching army rather than a deployed one: a long train of provision wagons with escorting foot strung out along it, cavalry ahead and behind, and the whole thing several miles from end to end. Jaime Lannister commanded, with Randyll Tarly beside him — a genuinely formidable field soldier, generally reckoned the best in the Reach, whose recent defection had made Highgarden possible in the first place. Both men were competent and neither had any doctrine for what was about to happen, because nobody in Westeros had needed one for a century and a half.",
          "The attacking force was a Dothraki khalasar — several thousand horse-archers with no armour, no baggage and no interest in a set-piece — accompanied by Daenerys on Drogon. Numbers are not the useful measure here. The useful measure is that the Dothraki fight at speed on open plain, which is precisely the ground on offer, and that the dragon made every defensive formation the Lannisters knew either useless or actively fatal.",
          "One defensive asset existed. Qyburn had built for Cersei a heavy anti-dragon crossbow — a scorpion of enormous draw, capable of throwing a bolt hard enough to hurt a full-grown dragon — and a prototype had gone out with the column. It was a serious weapon, and its performance that day would shape the next two years of the war."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The first phase belonged to the horse. The Dothraki came out of a low ridge to the column’s rear at the gallop, in a line miles wide and with no attempt at concealment beyond the last minute, and the sound of them arrived before they did. Jaime and Tarly did the correct textbook thing and did it well: the wagons were abandoned where they stood, the infantry was pulled off the road and formed into a shield wall facing the threat, with pikes set and archers behind. Against cavalry alone that line would have held. Steady infantry in formation beats horsemen who have nothing but bows and courage, and the Lannisters had the better troops.",
          "That was the trap, and it was not a trap anyone had laid deliberately. Forming a dense body of men shoulder to shoulder is the answer to cavalry and the invitation to a dragon. Drogon came in low along the line from the flank, in a single pass down the length of the formation, and the shield wall that was the army’s protection became the reason its casualties were what they were. There was no second decision to make. Men in ranks cannot disperse fast enough, and men who break ranks are ridden down by the Dothraki waiting for exactly that.",
          "The centre of the column went in the first minutes. What followed was not really a battle at all: it was cavalry work among broken infantry, punctuated by further passes over anything that reformed, and the systematic destruction of the wagon train. Some of the burning was deliberate and some of it was simply what happens when a dragon operates near several hundred tons of dry grain. Either way the harvest of the Reach ceased to exist in about a quarter of an hour.",
          "The one moment when the attack could have gone wrong was the scorpion. It was hauled into position, aimed and shot, and it struck — the first evidence in living memory that a dragon could be wounded by something built in a workshop. The bolt did not bring Drogon down. The dragon turned on the weapon and destroyed it, and Daenerys dismounted on the field to work the shaft free, which put the most important person on either side on the ground in the middle of a burning battlefield with her mount grounded beside her.",
          "That produced the closing episode, which was a single cavalry charge by one man. Jaime Lannister, seeing the dragon down and its rider exposed, took a lance and rode at her — a decision that was tactically hopeless and strategically the only one on offer, since killing Daenerys Targaryen personally was the last way the day could still be won. He got closer than he had any right to. He was intercepted before the point of contact by another rider who put them both into the deep water of the river beside the road, which is the only reason the charge did not end the way such charges end.",
          "There was no pursuit worth the name. There did not need to be. The column had been annihilated in place, and the survivors were men who had run early and far."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Lannister field army lost a large part of its infantry and the whole of the supply train it had marched into the Reach to collect. The crown kept its gold, which had already gone through the gates of King’s Landing, and lost its food, which had not. For a regime whose plan for winter was to sit behind the strongest walls in Westeros and wait, that was the more expensive of the two by a wide margin.",
          "The Dothraki lost some tens of riders. That is not a misprint and it is the entire point of the engagement.",
          "Something else was spent that day that does not appear on a casualty return. A dragon had been shot and had bled, in front of an army, and the men who built the weapon now knew the range at which it worked."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It settled the military question of the war immediately and permanently. There was no combination of Westerosi troops that could meet a dragon in the field, and everyone on both sides understood it by nightfall. From that point the war in the south was not about armies. It was about walls, about anti-dragon artillery, and about whether Daenerys Targaryen could be persuaded not to use the weapon she had.",
          "It changed the political question at the same time and in the opposite direction. Her advisers had spent a year telling her that a Targaryen who arrived burning Westerosi soldiers would be received as her father’s daughter rather than as a liberator, and the first thing she did with a dragon in Westeros was burn a Westerosi army in a field. That the army was carrying stolen grain and that she had been losing without it are both true and neither is what the survivors told people.",
          "The screen frames the whole engagement deliberately: it is the Field of Fire staged again three centuries on, but filmed from among the men on the ground rather than from the sky, so that the arrival of dragons in Westeros is experienced as a catastrophe rather than a triumph. That choice of vantage is the episode’s argument, and it is the same argument the rest of the story makes about her."]}],
    fate: [
      "Randyll Tarly and his son Dickon survived the field and were taken with the other prisoners. Offered the choice between bending the knee and the alternative, Lord Randyll refused on the grounds that he had sworn to a Westerosi house and would not kneel to a foreign queen with a horde at her back; his son refused with him rather than let him stand alone. Daenerys had them both burned in front of the assembled captives, over Tyrion’s objection. It cost her the Tarly name, and rather more than that.",
      "House Tarly ended there, in the field, which mattered a great deal to one man at Oldtown who was the last of it.",
      "Jaime Lannister was dragged out of the river by Bronn and survived. What he had seen — a shield wall burned in one pass, and his sister’s war lost in an afternoon — is the beginning of the end of his service to Cersei, though it took him a long while to admit it.",
      "Qyburn’s scorpion was rebuilt in numbers on the strength of what it had done. The proof that a dragon could be hit from the ground was the single most valuable thing the crown took out of the battle, and it was paid for later, at sea and over the walls of King’s Landing."] },

  { id: "long-night-battle", name: "The Battle of Winterfell", sub: "305 AC — the Long Night returns",
    meta: [["Who", "The living vs. the Army of the Dead"], ["Result", "The Night King destroyed"],
           ["Commanders", "Jon Snow, Daenerys Targaryen, Ser Jorah Mormont, Grey Worm · the Night King"],
           ["Strength", "The combined armies of half a continent against a host that doubles itself"],
           ["Ground", "The fields before Winterfell, the walls, the yard and the godswood"],
           ["Telling", "Screen only — the novels have not reached this battle"]],
    blurb: "Everything the story had been whispering about for eight seasons, decided in one night in a godswood.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "Everything in the saga that is not about a chair is about this. The Long Night is the event at the bottom of Westerosi history — an eight-thousand-year-old winter in which the dead came south, ended by an alliance of the first men and the children of the forest and commemorated by a wall, an order of sworn brothers and a great many stories nobody believed. The Night’s Watch had spent the entire main narrative writing letters about it and being ignored by everyone with an army.",
      "The alliance that finally formed did so late and grudgingly. It took Jon Snow going south to bend the knee to Daenerys Targaryen, a hazardous expedition beyond the Wall to bring a captured wight back as evidence, and a parley in a pit in King’s Landing at which the crown agreed to send its armies north and then simply did not. What actually marched to Winterfell was the North, the Vale, the free folk, the Unsullied, the Dothraki, two dragons and a handful of individuals who mattered out of all proportion to their number.",
      "The strategic decision that shaped the battle was made in Winterfell’s hall in the days before it. The enemy’s host could not be beaten by killing it, because killing it made it larger. But the wights were not independent creatures — they were animated and directed by the White Walkers, and the Walkers themselves derived from the figure the show calls the Night King. Kill him and the whole army falls. That reduced the problem from an unwinnable battle of attrition to a decapitation, and decapitations require you to make the target come to you. So the defenders offered him the one thing he had crossed a continent for: the boy in the godswood who is the living memory of the world, sitting alone under a tree with almost nobody around him, as bait.",
      "This battle is screen material entirely. The novels have not published anything of the kind and none of its particulars should be treated as canon of the page."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "Winterfell is a strong castle with two curtain walls, a broad ditch between them and the deepest crypts in the North beneath it. It is not, however, a fortress designed for a siege by an enemy that does not eat, and its greatest asset — the godswood inside the walls — was also the objective the enemy was coming for.",
          "The defenders laid out their position in depth: a screen of trenches and stakes on the open field, the Dothraki cavalry massed in front of everything, the Unsullied in formed ranks behind them as the main line of battle, catapults forward of the walls, a broad fire trench dug in a ring outside the ditch to be lit when the outer ground was given up, archers on the walls, and the noncombatant population sent down into the crypts. Both dragons were held airborne with their riders, waiting for the Night King, since killing him from the air was the plan they actually intended to win by.",
          "Two of those decisions were poor and were criticised the moment the episode aired. Cavalry deployed forward of the infantry is a formation for charging a line that can be broken and pursuing an enemy that runs, and this enemy did neither. Artillery placed in front of the infantry is artillery that can only shoot once. Both arrangements assumed a conventional opponent, and the opponent was not conventional in any respect.",
          "Against them came a host without number, in three parts. The wights were the mass: fast, silent, tireless, entirely without self-preservation, and reinforced by every casualty on either side. The White Walkers were the officers — a small number of them, mounted, armed with ice and difficult to kill by any means the defenders had in quantity. And there was one dead dragon, taken beyond the Wall and raised, which cancelled out the living’s single greatest advantage.",
          "The living’s effective weapons remained what they had been at Hardhome: fire, dragonglass and Valyrian steel. The dragonglass had been mined and worked at Dragonstone and shipped north in bulk, so for the first time every soldier in the line had a weapon that worked. It did not help as much as it should have."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "The opening was the worst quarter-hour of the night. The Dothraki, their blades set alight by the red priestess so that the whole van of the army was visible for miles, charged into the darkness beyond the trenches at an enemy nobody had yet seen. The lights went out — not all at once but in a wave, from the far end back — and then there was silence, and then a few riderless horses came back. Several thousand of the finest light cavalry in the world had been swallowed without a single message reaching the line. It is the most economical demonstration in the whole story of what this enemy does to conventional force.",
          "The first shock came against the Unsullied, who were the only troops on the field with the discipline to receive it. A phalanx of spearmen in armour, ranked deep, with shields locked, is the correct formation against a mass attack, and it held longer than anything else on the field held that night. But there is no such thing as breaking the enemy’s morale here. The wights climbed over their own dead to reach the spears, and the line was ground back rather than broken, which is the same thing arriving slowly.",
          "The dragons went in and made the second discovery of the night: a blizzard of the enemy’s own making had closed over the field, and two riders in cloud could see neither the ground nor each other. Fire cleared lanes through the wights, and the lanes filled in behind them. Meanwhile the dead dragon came up through the cloud and there was a dragon fight over Winterfell, which occupied both living dragons and their riders for a long stretch of the battle and removed the only weapon the decapitation plan depended on from the place where it was needed.",
          "On the ground the withdrawal to the walls was made under pressure and the fire trench was lit — late, and only after Daenerys came down low to burn it alight herself. Fire is one of the two things the dead genuinely cannot pass, and for a while it worked exactly as intended and the enemy simply piled up along it. Then the enemy piled into it. They walked into the flames in numbers until enough bodies had smothered enough of the trench to make a causeway of themselves, and crossed on their own dead. There is no defensive obstacle that works against a force willing to spend an unlimited quantity of itself to fill it.",
          "The walls went next, and went quickly, because wights climb. What followed was the long middle of the battle: fighting in the yards and along the parapets and inside the halls, in the dark, in a castle the defenders knew and the attackers did not care about. It was survivable only in small knots, and the units that had fought as units for the first two hours ceased to exist as units.",
          "Then the enemy commander did the thing everybody had known he would do and nobody had solved. He raised the dead of the battle itself — both armies’ fallen, where they lay, in the middle of the fighting, including the several thousand Dothraki lost in the first minutes and the defenders killed on the walls in the last hour. The living’s numbers halved and the enemy’s doubled in a single moment, inside the castle, with no line left to reform behind. He also raised the dead in the crypts, which were the one place in Winterfell held to be safe and were the one place full of Stark dead going back thousands of years. That was the collapse. From that point there was no battle, only survival.",
          "And it was at that point that the actual plan came due. The whole night had been an argument about the godswood — the enemy commander had come for the boy under the tree, and the defenders had let him come, holding the tree with a token force and gambling everything on his choosing to walk in and finish it himself rather than send a subordinate. He walked in. The dead outside the godswood stood still while he did, because they do what he does. What ended the Long Night was one strike delivered by one person against one target, and the entire army on both sides was, in the end, the diversion."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The Dothraki were effectively destroyed as a fighting force in the opening minutes and the survivors amounted to a fraction of the horde that had crossed the narrow sea. The Unsullied lost most of their strength holding the line and covering the withdrawal, which is what heavy infantry is for and does not make it cheaper. The northern houses and the free folk lost heavily in the yards.",
          "By the accounting given afterward, roughly half of the entire combined army was gone, and what remained was in no condition to fight anyone. That fact governs everything that happens next, because the survivors were then asked to march a thousand miles south and fight a second war within weeks.",
          "The dead paid nothing, and then paid everything at once, which is the only way this enemy could ever have been made to pay."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "It ended the threat that the whole story had been built around, in one night, at Winterfell rather than at the Wall, and it ended it decisively enough that it never returned. Eight thousand years of institutional dread was resolved by a small number of people standing in the right places at the right moment.",
          "It also, immediately and disastrously, converted a war of survival back into a war over a chair. The alliance had held only because of the enemy; the morning after, the two claimants at Winterfell had nothing left in common and a great deal in dispute, and the army they shared was half-strength and largely foreign.",
          "The North had been fought over, burned and half-emptied twice in five years for causes chosen elsewhere. What the northern lords concluded from that is the seed of everything they demanded afterward."]}],
    fate: [
      "Arya Stark ended it. She came out of the dark behind the Night King in the godswood, was caught by the throat, and dropped the Valyrian dagger from one hand to the other to put it into him — the same blade that had been sent to cut a sleeping boy’s throat in the first season, returned by Bran to the person who would use it correctly. The Night King shattered, and with him every Walker and every wight on the field went down at once, mid-stride.",
      "Theon Greyjoy held the godswood approach with the ironborn he had left and stood alone in front of Bran at the last, and was killed by the Night King in the moment before the strike. Bran thanked him first, which was the only thing anyone could have given him.",
      "Ser Jorah Mormont died on the field outside the walls, having fought his way back to Daenerys through the collapse and stood over her until he could not. Lyanna Mormont, ten years old and Lady of Bear Island, was crushed by a wight giant at the gate and killed it as it did so. Beric Dondarrion spent the last of his six or seven lives holding a corridor so that Arya could reach the godswood, and this time the red priest was not there to give another back. Dolorous Edd, last Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch, died in the first minutes of the fighting on the field.",
      "Melisandre lit the Dothraki blades, lit the trench, and reminded Arya of a thing said to her years earlier about the eyes she would close. Her work being finished, she walked out beyond the walls at dawn, took off the choker she had worn her whole life, and let her true age arrive.",
      "Viserion, raised by the enemy at the Wall, was destroyed over Winterfell. Rhaegal survived the night and did not survive the following week.",
      "The alliance survived the battle by about a fortnight. What it did to itself afterward is the subject of the last two entries in this collection."] },

  { id: "bells-battle", name: "The Fall of King's Landing", sub: "305 AC — the last war",
    meta: [["Who", "Daenerys & Drogon vs. a city that had surrendered"], ["Result", "The city burned"],
           ["Commanders", "Daenerys Targaryen, Jon Snow, Grey Worm · Cersei Lannister, Euron Greyjoy, Harry Strickland"],
           ["Strength", "One dragon, the remains of two armies, against twenty thousand sellswords and a garrison"],
           ["Ground", "The Blackwater, the Mud Gate, and half a million people inside a ring of stone"],
           ["Telling", "Screen only — the novels have not reached this"]],
    blurb: "The bells rang for surrender, and the dragon kept going.",
    parasTitle: "Why it came to this",
    paras: [
      "The alliance that survived the Long Night went south immediately and in poor condition. Half of it was dead, its cavalry arm was gone, its infantry was a foreign professional force in a country that regarded it as an occupation, and its commander had just spent everything she had to save a kingdom that had not thanked her. Cersei Lannister, who had promised armies to the north and sent none, had spent the same period buying the Golden Company out of Essos with Highgarden’s gold — twenty thousand disciplined sellswords, the best in the free cities, delivered to King’s Landing by Euron Greyjoy’s fleet.",
      "Then came a fortnight that removed every restraint. The Targaryen fleet was ambushed at sea by Euron with the rebuilt scorpions, and Daenerys lost a dragon and much of her fleet in a single engagement. Her closest friend was taken and executed on the walls of the city, in front of her, as a deliberate provocation. Her chief adviser had already begun corresponding against her; her other adviser kept negotiating for terms with the woman who had done all of this. And the man she loved had turned out to have a better claim to her throne than she did and had told his family about it.",
      "The negotiations that followed were real. Tyrion Lannister made an arrangement in good faith: if the city’s bells were rung and its gates opened, the assault would be halted and the surrender accepted. He got that promise from Daenerys and he made the arrangement possible from inside by way of his brother. Everything after the bells is what the whole show argues about, and it should be said plainly that neither the arrangement nor the crime that followed it appears anywhere in the published novels."],
    sections: [
      { h: "The ground and the hosts",
        paras: [
          "King’s Landing is a walled city on a bay with a river mouth beneath it, half a million people inside, and a fortress at the top of the hill. Its defences are excellent against armies. Its dependency, as the Blackwater had already demonstrated once, is that everything in it — the wooden districts, the caches, the population — is combustible and cannot be moved.",
          "Cersei’s defensive plan was built on three things and each of them was reasonable. Euron’s fleet lay in the bay with heavy scorpions mounted, having already proved on open water that they could kill a dragon. More scorpions lined the city walls in numbers. And the Golden Company was drawn up outside the main gate under Harry Strickland to meet the assault in the field. Behind all of that she had brought the population of the surrounding countryside inside the walls and housed a great many of them in the Red Keep’s outer yard, on the calculation that an attacker who wished to rule the city afterward would not destroy it with the smallfolk in it. That calculation was not stupid. It was simply made about the wrong person on the wrong week.",
          "The attacking force was the remnant of the Winterfell army: the Unsullied under Grey Worm, the surviving northmen and Vale men under Jon Snow, what Dothraki were left, and one dragon.",
          "The decisive factor was neither numbers nor walls. It was that the anti-dragon weapon had been proved at sea against a rider flying straight and level in daylight, and had never been tested against one who had learned from it."]},
      { h: "How it was fought",
        paras: [
          "It opened with the fleet and it was over almost as it began. Drogon came out of low cloud from behind and above the anchored ships rather than approaching across open water in the sightline of the scorpions, which was the entire lesson of the previous engagement applied once. A scorpion is a slow-traversing weapon on a fixed mount aimed by eye. Against a target that arrives from an unexpected quarter at speed it gets one shot, usually late. The Iron Fleet was destroyed at anchor in a matter of minutes, and with it the crown’s naval arm and the greater part of its confidence.",
          "The wall batteries went the same way and for the same reason, from the landward side and along the line of the ramparts rather than across them. Once the scorpions on the walls were gone the city had no weapon that could reach the sky at all, and the defence of King’s Landing reverted to what it had been for three hundred years, which is masonry.",
          "The Golden Company was destroyed next, and this is the part that has drawn the most professional criticism. Twenty thousand of the finest heavy infantry available for hire had been drawn up outside the gate in close order, in the open, with their backs to a wall they could not retreat through quickly, facing an enemy with a dragon. There is no formation that survives that and there was no reason to be standing there. A single pass along the front, followed by the collapse of the gate behind them, ended the Golden Company as a going concern before it had received a charge.",
          "With the gate down and the walls silent the fighting fell to the two infantry forces in the streets around the Mud Gate, and it lasted about as long as it takes for professional soldiers to work out that nobody is coming to help them. The Lannister garrison was outnumbered, out-generalled and had just watched their own artillery, their fleet and their sellswords destroyed in under an hour. They threw down their swords in the square almost as one, which is the ordinary and honourable end of a siege.",
          "Then the bells rang. The bells of the Great Sept are the recognised signal of a city’s surrender and everyone on the field understood them. The attacking commanders in the street stopped. The garrison was disarmed. By every convention of Westerosi war the battle had ended and the sack was the only question remaining, and the terms had been agreed in advance.",
          "What happened instead was a decision taken by one person alone on a rooftop, and there is no tactical account of it, because it was not tactics. Drogon turned from the Red Keep and began working systematically through the city — not the fortress, not the garrison, not the surviving batteries, but the streets. The fires that followed did the rest, because a medieval city of timber and thatch packed with refugees needs only to be started. Once the burning was general the two armies in the streets came apart into their own worst instincts: the Dothraki and a portion of the Unsullied went through the districts behind the fire, and the northern contingents in the square found themselves standing between their own allies and the people they had come to liberate, unable to stop either.",
          "There was no pursuit, no defence, no second phase. The city had already surrendered when the destruction began and continued to be destroyed for hours afterward, which is precisely why it is not remembered as a battle."]},
      { h: "What it cost",
        paras: [
          "The number of dead in King’s Landing is beyond serious estimate and the story does not attempt one. It was a city of half a million with the countryside crowded in on top of it, and a large part of it burned with the people inside. The dead were overwhelmingly not soldiers.",
          "The Golden Company was annihilated, the Iron Fleet was destroyed at anchor, and the Lannister garrison ceased to exist. On the other side the losses were negligible in men and total in something else.",
          "The Red Keep itself was brought down in large part, and the sept, and the whole of the low ground by the river. What was left was a burnt shell holding a throne."]},
      { h: "What it changed",
        paras: [
          "Militarily it ended the last war in Westeros in an afternoon and left one person in undisputed possession of the continent, with no rival army, no fleet, and no walls anywhere that meant anything.",
          "Politically it destroyed the thing it had won. The case for Daenerys Targaryen had always rested on a distinction between her and the dynasty she came from — that she broke wheels rather than turned them, that she took cities in order to free them. That distinction did not survive the afternoon, and the people who had spent years arguing it on her behalf were the ones standing in the square when the bells rang.",
          "Whether this was a betrayal of the character or the destination she had been walking toward from the first city she burned is the argument that has run ever since, and the show’s own answer is less confident than it believes. What is not in dispute is the strategic consequence: the throne was taken, and taking it that way made holding it impossible."]}],
    fate: [
      "Varys had already been executed before the assault, on Dragonstone, for writing letters proposing Jon Snow as the better claimant. He knew what he was doing and he did it anyway, on the grounds that a spymaster who serves the realm rather than a person occasionally has to prove it.",
      "Harry Strickland was ridden down in the street by Grey Worm as the Golden Company broke. Euron Greyjoy came ashore from his burning fleet and met Jaime Lannister on the rocks below the Keep; both men wounded each other, and Euron died on the shingle believing he had killed the queen’s lover, which was the only thing he had ever wanted on the record.",
      "Sandor Clegane found his brother on the Red Keep’s stair and got the fight he had been owed for thirty years. Nothing short of the collapsing tower and the fire below it would end it, and that is how it ended, with both of them going into it. He had talked Arya Stark out of coming into the Keep with him a few minutes earlier, which is very probably the only unambiguously good thing he did in his life.",
      "Cersei and Jaime Lannister died together beneath the Red Keep, crushed as the cellars came down on them while trying to reach the boats. Qyburn was killed on the stair by the creature he had made, for standing in its way.",
      "Daenerys Targaryen was killed days later in the throne room by Jon Snow, who had spent the interval being told by his conscience and by Tyrion that there was no version of the future in which she stopped. Drogon burned the Iron Throne rather than the man who did it, which is either the most or the least intelligent thing any animal does in the story, and carried her body east.",
      "What followed was a council of the surviving lords in the ruins, the election of Bran Stark as king of six kingdoms, the North’s departure under Sansa, and Jon Snow’s return to the Wall — a punishment that was also, on any honest reading, the only place left that would have him."] },
  ],
}
);
